\par\par \b \uc2 Up & Down Wall Street: The Fate of Free Trade\b0\par\par\uc2 1,719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2016\par 11:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 7\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Randall W. Forsyth \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To paraphrase Gerald Ford, our long national nightmare is almost over. That, of course, is the presidential campaign, whose pall has hung over this great land like the fetid air of Beijing or Mumbai. After Tuesday, the voting will be over and, hopefully, the battles as well, although nobody can be sure of anything in this insane year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Amid this atmosphere of trepidation, the \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500 index extended its worst losing streak this year to nine sessions on Friday. This came as the presidential polls tightened over the past week, with Republican \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  narrowing the gap with Democrat \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , who seemingly had a comfortable lead until the revelation of further investigation into her infamous emails.\par \par \uc2 What is truly depressing is the doleful realization that, come Tuesday, one of them will win. (I wish I could claim authorship, but credit goes to Jim Grant, friend, former Barron's colleague, and the name atop the incomparable Grant's Interest Rate Observer.)\par \par \uc2 In a week, Clinton has gone from being a near shoo-in to gain the requisite 270 Electoral College votes to going down to the wire against the surging Trump. On Friday, FiveThirtyEight, the data-crunching Website, had the former secretary of state ahead by 3.3 percentage points in its poll-based forecast -- close to the average two-point error of polls in the 12 presidential races going back to 1968.\par \par \uc2 Despite all the parsing about the economic policy prescriptions of the two major presidential candidates, in actuality, the future occupant of the White House won't be able to do anything about taxes or spending without the approval of Congress. And the House of Representatives seems certain to remain controlled by the GOP, while the Senate could revert to the Democrats. The judiciary is the other branch in the U.S. system of checks and balances that keeps a rein on the executive and legislative branches.\par \par \uc2 International trade is an exception, points out Brian Horrigan, chief economist at Loomis Sayles. There already is a bipartisan backlash against free trade, with both candidates voicing opposition to the Trans-Pacific Partnership. As for Trump's promise to "rip up" pacts such as the \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2 , a president can pretty much do that. And he or she will have wide discretion to raise tariffs, impose quotas, and erect other barriers.\par \par \uc2 One of the other big weapons on trade is for the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  to declare a country a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator," which Trump has vowed to do in the case of China. Yet, as I wrote first in a cover story that ran almost exactly a year ago (" Trump Is Wrong on China," Nov. 14), Beijing is no longer manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lower its value; it is doing precisely the opposite.\par \par \uc2 On the face of it, China's yuan has declined to a six-year low of around 6.75 to the greenback, with a 4% drop this year. At the same time, however, the dollar has risen against most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including those of most Asian exporters, so the yuan is adjusting in tandem.\par \par \uc2 But the data belie a deliberate devaluation attempt by China. A country that is trying to push its exchange rate lower would be selling its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and accumulating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 In actuality, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves plunged by $18.8 billion in September, to $3.17 trillion, as its monetary authorities spent them to slow the yuan's decline. According to analysts quoted by Bloomberg, Beijing might have spent as much as $80 billion during October and more than $19 billion in the week ended on Oct. 24 alone.\par \par \uc2 Only aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the yuan's decline from being disorderly, they said. (Some circumstantial evidence: Securities held at the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2  for foreign and official accounts plunged by $30 billion in the four weeks ended on Nov. 2. There's no way of knowing whether this is part of Beijing's stash, but China is the biggest holder of \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  securities.)\par \par \uc2 All else being equal, China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should rise, given its trade and current-account surpluses. But capital is fleeing -- in no small part because of the country's increasingly unstable debt bubble, as described by Jon Laing in the special report on China. Stories abound of Chinese using imaginative means to stretch the $50,000 per-person limit on taking cash out of their country.\par \par \uc2 In addition, investors and businesses are repaying dollar borrowings that were used to fund "carry trades" to buy higher-yielding Chinese assets -- a relatively risk-free proposition as long as U.S interest rates don't rise and the yuan doesn't lose ground against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Last year's Fed rate increase, plus the expectation of another next month, have altered that equation, resulting in demand for dollars to cover the borrowings of Chinese carry-traders. China's monetary authorities apparently are supplying the dollars and sopping up the surfeit of yuan by spending their nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.\par \par \uc2 I interrupted the nonstop talk of the presidential race with this admittedly tedious \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  discussion to show that the data contradict the claims that China is pushing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. But more than ever in this campaign season, facts have had a hard time of getting in the way of a good story.\par \par \uc2 THE EFFECTS OF THE U.S. PRESIDENTIAL race are being directly felt by another \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Mexican peso, which has descended a proverbial wall of worry in response to Trump's rise in the polls.\par \par \uc2 In late April, 17.2 pesos bought a dollar, but by late September it took nearly 20 pesos to get a buck. The Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened subsequently, to around 18.5 to the dollar by Oct. 24, but has weakened again, to 19, as Clinton's lead in the polls has shrunk. A similar pattern could be seen in the iShares MSCI Mexico Capped exchange-traded fund (ticker: EWW), which is off by more than 8% since mid-August, even after a 1% pop on Friday.\par \par \uc2 As for the U.S. stock market, the S&P 500 looked as if it might break its losing streak until a slump on Friday afternoon left it lower for the ninth straight session, the longest string of daily declines going back nearly 36 years -- longer even than the \uc2 Cleveland Browns\uc2 ' slide this year.\par \par \uc2 But as S&P's Howard Silverblatt points out, the large-cap benchmark was off just 3.07% over its losing streak, a far cry from the 9.4% swoon over a similar span seen in December 1980. And -- something to think about on Monday -- the last 10-day slump was in July 1975, when the S&P 500 dropped 7.8%. The record is 12 straight losing sessions in April 1966, when the index lost 6.6%.\par \par \uc2 The 1980 nine-day slide came while the Fed was ratcheting up its federal-funds rate target to near 20% -- rather a different situation from the present. The Federal Open Market Committee last week held the fed-funds target at 0.25%-0.5% but strongly hinted that it would hike it a quarter-point next month -- which surprised exactly nobody.\par \par \uc2 The October employment report released Friday also shouldn't change things for the Fed. The numbers aren't likely to have a big impact on the last, frenetic days of the presidential campaign, although the steady gains in payrolls might be expected to work in Clinton's favor. Yet Trump could point to flaws in the figures (although he has dismissed the data overall as phony).\par \par \uc2 Nonfarm payrolls rose by 161,000 in October, an insignificant 12,000 below economists' projections, but offset by a net upward revision of 44,000 in the preceding two months, giving a three-month average of 176,000. However, private-sector jobs lagged, noted Peter Boockvar of the Lindsey Group, gaining just 142,000, 28,000 less than expected, with the public sector making up the difference. And while the jobless rate dipped one-tenth of a percentage point, to 4.9%, that was entirely because 195,000 folks exited the workforce, lowering the labor-force participation rate by a 10th, to 62.8%.\par \par \uc2 David Rosenberg of Gluskin Sheff also observes the employment-to-population rate dipped 0.1%, to 59.7%, but more importantly has shown virtually no improvement since mid-2009. That seemingly obscure number helps explain the distress felt by many voters.\par \par \uc2 Those who were working did get a raise. Average hourly earnings rose 0.4% in the latest month, bringing the gain to 2.8% from the level a year ago. That higher pay has to come from somewhere.\par \par \uc2 "We are concerned that the pickup in wage growth will further squeeze profit margins," write John Ryding and Conrad DeQuadros of RDQ Economics. That, adds Joshua Shapiro of MFR, "will spur increasingly aggressive cost-cutting, which in turn will lead to heightened pressure on job growth," thus slowing the economy next year.\par \par \uc2 Despite the stock market's losing streak, Doug Ramsey, the \uc2 Leuthold Group\uc2 's chief investment officer, thinks the current oversold condition is a "good" one that ought to be bought if you're a tactical type. He also thinks investors should be looking more at policies than personalities in the election.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of who wins the White House, he suggests that the new president will probably be playing between "the 40-yard lines" of the political gridiron against a Congress with at least one chamber controlled by the opposition. If both houses are held by the opposing party, the action probably could be stymied between "the 47-yard lines" -- likely beyond even field-goal range to score any policy points.\par \par \uc2 And once the elections are past, maybe the weary nation will turn back to the real gridiron to boost the National Football League's flagging TV ratings.\par \par \uc2 Email: randall.forsyth@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 Like Barron's on \uc2 Facebook\par \par \uc2 Follow Barron's on \uc2 Twitter\par \par \uc2 To subscribe to Barron's, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.barrons.com/subscribe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.barrons.com/subscribe}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 05, 2016 06:00 ET (10:00 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020161105ecb5000ee\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
