\par\par \b \uc2 Yuan's Impact on Global Markets Is Not What It Used to Be -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 198 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2016\par 06:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0351 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Why isn't a declining yuan and China's weak stock market having an impact on global markets, as they did earlier this year? Brown, Brothers & Harriman Co. offer a few explanations: First, Chinese equities haven't been as volatile. China stocks fell 22.6% in January, but since March has not moved more than 3.6% in any month. Second, the yuan's devaluation still has a modest impact on Chinese exports, which are lower compared to a year earlier. The depreciation of the yuan has not unleashed the same amount of backlash from the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 , which has shied from calling the country a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also been slow in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yuan, allowing the market to play a bigger part. "The role of the yuan and Chinese stocks in the world economy is modest, and the current pattern may be more sustainable than its markets dictating the investment climate," write analysts. (anjie.zheng@wsj.com; Twitter: @anjiezheng)\par \par \uc2 Editor JSM\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 26-10-16 0421GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020161026ecaq0004g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
