\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  Shifts Yuan  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Tactic, Eyes Gradual Depreciation -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 242 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2016\par 05:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0256 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  apears to have adopted a subtle shift to its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics in the yuan market, moving away from heavy, costly defense of particular levels of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a more sporadic approach aimed at ensuring an orderly depreciation trend, traders said. The onshore yuan is under pressure again this morning, down against the U.S. dollar at 6.7738 from 6.7655 Friday. "There's no strong dollar sales from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as we used to see but it did step in to when the yuan opened at a low of 6.7770 today," said a Shanghai-based trader with a Hong Kong bank. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  doesn't appear to have set a firm target for its yuan defense, such as 6.7800, this time. "Instead, their strategy is such that they will come in when there seems to be panic among market participants, but the overall message of guiding the yuan weaker bit by bit isn't changed," said the trader.\par \par \uc2  Now that the yuan has joined the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's elite reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  status known as the Special Drawing Rights, the pressure on Beijing to artificially make the yuan look stronger despite a weak economy appears to have dissipated, she added. (hong.shen@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Editor: JFN\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 24-10-16 0335GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020161024ecao0003r\par }