\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-China's yuan sinks to six-year low, then erases losses\b0\par\par\uc2 819 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2016\par 07:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Yuan reaches weakest since Sept 2010 before rebounding\par     * Traders say rebound is a 'technical' one\par     * Onshore market had been shut since Sept 30\par \par  (Adds quotes, latest yuan level, tables)\par     By Winni Zhou and John Ruwitch\par     SHANGHAI, Oct 10 (Reuters) - China's yuan fell to its lowest\par level in six years early Monday, breaching a key psychological\par threshold, before erasing the losses on the first day of trading\par after a week-long holiday.\par      Traders said the weakening of the yuan reflected\par strength in the dollar last week, and they did not see any signs\par of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by state banks to support the yuan after it\par fell.\par      In mid-July, when the yuan last breached the 6.7 mark,\par state banks \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily. \par      Some \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  watchers have wondered if any signs of yuan\par weakness following the holiday would signal that Beijing was\par putting the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  back on a slow depreciation path after\par holding it steady through September.\par      The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the\par midpoint at 6.7008 yuan per dollar, its weakest fix\par since September 2010 and about 0.3 percent weaker than the\par setting on Sept. 30, before a one-week National Day holiday.\par      The spot market opened at 6.6904 per dollar, quickly fell\par below the key 6.7 level for the first time since July and traded\par as low as 6.7051, the weakest rate since September 2010.\par      Then it began to pare losses, and at 0403 GMT was changing\par hands at 6.6991, or 246 pips weaker than the\par Sept. 30 late session close and 0.03 percent firmer than that\par day's midpoint.\par      A trader at a foreign bank in Shanghai said the market "is\par actually quite calm now as the willingness to purchase the\par dollar is not strong. And the yuan has been pulled back to\par around 6.7 level after some investors sold the greenback after\par the yuan hit 6.7050 as they were afraid the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would\par soon step into the market to stabilise."\par      "The market is holding a wait-and-see approach towards the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  attitude," he added.\par      Another trader at a European bank in Shanghai said "so far\par we haven't seen any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  actions",  though he added that\par it's possible the yuan could soon breach the 6.75 level "if the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does not show up at all"." \par      Late last week, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced that its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves dropped for a third month in September, by\par more than expected, suggesting fresh capital outflows from the\par world's second-largest economy.\par     \par      STRENGTHENING DOLLAR\par      Pressure has also risen on the yuan recently as the U.S.\par dollar has strengthened in global markets, most recently on the\par back of strong labour market data that supports a possible U.S.\par interest rate hike later this year.\par      A trader at a Chinese bank said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have\par loosened its grip at the 6.7 per dollar level after the yuan's\par Oct. 1 official inclusion into the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Monetary Fund\f28 \uc2 \f2 's\par reserve basket, known as Special Drawing Rights (SDR).\par      "We will have to see what the next threshold is," the\par trader added.\par      On Friday, the offshore yuan slumped to its lowest\par rate against the dollar since Jan. 7. Offshore yuan mostly\par trades in Hong Kong and is not bound by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's tight trading restrictions.\par      Hong Kong markets are closed Monday for a holiday.\par      In the weeks before onshore markets closed for China's long\par holiday, the spot rate flirted with 6.7. The last close was at\par 6.6745.\par      On Oct. 6, a Reuters poll of more than 70 foreign exchanges\par strategists showed they expected the yuan to fall another 3\par percent by next September. \par     \par         The yuan market at 0403 GMT: \par     \par     ONSHORE SPOT:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  PBOC midpoint      6.7008   6.6778    -0.34%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.6991   6.6745    -0.37%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    -0.03%             \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change YTD                       -3.07%\par  Spot change since 2005                23.55%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par     Key indexes:\par      \par  Item            Current     Previous  Change\par                                        \par  Thomson         94.81       95.09     -0.3\par  Reuters/HKEX                          \par  CNH index                             \par  Dollar index    96.526      96.632    -0.1\par  \par     \par     \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\pa