\par\par \b \uc2 Donald Trump Charges China With Yuan Manipulation--Again\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saumya Vaishampayan \par \uc2 1,019 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2016\par 07:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG-- Donald Trump accused China of devaluing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the opening minutes of the first U.S. presidential debate--adding fresh spice to an old debate: Does China gain an unfair trade advantage by manipulating the yuan?\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's accusation--he has previously called China "the single greatest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator that's ever been on this planet"--may have rung truer a decade or so ago. This year, though, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  more to prop up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts and economists say, rather than driving it lower to win an even greater share of world trade.\par \par \uc2 The Republican nominee isn't alone among leading U.S. politicians in criticizing China's perceived policy of keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  undervalued to make its exports more competitive. That, according to most economists, is the definition of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator.\par \par \uc2 The issue, though, remains far from simple.\par \par \uc2 Manipulation--or just a tight rein?\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does keep close control of the yuan. It sets the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value against the greenback daily, with reference to where it ended the previous day and movements of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , allowing it to trade 2% higher or lower than this so-called fix.\par \par \uc2 Despite dropping sharply in early 2016, the yuan is down just 2.6% against the dollar this year. \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is worried a bigger drop would encourage more Chinese people to send money abroad, weakening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  further.\par \par \uc2 It has sold some of its foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings and bought yuan to prop up its value--contributing to a 10.5% drop in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in the year to August.\par \par \uc2 "There's no way someone could charge [China] with manipulation right now, " says Fred Bergsten, founding director of the \uc2 Peterson Institute for International Economics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  agrees, arguing in April that China's sales of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed it isn't manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  downward now. Trump campaign spokeswoman Hope Hicks declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Bergsten says China was guilty of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation "big time" in the past.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held the yuan steady against the dollar for about a decade up to mid-2005, a period when China's economy expanded rapidly. Even after China scrapped the yuan's dollar peg, many felt it gained too slowly: It rose 21% from 2005 to 2008, while China's trade surplus in goods nearly tripled.\par \par \uc2 "They did have a very adverse effect on the U.S. economy, and on the economies of most of their trading partners, basically by cheating," said Mr. Bergsten.\par \par \uc2 Does China still get a trade advantage?\par \par \uc2 China's critics say its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has helped it become the world's leading exporter of goods with a 13.8% market share, up from 5% in 2002, its first full year as a member of the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2 . Its goods trade surplus hit a record $567 billion in 2015. The U.S.'s trade deficit with China alone has more than tripled since 2002 to $367.2 billion.\par \par \uc2 Still, whatever advantage China has gained may be fading.\par \par \uc2 China has a large surplus in its current account, a broad measure of an economy's international finance position heavily influenced by trade. Yet that surplus is now equivalent to around 3% of China's gross domestic product, down from its 2007 peak of 10%, according to the \uc2 World Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nor is the link between \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values and trade mechanical. The U.S.'s goods trade deficit with China has widened almost every year since 1985, regardless of the yuan's value.\par \par \uc2 A weaker yuan isn't an undiluted advantage for manufacturers in China, as goods assembled there often include parts from other countries. For example, \uc2 Foxconn Technology Group\uc2  runs the main site for \uc2 Apple Inc\uc2 .'s iPhone production in China. The screen is supplied by companies including \uc2 Japan Display Inc\uc2 ., while Taiwan's \uc2 Largan Precision Co\uc2 . makes camera modules often used in the smartphone.\par \par \uc2 "We have really intricate supply chains in Asia," said Frederic Neumann, co-head of Asian economic research at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . That makes it more difficult to link a decline in a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to an increase in trade competitiveness, he said.\par \par \uc2 And a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  does little if overall demand is sluggish. Despite the yuan's fall this year, U.S. imports of goods from China totaled $251.7 billion in 2016 through July, down 6.5% from the same period a year ago, according to the \uc2 U.S. Census Bureau\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Be careful what you wish for\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's campaign website says the yuan is "undervalued by anywhere from 15% to 40%" and that freer trade and floating exchange rates would help reduce the U.S. trade deficit with China.\par \par \uc2 Assessing any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fair value is tricky. Some economists look at a country's real effective exchange rate, a gauge of its exchange rates against several trading partners adjusted for inflation.\par \par \uc2 For China, that reading has fallen over the past year, but remains higher than both its five- and 10-year averages--suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently overvalued, based on data from \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  meanwhile said in July that the yuan was "broadly in line with fundamentals and desirable policies, although its assessment is subject to a high degree of uncertainty."\par \par \uc2 Even if China removed its controls over the yuan, it may not move in the direction U.S. politicians hope. China's cooling economy, capital outflows and the prospect of the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  raising interest rates again could all push the yuan down further.\par \par \uc2 "You can argue that the Chinese government should have let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  go down more, and instead they are not," said \uc2 Chen Zhao\uc2 , co-director of global macro research at Philadelphia-based Brandywine Global Investment Management, which manages $70 billion.\par \par \uc2 "So they are doing the U.S. a favor in that sense," he said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 27-09-16 0519GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020160927ec9r00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
