\par\par \b \uc2 Cost to Borrow Yuan Overnight in Hong Kong Hits Seven-Month High -- Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Saumya Vaishampayan \par \uc2 422 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2016\par 11:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG--The cost for banks to borrow yuan from each other overnight in Hong Kong surged to a fresh seven-month high Wednesday, a move traders said was likely due to further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market from \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The overnight interbank borrowing cost jumped to 8.16%, its highest level since Feb. 19 and up from 2.84% on Tuesday. That short-term borrowing cost had hit more than 5% on Thursday last week and on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The one-week interbank rate meanwhile advanced to 10.152% Wednesday, its highest level since January. That tenor on Wednesday was more expensive than longer-dated borrowing costs, an unusual phenomenon.\par \par \uc2 Market participants said yuan buying by branches of Chinese state-owned banks in Hong Kong was likely behind the rise in borrowing costs. In turn, their buying was likely directed by the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , they said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 PBOC\uc2  said "the stated information is not true."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the so-called "offshore" yuan market is likely aimed at curtailing bets that the yuan will decline. The yuan trades freely in Hong Kong, while the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  exerts tight control of the yuan's exchange rate onshore.\par \par \uc2 Investors looking to bet the yuan will fall often borrow the yuan overnight in Hong Kong, and exchange it for dollars, exchanging the money back the following day after the yuan has dropped--a practice known as "shorting." A rise in the overnight rate makes this trade more expensive, deterring investors from betting against the yuan.\par \par \uc2 "Every short position I've tried to fund has cost a fortune," said Stephen Innes, a senior trader at Oanda Asia Pacific.\par \par \uc2 The Chinese authorities are typically keen to ensure that the rate at which the yuan trades against the dollar in the offshore market remains in line with the rate in onshore domestic markets. They appear especially keen to ensure that the two rates remain in line ahead of the yuan's inclusion in the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's basket of reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Oct. 1, said Sue Trinh, head of Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 "This volatility in CNH funding is really not the kind of behavior you'd expect from any reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Ms. Trinh said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Saumya Vaishampayan at saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 14, 2016 05:22 ET (09:22 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160914ec9e00119\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
