\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan edges up in cautious mood on fear of  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 465 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2016\par 06:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, Aug 31 (Reuters) - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yuan firmed on\par Wednesday, with investors cautious about possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  whenever the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  neared the\par psychologically-important 6.7 per dollar level.\par     While a resurgent dollar has pushed the yuan lower in recent\par sessions, Chinese authorities are reluctant to see it breach the\par 6.7 to the dollar mark again. When it dipped below that level in\par mid-July, state-owned banks aggressively sold dollars, a move\par traders believed was initiated by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     "Shorts received a big blow in July when the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to maintain the yuan's stability around 6.7," said a\par senior trader at a Chinese commercial bank in Shanghai.\par     "So whenever the yuan weakened beyond 6.68 this week,\par corporate dollar sales emerged," he said. "No one wants to fight\par against the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the midpoint rate \par at 6.6908 per dollar prior to market open, weaker than the\par previous fix of 6.6812. \par     The spot market opened at 6.6835 per dollar and\par was changing hands at 6.6780 at midday, 40 pips lower than the\par previous close.\par     The global dollar index fell to 95.977 from the\par previous close of 96.054.\par     It strengthened 0.5 percent overnight, and theoretically\par should have weakened the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Wednesday.\par     The Thomson Reuters/HKEX Global CNH index, which\par tracks the offshore yuan against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a\par daily basis, stood at 95.07, firmer than the previous day's\par 94.95. \par     The offshore yuan was trading 0.19 percent weaker than the\par onshore spot at 6.6904 per dollar. \par     \par     The yuan market at 4:26AM GMT: \par     \par     ONSHORE SPOT:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  PBOC midpoint      6.6908   6.6812    -0.14%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.6785   6.682     0.05%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    -0.18%             \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change YTD                       -2.77%\par  Spot change since 2005                23.93%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par     Key indexes:\par      \par  Item            Current     Previous  Change\par                                        \par  Thomson         95.08       94.95     0.1\par  Reuters/HKEX                          \par  CNH index                             \par  Dollar index    95.952      96.054    -0.1\par    \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument            Current   Difference\par                                  from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    6.6902    -0.17%\par         *                      