\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan rises to three-week high as Fed sends dollar lower\b0\par\par\uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Juli 2016\par 05:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HONG KONG, July 28 (Reuters) - China's yuan rose to\par three-week highs against the dollar on Thursday, as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set another stronger midpoint to reflect weakness in global\par dollar index after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  stopped short of\par signalling a near-term rate rise.\par     Authorities had allowed the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slip to near 6-year\par lows to the dollar earlier this month but have guided it higher\par in the past few days by stronger fixings and suspected\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set the midpoint rate \par at 6.6597 per dollar prior to market open, firmer than the\par previous fix 6.6671. It was the strongest level since July 5.\par     Analysts expect the yuan to continue to edge higher in the\par short-term as its depreciation so far this year was not in line\par with China's still big trade surplus. The yuan has lost 2.5\par percent against the dollar this year.\par     "Given China's extremely high (goods) trade surplus at\par almost $50 billion a month, the yuan's depreciation has surely\par raised the concerns of its trading partners," said Macquarie\par analysts in a report.\par     "China will host its first ever G20 Summit on Sept 4-5 in\par Hangzhou, so most likely it might guide the yuan stable or even\par stronger against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in weeks ahead."\par     The U.S. dollar weakened on Wednesday after the Fed's\par statement was seen as increasing the possibility of a September\par rate hike, but not strongly pointing towards one. \par     The spot market opened at 6.6633 per dollar and\par was changing hands at 6.6622 at midday, -78 pips away from the\par previous late session close and 0.04 percent away from the\par midpoint. The spot rate is currently allowed to trade with a\par range 2 percent above or below the official fixing on any given\par day.\par     The Thomson Reuters/HKEX Global CNH index, which\par tracks the offshore yuan against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a\par daily basis, stood at 95.91, weaker than the previous day's\par 96.14. \par     The global dollar index fell to 96.583 from the\par previous close of 97.053. \par     The offshore yuan was trading -0.09 percent away from the\par onshore spot at 6.668 per dollar. \par     Offshore one-year non-deliverable forwards contracts\par (NDFs), considered the best available proxy for\par forward-looking market expectations of the yuan's value, traded\par at 6.8115, -2.23 percent away from the midpoint.\par     One-year NDFs are settled against the midpoint, not the spot\par rate.\par     \par     The yuan market at a glance: \par     \par     ONSHORE SPOT:\par  Item                         Current  Previous  Change\par  PBOC midpoint                \{S^F1\}   \{S^F2\}    \{;S^F2%S^F\par                                                  1:2\}%\par  Spot yuan                    \{D^F1\}   \{D^F2\}    \{;D^F2%D^F\par                                                  1:2\}%\par  Divergence from midpoint*    \{;D^F1%            \par                               S^F1:2\}            \par                               %                  \par  Spot change YTD                                 \{;6.4936%D\par                                                  ^F1:2\}%\par  Spot change since 2005 revaluation              \{;8.2765%D\par     