\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-G20's deference for China's economic policies irks Japan\b0\par\par\uc2 719 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juli 2016\par 01:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 (Repeats for early Asia trade)\par \par \uc2 By Pete Sweeney and Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI/TOKYO, July 25 (Reuters) - China dodged criticism of its economic management at a G20 meeting it hosted on the weekend, even winning plaudits for yuan transparency, much to the frustration of Japanese officials who are calling for more reforms from Beijing.\par \par \uc2 It was a marked contrast to a February G20 gathering of finance heads in Shanghai, when Chinese policymakers were on the defensive about the risk of another devaluation of the yuan.\par \par \uc2 While the world's second-largest economy has slowed and the yuan has fallen to 5-1/2 year lows against the dollar over the past five months, G20 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs appeared more concerned about the fallout from Britain's June vote to leave Europe.\par \par \uc2 "We have been very polite with China," said a European official at the meeting in the southwestern Chinese city of Chengdu, adding Tokyo failed to keep its concerns about China's economic performance - a major risk for Japan - on the table.\par \par \uc2 "China's growth problems and exchange rate decline have not been much of an issue here. Japan with its concerns has been left a bit alone, no one wanted to join in," the official said.\par \par \uc2 Japan has already failed this year to win support from its industrialised peers to curtail a strengthening yen, which has soared to 2-1/2 year highs against the dollar even as the economy faltered and exports fell.\par \par \uc2 "Japan remains concerned about China's economy and we will call on the U.S. and Europe not to shift attention away from China," a Japanese official said, but added that Tokyo had decided to "be quiet" on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the meeting.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has fallen over 5 percent against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tracked by \uc2 Thomson Reuters\uc2  since the G20 finance ministers meeting in February.\par \par \uc2 The yen, meanwhile, has become both stronger and more volatile this year, surging on safe-haven buying even as Japan adopted negative interest rates in its so-far futile effort to escape deflation.\par \par \uc2 Data on Monday showed Japan's quandary, with exports falling 7.4 percent in June from a year earlier, the ninth straight month of decline. The rising yen has weakened Japan's export engine as China increases its share of global trade.\par \par \uc2 At the G20 meeting, Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  said it was closely watching the impact of a declining yuan , and that he had agreed with U.S. Treasury Secretary Jack Lew on the need for structural reform in China and transparency in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 THE RIGHT DIRECTION\par \par \uc2 In contrast to Japan, the United States, a long-time critic of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, appeared satisfied with Beijing's recent handling of the yuan.\par \par \uc2 Lew complimented Beijing's improved transparency and said the yuan had been moving in response to market factors. U.S. officials even noted \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  had actually been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the yuan from falling too quickly.\par \par \uc2 The G20 meeting came at a sensitive time for China-U.S. relations after the \uc2 Permanent Court of Arbitration\uc2  in the Hague earlier this month ruled Beijing's claims to vast swathes of the South China Sea invalid.\par \par \uc2 It also immediately followed the nomination of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as the Republican Party candidate in the U.S. presidential election. Trump has said if elected he would have China declared a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator as one of his first acts in office.\par \par \uc2 Sources at the G20 said there was little discussion of China's new regulations on cybersecurity, seen as regulating foreign software companies out of the domestic market.\par \par \uc2 There was also little progress in opening protected parts of China's economy to foreign investment, sources said, although the final communique did express concern about protectionism.\par \par \uc2 However, the G20 did refer to worries about industrial overcapacity, particularly in the steel sector.\par \par \uc2 Many of China's trading partners accuse it of dumping excess steel in overseas markets to avoid massive layoffs in its state-dominated steel sector, in turn eroding steel employment in other countries.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Gernot Heller, David Lawder, Elias Glenn, Kevin Yao and John Ruwitch; Editing by \uc2 John Mair\uc2  and Sam Holmes) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-7-26T00:00:01.000Z  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160725ec7p017ll\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
