\par\par \b \uc2 China's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  In Line for New Powers -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 1,230 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2016\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par C1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 PBOC\uc2  could take on a bigger role as Beijing looks to consolidate a fragmented system \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Lingling Wei \f28 \par \par \uc2 BEIJING -- China is weighing how to consolidate financial regulation, following missteps that exacerbated market turmoil and embarrassed Beijing. One option under consideration would empower the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as other countries have done in times of risk.\par \par \uc2 The government has a number of tools for reining in its often freewheeling markets and managing its economy, and it hasn't shied from using them. But its credibility has been hurt by violent swings in the stock market and unexpected devaluations of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past year.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  has ordered up a plan for fixing a fragmented system in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and regulators overseeing the banking, securities and insurance industries often act in isolation and sometimes even at cross-purposes.\par \par \uc2 One option under discussion is to centralize oversight by putting all regulation under the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , according to officials familiar with the talks. Central banks have traditionally been the go-to agencies in times of turmoil. The idea, however, would require some powerful groups to cede turf, the officials involved in the process say.\par \par \uc2 "There is a basic agreement on the need to reform, but there remains an intense debate over how," said Zhang Chenghui, head of the financial-research institute at the Development Research Center, a think tank under China's State Council.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Xi and other leaders had hoped to see a draft proposal last month, the people said, but June came and went without one being put forth.\par \par \uc2 The potential adjustments in China are the latest in a long effort to reconcile two potentially incompatible aims: encouraging open markets as a source of capital and international recognition, while controlling them to produce desired outcomes. It may not be possible to do both at once.\par \par \uc2 Regulatory reform is "a complicated task in any economy and particularly so in a command economy," said Eswar Prasad, a senior fellow at the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2  and former \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  head for the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Direct government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in financial markets, like \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's market-rescue mission last summer, "not only vitiates the objective of having a robust and efficient regulatory framework, but can often generate added volatility," he said.\par \par \uc2 China had long been a crucial engine of the world's growth. But beginning last summer, it took on an unwanted role as the major source of uncertainty in world markets.\par \par \uc2 Trillions of dollars in wealth were wiped out as its stock market plunged. Particularly embarrassing, the turmoil hit just as China was pressing for a broader international role for its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and holding itself out as a key source of international aid.\par \par \uc2 Inconsistent regulation was a factor in the market's boom and bust. With the country's leaders looking to the stock market as a venue for state companies to raise cash, regulators left unchecked a surge in loans to investors to buy shares. When stocks fell, conflicting moves and a lack of regulatory coordination made things worse.\par \par \uc2 At one point, the securities regulator tightened requirements on margin financing, putting downward pressure on the market in the same week that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was taking action to prop it up.\par \par \uc2 Such conflicts could be headed off under a system in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fully in charge of so-called macroprudential policy, or rules targeting specific risks, advocates for such systems said. For instance, firms found violating limits on the use of borrowed money or other standards set by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be subject to more-stringent requirements, such as having to carry additional capital.\par \par \uc2 A regulatory consolidation would, in effect, be a return to an earlier model.\par \par \uc2 In the late 1990s, the Chinese government stripped away some of the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's regulatory authority and formed three separate agencies -- the \uc2 China Banking Regulatory Commission\uc2 , the \uc2 China Securities Regulatory Commission\uc2  and the \uc2 China Insurance Regulatory Commission\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The goal at the time was to foster independent monetary policy, but it had unexpected side effects. For instance, when China's commercial banks aggressively expanded lending in 2009 as part of Beijing's effort to shield the Chinese economy from the global crisis, the country's top banking regulator made them hold more capital and set aside more money against bad loans. Those moves served to undo the effects of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's credit loosening.\par \par \uc2 A less-radical concept under discussion involves setting up a new super-regulator under the State Council. It would be made up of senior officials from the three industry-specific regulatory bodies as well as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- and likely would meet less political resistance, officials said.\par \par \uc2 But some argue that it doesn't address the current fragmentation. China has had an interagency group in place since 2013, yet it hasn't proved to be an effective coordinator.\par \par \uc2 In a report issued in June, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  said the interagency group held three meetings last year and carried out an in-depth study on important financial matters. Media officials at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  declined to comment. The banking, securities and insurance regulators, as well as the State Council, didn't respond to inquiries.\par \par \uc2 The debate in China has parallels in the regulatory shake-ups in the U.S. and Europe in the aftermath of the global financial crisis. The \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  has long struggled to strike a balance between managing monetary policy and safeguarding the broader financial system with tools such as capital requirements and leverage caps.\par \par \uc2 While the Fed oversees banks and large financial institutions, other agencies hold sway over different parts of the financial industry, which slows down decision making. The issue hasn't seen notable improvement even after the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  formed a new entity in 2010 to bring together various financial regulators.\par \par \uc2 In the U.K., the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  has acted as a super-regulator since the 2008 crisis. In addition to the existing monetary-policy committee, it now has two others that keep an eye on financial stability and individual companies. The jury is still out on the effectiveness of the system.\par \par \uc2 The urgency for regulatory reform is becoming acute, as risks accumulate in China's financial system. Bad loans and credit defaults are on the rise. Overall debt nearly doubled to 245% of the country's GDP last year from 133% in 2008. Meanwhile, banks, brokerages and other institutions create more-risky products that fall into a regulatory vacuum.\par \par \uc2 But giving the task of filling such loopholes to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sets it up for the dilemma of needing to curb risk while also using monetary policy to boost growth.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  has refrained from taking more-aggressive credit-easing measures, as data show much of the money created by easing has flowed into speculative trading activities.\par \par \uc2 "It would be a very difficult balancing act for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  between trying to rein in reckless lending and ensuring loose monetary conditions to boost growth," said Zhang Ming, a senior economist at the China Academy of Social Sciences, a government think tank.\par \par \uc2 --Jon Hilsenrath in Washington contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 15, 2016 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160715ec7f000gl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
