\par\par \b \uc2 Philippine Peso Recovers,  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention \highlight\uc2  Suspected -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 222 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2016\par 06:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0440 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The Philippine peso has recovered from Tuesday's four-month low of 47.50, as suspected dollar-selling by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the USD/PHP back down to 47.38. The peso is still weaker than its Monday closing of 47.31, and is at risk of falling further. Speculative USD/PHP contracts traded offshore suggest the market is placing very bearish bets against the peso. The price gap between the onshore USD/PHP and the 1-month non-deliverable forward contract has grown to 0.20 from nearly par just four days ago. The widening gap, with the offshore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading much weaker than the onshore equivalent, reveals that speculators are betting against the peso in the offshore market where \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces aren't present. Anxiety over \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 -Philippine relations may be partly to blame for the peso's selloff.\par \par \uc2  The international court is due to pass its judgement today on a 2013 case brought by the Philippines against China's claims to large swathes of the South China Sea. The USD/PHP is now at 47.38 from its Monday closing of 47.31. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 July 12, 2016 00:40 ET (04:40 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160712ec7c000b7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
