\par\par \b \uc2 EM ASIA  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  down before US jobs data; yuan set for 5th week of losses\b0\par\par\uc2 791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juli 2016\par 08:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par * Yuan to see longest weekly loss in 7 months\par     * Higher \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves indicates less \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par     * Emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fall for week\par \par  (Adds text, updates prices)\par     By Jongwoo Cheon\par     SINGAPORE, July 8 (Reuters) - Most emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par slid on Friday ahead of a key U.S. jobs report, with regional\par units set to see weekly losses on anxiety over the fallout from\par the Brexit vote.\par     China's yuan edged down as a surprise rise in\par June \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves suggested the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be\par scaling back suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in support of the renminbi.\par  The Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was on course for a fifth\par straight week of depreciation, the longest weekly losing streak\par in nearly seven months.\par     The South Korean won and the Malaysian ringgit\par  eased in thin trading as traders added dollar holdings\par ahead of U.S. nonfarm payrolls data for June due later in the\par day.\par     The consensus forecast is for 175,000 new jobs for June,\par according to a Reuters poll. Data on Thursday already showed\par U.S. private payrolls increased more than expected last month as\par small businesses ramped up hiring, while fewer Americans applied\par for unemployment benefits last week. \par     "In the event of upside surprises in tonight's U.S. jobs\par data, the bond market will have to ask itself if it had been too\par hasty in using Brexit as an excuse to remove all expectations\par for the Fed to hike this year," analysts for DBS said in a note.\par     Still, many analysts and traders said a solid U.S. jobs data\par may not necessarily revive expectations of a near-term interest\par rate hike by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     Investors have virtually priced out short term rate hikes in\par the United States on concerns over the negative impact on the\par global economy and financial markets from Britain's vote to\par leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par     "The Fed will have a very small window to raise rates this\par year. I expect only one hike at most for 2016, but I doubt they\par will be able to do so," said a senior Philippine bank trader in\par Manila.\par     \par     FIFTH WEEKLY LOSSES IN YUAN\par     The yuan has eased 0.4 percent against the dollar so far\par this week for the fifth straight weekly loss, longest such\par losing streak since mid-December.\par     Bets on further declines in the renminbi hit a five-month\par high in the last two weeks on expectations that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par will allow it to weaken further to support the world's\par second-largest economy, a Reuters poll showed on Thursday.\par  \par     "The CNY will of course stay weak and will be impacted by\par the weaker GBP and EUR, given China's large trade ties with the\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2 ," said Heng Koon How, a senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist for Credit\par Suisse Private Banking Asia Pacific.\par     "Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will tend to remain soft alongside the CNY as\par market sentiment remains rather risk adverse."\par     The won led regional weekly losses with a 1.4 percent slide\par against the dollar, as offshore funds sold the best-performing\par emerging Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week.\par     The ringgit has eased 1.2 percent in the holiday-shortened\par week to mark the end of the Muslim fasting month. Most of\par Malaysia's government bond prices fell on Friday.\par     The Philippine peso has lost 0.4 percent, while\par the Singapore dollar has skidded 0.3 percent. Taiwan's\par dollar fell 0.3 percent.\par \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR\par     Change on the day at 0605 GMT\par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                Latest bid   Previous day  Pct Move\par   Japan yen                  100.46         100.70     +0.24\par   Sing dlr                   1.3496         1.3494     -0.01\par  *Taiwan dlr                 32.306         32.306      0.00\par   Korean won                1160.79        1154.60     -0.53\par   Baht                        35.20          35.18     -0.07\par   Peso                       47.100         47.000     -0.21\par  *Rupiah                      13135          13135      0.00\par   Rupee                       67.46          67.39     -0.10\par   Ringgit                    4.0440         4.0250     -0.47\par   Yuan                       6.6875         6.6832     -0.06\par                                                             \par   Change so far in 2016                                     \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                Latest bid  End prev year  Pct Move\par   Japan yen                  100.46         120.30    +19.75\par   Sing dlr                   1.3496         1.4177     +5.05\par   Taiwan dlr                 32.306         33.066     +2