\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-Yuan's stealthy slide makes for cooler summer\b0\par\par\uc2 588 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juli 2016\par 09:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are his own.)(Refiles to remove extraneous word "to" in paragraph 2.)               \par \par \uc2 By Pete Sweeney               \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, July 7 (Reuters Breakingviews) - The yuan is back on the skids, but investors and regulators aren't panicking this time around. That's partly because Chinese companies have paid down some of their U.S. dollar debts, but also because the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  has become more transparent.               \par \par \uc2 It's almost a year since \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  spooked global markets by engineering a poorly timed depreciation. That prompted investors to dump the yuan while companies rushed to pay down dollar-denominated debts. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves shrunk dramatically, attracting a circle of short-sellers who bet that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2 's attempts to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would fail. Brokerage CLSA, for example, predicted in April that the yuan could lose as much as 25 percent of its value against the dollar by 2017.                              \par \par \uc2 Graphic: China reserves and bank \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/1J9rI7a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/1J9rI7a}}}\par \par \uc2 The global market selloff triggered by Britain's departure from the \uc2 European Union\uc2  has sent the yuan sliding again. It's down 1.5 percent against the dollar since the referendum result, touching 6.98 on July 6 - a level not seen since 2010. Over the same period, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has also fallen against the basket of other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  professes to track.               \par \par \uc2 Yet the market reaction this time could not be more different. There's little sign so far of the aggressive short-selling activity that followed last year's decline. Chinese investors appear calm: Shanghai's stock market index has risen more than 5 percent since the Brexit vote, even as other major benchmarks have tumbled.               \par \par \uc2 Chinese corporations also have less to fear from a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slide. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  estimates that mainland companies repaid $216 billion of overseas debt between June last year and the end of the first quarter. Lower domestic interest rates mean it is now cheaper to borrow at home.               \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deserves some credit for being more clear regarding its intentions. Last year the \uc2 PBOC\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  and overseas to prop up the yuan and squeeze short-sellers, and there's little doubt it has the firepower to do so again. But it has also made the guidance rate, which is fixed daily, more transparent and responsive to market conditions. As other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the pound and the Japanese yen swing wildly, there are worse places to be than yuan.               \par \par \uc2 On \uc2 Twitter{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/petesweeneypro"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/petesweeneypro}}}\par \par \uc2 CONTEXT NEWS               \par \par \uc2 - The Chinese yuan strengthened slightly against the U.S. dollar on July 7, ending a slide that has seen the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lose 1.6 percent of its value since Britain voted to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2  on June 23. By mid-morning in Hong Kong the dollar was worth 6.683 yuan, up about 0.1 percent.               \par \par \uc2 - An index of the yuan's value against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  compiled by \uc2 Thomson Reuters\uc2  and the Hong Kong Stock Exchange (.RXYY) fell to 95.17, down from 96.06 before the British referendum.               \par \par \uc2 - Reuters: China to tolerate weaker yuan, wary of trade partners' reaction - sources               \par \par \uc2 - Reuters: China foreign debt falls as yuan depreciation fears resurface               \par \par \uc2 - Graphic: China reserves and bank \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  flows: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/1J9rI7a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/1J9rI7a}}}\par \par \uc2 RELATED COLUMN               \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Editing by Peter Thal Larsen and Katrina Hamlin) \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-7-7T08:50:47.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160707ec770073o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
