\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2  c.bank suspected of  \highlight22\uc2 intervening \highlight\uc2  to stabilise onshore yuan-traders\b0\par\par\uc2 158 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juni 2016\par 10:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI, June 30 (Reuters) - State-owned Chinese banks were suspected of selling dollars on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan in the onshore market on Thursday, after Reuters reported that Beijing was willing to let the yuan weaken to 6.80 per dollar this year, traders said.\par \par \par \par \uc2 "Major state-owned banks were offering dollar liquidity," said a trader at a European bank in Shanghai. "Although the market knows that China is tolerating more yuan weakness, investors were still surprised by this sort of confirmation."\par \par \par \par \uc2 The yuan weakened to as low as 6.6549 per dollar in mid-afternoon trading after the Reuters report, down from its intraday high of 6.6398.\par \par \par \par \uc2 Traders said state banks then started selling dollars and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilised around 6.6450.     (Reporting by Lu Jianxin and John Ruwitch; Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-7-3T08:34:10.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160630ec6u00dw1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
