\par\par \b \uc2 'Brexit' Upends China's Plans for Turmoil-Free Summer\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lingling Wei   \par \uc2 981 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Juni 2016\par 16:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BEIJING -- A year after an epic Chinese stock market crash reverberated around the world, China's leaders took steps not to be caught off guard by market gyrations again -- a calculation upended by the British vote to exit the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A week before Britons voted, officials at China's top securities watchdog emphasized in a meeting that the stock turmoil of last summer must not be repeated this year, according to people close to the agency.\par \par \uc2 A couple of days later, when Premier \uc2 Li Keqiang\uc2  visited the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary-policy department, he made a similar point: The yuan must be kept stable.\par \par \uc2 Now, China is on the receiving end of global market turbulence triggered by the 'Brexit' shock. And no part of its financial system is more vulnerable than the yuan, already the subject of a precarious \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  high-wire act.\par \par \uc2 With all major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  except the dollar and the yen plunging in the wake of the British vote, pressure grows on the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  to also let the yuan weaken. But if the gap with the dollar becomes too big, capital outflows speed up again.\par \par \uc2 A continued surge in the dollar and plunge in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could present a "nightmare scenario" for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it seeks to control the descent of the Chinese yuan, said a trader at one of China's largest state-owned banks.\par \par \uc2 Both Chinese stocks and the yuan fell Friday as the vote results became clear, though not as precipitously as elsewhere, with traders saying state \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  cushioned the fall.\par \par \uc2 Many of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's large state-owned banks in the past several days have cautioned customers about heightened risks in trading of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gold as a result of market mayhem following the U.K.'s vote.\par \par \uc2 The 'Brexit' decision has heightened uncertainty for markets and "will cast a shadow over the global economy," China's Finance Minister Lou Jiwei said Sunday.\par \par \uc2 At a closed-door Communist Party conclave in December, President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  named "unpredictable" international situations as a major risk faced by China in its attempt to move toward a "new normal" of slower but more sustainable growth, according to officials who attended the meeting.\par \par \uc2 How the Chinese authorities respond to the shock matters to markets world-wide because instability in China's markets could easily add to global jitters. The growing financial links between China and the world were on full display twice in the past year, when first the stock market crash and then a surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation sent shock waves through global markets.\par \par \uc2 Conflicting messaging from Beijing in the past year contributed to volatility and Chinese officials have gingerly moved toward more openness.\par \par \uc2 Late Friday, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  issued a brief statement, saying it has plans in place to withstand any shock from the 'Brexit' vote. International Monetary Fund Managing Director Christine Largarde called the statement "very helpful and very reassuring." Speaking at the \uc2 IMF\uc2  headquarters in Washington on Friday, \uc2 Zhou Xiaochuan\uc2 , governor of the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2 , also said China is in talks with the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , global central banks and other authorities to "safeguard financial market stability."\par \par \uc2 China's lines of defense include restrictions on money flowing in and out of the country, a large -- albeit dwindling -- stockpile of dollar reserves that can be used to prop up the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and otherwise buttress the financial system, a relatively low level of foreign debt and room for further easing to bolster growth.\par \par \uc2 The British decision to leave the \uc2 EU\uc2  will also affect investment and trade flows with China, but that impact will take months if not years to play out. In the near term, the most immediate impact on China will be in financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Officials at the \uc2 China Securities Regulatory Commission\uc2  pledged to "protect the markets" in the next two months at their meeting June 17, the people with knowledge of the gathering said. Foreign investors have limited access to stocks traded in the mainland, while Chinese individuals' abilities to buy foreign stocks are also tightly controlled.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index dropped 1.3% on Friday, a mild consequence compared with the 6.8% stock fall in Germany, 8% slide in France and the more than 12% declines in Greece, Spain and Italy.\par \par \uc2 Beijing's tougher task is how to continue its strategy of letting some air out of the yuan without triggering cash outflows and market instability. Analysts estimated cash streaming out at as much as $1 trillion in 2015. China recently tightened controls over companies and individuals trying to move money out. \uc2 UBS Group AG\uc2  estimates that outflows dropped to about $40 billion a month recently from more than $150 billion a month in December and January.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sought to make China's exchange-rate regime more predictable and shift the focus away from how the yuan trades against the dollar to its value against a broader group of 13 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, its maneuvering remains driven by the dollar and stands to get more perilous if fallout of the British vote keeps the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rising for an extended period, traders and analysts say.\par \par \uc2 Many traders expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to more aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the yuan if it weakens past 6.7 against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Brexit will be a stress test for China's new exchange-rate regime," said David Loevinger, the U.S.'s former Treasury representative in China and now a fund manager at TCW in Los Angeles. "Priority No. 1 will be avoiding a return of large depreciation expectations and capital flight."\par \par \uc2 Write to Lingling Wei at lingling.wei@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 26, 2016 10:12 ET (14:12 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160626ec6q0006s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
