\par\par \b \uc2 Brexit Is Bad for Growth and Everything Linked to It -- Brexit Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,358 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Juni 2016\par 06:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0437 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] The U.K.'s vote to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2  will be bad for growth, and not just in Europe. The U.S. is now more likely to enter a recession this year, while the Chinese manufacturing sector's recession is due to continue. Meanwhile, prepare for a selloff in all assets linked to growth: stocks, industrial metals, oil and bond yields, to name but a few. "The negative economic and financial market implications of a Brexit are likely to play out in a pronounced way for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , commodity and equity markets in the coming days," writes Jason Schenker of Prestige Economics. (daniel.strumpf@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0435 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] As a U.K. decision to leave the \uc2 European Union\uc2  becomes a reality, central banks will offer "all the liquidity that may be needed to prevent any shortage of liquidity in the financial system," says Berenberg chief economist Holger Schmieding, noting that swap arrangements among each other are in place so that liquidity can be offered in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  required. He said \uc2 the ECB\uc2 's QE program of EUR80 billion per month "limits the risk of a dramatic blowout in yield spreads as markets ratchet into risk-off mode." (todd.buell@wsj.com; Twitter: @ToddBuell) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0435 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Although the outcome of the U.K. referendum is unlikely to affect China's immediate economic outlook, ANZ says it keeps in play one more additional RRR cut. "China can exhibit its status as a global citizen and signal its willingness to lift market sentiment due to Brexit," it adds. This morning we have seen a significant increase in market volatility globally and we wouldn't overlook any \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  action this weekend, ANZ adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0435 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The UK's vote to leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  will place added pressure on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  to take action at its policy meeting next month, after the yen hit its highest level since Nov. 2013. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stood pat just last week, despite faltering inflation and a stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But will the BOJ have the firepower to reverse the tide of yen appreciation in a risk-off market? The yen, whose weakness is a key element of the government's economic program, is seen as a haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . (henry.hoenig@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0432 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The British vote to exit the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2  will have "very disruptive" effects in the short-term to financial markets and "drag" economic growth and employment, particularly in the U.K., in the long-term, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tim Adams\f28 \uc2 \f2 , president and CEO of the Institute of International Finance, a trade group of finance institutions based in Washington D.C. He says that the full extent of the impact is unclear. After volatile trading during the counting of the polls, the pound is down 10% at $1.34 since polls closed. Now comes the hard part: designing an exit from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 , a process that analysts say could take two years. Policymakers "now have a responsibility to quickly clarify the long-term relationship between the two parties in order to minimize uncertainty and promote investment and hiring," says IIF. (anjie.zheng@wsj.com; Twitter: @anjiezheng) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0431 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Bitcoin has become something similar to gold in the world markets -- a kind of electronic safe haven in times of turmoil. Such is the case today, with the price of Bitcoin, as tracked by CoinDesk, up about 7% to $669.12. (dennis.berman@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0417 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Shares of information-technology firm \f28 \uc2 \f2 HCL Infosystems\f28 \uc2 \f2  (500179.BY) and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tech Mahindra\f28 \uc2 \f2  (532755.BY), companies which get around 30% of their revenues from the eurozone, fell 4.5% and 3.7%, respectively, on Friday, as results showed that the U.K. will likely leave the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The broader S&P \f28 \uc2 \f2 BSE\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sensex is down 2.8%. (shefali.anand@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0407 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Indian shares and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  open sharply lower as a reaction to the U.K. polls results that suggest a growing lead for the 'Leave' vote in the country's referendum. The S&P \f28 \uc2 \f2 BSE\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sensex falls 2.8% to 26,237.74, while the National Stock Exchange's Nifty 50 index slips 2.7% to 8,044.20. The rupee also declines to 68.13 against the dollar, compared with Thursday's close of 67.25. Among major losers, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tata Motors\f28 \uc2 \f2  (500570.BY) falls 8.7%, the most among Sensex stocks, as it gets a large chunk of its revenue from the U.K. through its unit \f28 \uc2 \f2 Jaguar Land Rover\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tata Steel\f28 \uc2 \f2  (500470.BY)declines 5.5%, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 ICICI Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  (532174.BY) falls 6.4%. (debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0407 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] A further cut in interest rates in Australia now looks more likely as the UK referendum points to the country's exit from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 EU\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Michael Blythe, chief economist at the Commonwealth Bank of Australia, said the Brexit vote could be the beginning of the end for the 'European Project.' "You would have to put your money on "leave" getting up," he added. It is going to add to the case for lower interest rates in Australia in the second half of the year, he added. (james.glynn@wsj.com; Twitter @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0406 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] With the yen strengthening beyond Y100 against the dollar for the first time since November 2013, Japanese government officials are highly concerned. Even before the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  breached the psychologically important level, Deputy Chief Cabinet Secretary Hiroshige Seko said any sharp moves in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates are "not desirable." Japan's top \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bureaucrat Masatsugu Asakawa also says the yen's movement over the past few hours has been "unusually rough" and he will "consider a response in consultation with the finance minister." Traders will closely watch a news conference by finance minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Taro Aso\f28 \uc2 \f2  set to begin 0415 GMT for any hints on the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The dollar is currently at Y100.96. (megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com and renanakachi.simkowitz@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0400 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was shoring up the yuan's value via \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the onshore market, after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fell to its lowest level in nearly five and a half years earlier due to concerns about the U.K. referendum, traders say. The USD/CNY is now at 6.6091, down a touch from an intraday high of 6.6148, which marked the yuan's weakest point since mid-January 2011. "\f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is definitely in the market but the magnitude of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't too big. I guess people are still waiting for the final outcome of the referendum in Britain," says a Shanghai-based senior trader at a domestic bank. (hong.shen@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0358 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] South Korea's won slumped as much as 2.5% against the U.S. dollar Friday, falling to 1,179.8 compared to its last closing of 1,150.2. The catalyst for the extreme move, the biggest intraday fluctuation since September 2011, was the incoming results of the U.K. referendum. As results continue to trickle in, financial market participants have gone into panic mode, buying the haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the U.S. dollar and the Japanese yen, which rose as much as 7.2% versus the greenback. The USD/KRW eased off slightly, to trade at 1,178.4 currently, possibly on expectations that \f28 \uc2 \f2 the Bank of Korea\f28 \uc2 \f2  might soon \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to tamp down this bout of excessive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility. A weaker won, though could be in the government's interest, since it helps prop up South Korea's exports. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 June 24, 2016 00:37 ET (04:37 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160624ec6o000dr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
