\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Sell-off abates as Brexit opinion seen shifting\b0\par\par\uc2 907 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2016\par 14:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Death of British lawmaker seen changing Brexit opinions\par \par \uc2 * European shares rise one percent\par \par \uc2 * Yen gives up some gains but still poised for winning week\par \par \uc2 * Oil recovers ground, gold stabilises after wild swings\par \par \uc2 By Marc Jones\par \par \uc2 LONDON, June 17 (Reuters) - Shares, oil and bond yields rose on Friday after a tumultuous week and as campaigning for Britain's \uc2 EU\uc2  membership referendum next week was suspended after the killing of a pro-"Remain" politician.\par \par \uc2 European bourses climbed more than 1 percent at the end of a third straight week of losses and benchmark 10-year German bond yields clawed back up to the zero mark as investors' risk averseness eased.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street was expected to start flat however, having snapped a 5-day losing streak on Thursday and with housing data and Canadian inflation figures due to be released.\par \par \uc2 Campaigning for Britain's June 23 \uc2 EU\uc2  referendum, which overshadowed this week's U.S. and Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings, was put on hold after British lawmaker, Jo Cox, was murdered on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The recently volatile pound rose 0.5 percent to $1.4277 with analysts noting that Cox's death could generate sentiment in favour of remaining in the \uc2 EU\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There was this incredible melt down in risk sentiment (yesterday) where everything seemed to be aligning and then this terrible incident in England seemed to be the root of everything reversing," said \uc2 Saxo Bank\uc2 's head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy John Hardy.\par \par \uc2 "I think everyone is still in a state of shock as to what this means. Does it tilt the odds on a vote next week? It's hard to know but it certainly disrupted what the market was doing."\par \par \uc2 Oil, which has been a major driver of the sharp swings in global markets this year, also helped shift the mood as it rose for the first time in seven sessions. That decline has pushed prices down by 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 Euro zone banking stocks rebounded almost 4 percent having hit a four-year low - with all-time lows for banks including \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  and \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  - on Brexit worries and sub-zero interest rates, which are eating into earnings.\par \par \uc2 Gold also advanced, rising 0.4 percent to $1,283 an ounce after wild swings overnight. It had surged to a near-two-year high of more than $1,315, only to slump 2.8 percent by the end of the session. It was on track for a third week of gains.\par \par \uc2 "Leading into the Brexit vote, we expect gold to remain around current levels between the $1,270-$1,300 range. But after then all bets are off as everything depends on the results of the referendum," ANZ commodity strategist Daniel Hynes said.\par \par \uc2 DIGGING FOR TREASURIES\par \par \uc2 Ten year \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bond yields were hovering at 1.60 percent ahead of U.S. trading, tumbling from 1.86 percent at the beginning of June as investors made a dash for safe-haven assets.\par \par \uc2 They had touched close to 1.50 percent on Thursday, the lowest for four-years and that has dovetailed with 10-year yields going sub-zero in Germany, 15-year yields doing the same in Japan and even 30-year yields in Switzerland.\par \par \uc2 "This is latest chapter of a long running theme that is going to continue to play out," Hermes chief economist Neil Williams said.\par \par \uc2 "We are going to live in this world until the major central banks start turning the taps off, but my question is how they are going to do it."\par \par \uc2 Overnight, Asia's main stock markets saw modest gains although that was little consolation after a difficult week that saw both weak Chinese data and the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  hold off on more stimulus but talk loudly about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's CSI 300 index made 0.5 percent and the Shanghai Composite added 0.4 percent. That helped them cut weekly losses to 1.7 percent and 1.5 percent respectively.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng gained 0.5 percent, but suffered a more than 4-percent loss on the week, emerging markets worldwide were down 2.3 percent for the week while Japan's Nikkei ended it down more than 6 percent despite a 1.1 percent rise on the day.\par \par \uc2 "Continued inaction by the BOJ in the face of these (global) risks only reinforces the market's suspicions that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is running out of policy options, feeding back into a stronger yen," HSBC economist Izumi Devalier said in a note.\par \par \uc2 The dollar had clawed back some lost ground against the yen. It steadied at 104.23 yen but was still down 2.4 percent this week, having dropped to 103.555, its lowest since August 2014.\par \par \uc2 The euro meanwhile added 0.3 percent to 117.39 yen . It too was down 2.4 percent for the week against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  having hit a 3-year low of 115.51 on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Taro Aso\uc2  said on Friday that he was deeply concerned about "one-sided, rapid and speculative moves" seen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and would respond if necessary to ensure stability in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Officials from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and the Financial Services Agency planned to hold a meeting on Friday to discuss financial markets, Aso said.\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Louise Ireland) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-17T13:12:13.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160617ec6h00zkv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw174\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2610\pichgoal600
