\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 5-China gives U.S. investment quota for first time to deepen financial ties\b0\par\par\uc2 870 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2016\par 17:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * China to give U.S. a $38 billion RQFII investment quota\par \par \uc2 * Move could influence \uc2 MSCI\uc2  on adding China stocks -analyst\par \par \uc2 * China and U.S. to each pick a bank to conduct yuan clearing\par \par \uc2 * Agree to push reforms to raise emerging economies' \uc2 IMF\uc2  quotas (Adds detail on foreign financial firms' access to Chinese market)\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao and David Lawder\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, June 7 (Reuters) - China will give the United States a 250 billion yuan ($38 billion) investment quota for the first time to buy Chinese stocks, bonds and other assets, officials said on Tuesday, deepening financial ties and interdependence between the world's two largest economies.\par \par \uc2 China has given such quota allocations to several countries, including the UK, France and Singapore, but this would be the biggest given to a single jurisdiction after Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Chinese officials also repeatedly pledged in two days of talks with U.S. counterparts that they saw no need for sustained weakening of the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which many investors fear could shock the already sluggish U.S. and global economies and roil financial markets as happened in January.\par \par \uc2 The moves will allow Beijing to pursue its ambition of making the yuan a more widely used global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while giving U.S. investors greater access to China's domestic markets.\par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  vice governor, \uc2 Yi Gang\uc2 , announced the quota at the bilateral Strategic and Economic Dialogue talks in Beijing, without providing further details such as a timeframe.\par \par \uc2 "We believe the U.S. market is very important, so we granted 250 billion yuan in RQFII quotas to the United States," he said.\par \par \uc2 The Renminbi Qualified Foreign Institutional Investor programme, which was set up in late 2011, allows overseas financial institutions to use offshore yuan to buy securities in mainland China, including stocks, bonds and money market investments.\par \par \uc2 "The ability to do RMB transactions in the United States will be a real advantage, to small firms in particular and to large businesses that are not financial businesses," U.S. Treasury Secretary Jack Lew said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 "It will make it easier, it will make it cheaper," he said.\par \par \uc2 China and the United States will also each pick a qualified bank to conduct yuan clearing business in the United States, Vice Premier Wang Yang said.\par \par \uc2 The new quota will significantly expand the RQFII programme, under which 501.77 billion yuan had been allocated as of May.\par \par \uc2 The quota, which financial institutions will apply to use, is the first granted to the United States.\par \par \uc2 Ivan Shi, head of research at Shanghai-based fund consultancy Z-Ben Advisors, said the move also increased the chances that global investment index compiler \uc2 MSCI\uc2  will include Chinese shares in its index, a decision that could come next week, as it broadens foreign access to China's stock market.\par \par \uc2 "But its implementation depends on how widely the yuan is used in the U.S. and how much interest U.S. investors have toward Chinese stocks and bonds," he added.\par \par \uc2 China's regulators have been pushing to expand foreign investors' access to domestic financial markets to make its markets broader and attract more capital inflows.\par \par \uc2 But foreign interest has waned after a near meltdown in Chinese equity markets last year and subsequent heavy-handed official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore them up.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's cooling economy, growing debt levels and anxiety over its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy have also kept investors at bay.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts said the quota move appeared to be largely symbolic, as many others channels for investing in Chinese assets have opened up since the RQFII programme was launched in 2011.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said in February it would allow all types of financial institutions that are registered outside the country to buy bonds in the interbank market and would scrap quotas for medium- and long-term investors.\par \par \uc2 Yi said on Tuesday that internationalisation of the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be market-oriented.\par \par \uc2 Lew said during the talks that China was committed to continuing "market-oriented exchange rate reform that allows for two-way flexibility" of its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a statement following the talks, the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  said China agreed to allow foreign companies to have bigger equity stakes in domestic securities and fund management companies.\par \par \uc2 China also committed to welcoming qualified foreign firms and joint ventures to apply to engage in the private securities fund management business, including secondary market trading of securities, \uc2 Treasury\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The optimal window to make progress on a U.S.-China bilateral investment treaty was before the G20 leaders meeting in September, he said.\par \par \uc2 Aside from discussions about the internationalisation of the yuan, China and the United States also agreed to push forward reforms at the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  to increase quotas for emerging economies, which determine their voting powers in the organisation and access to financing.\par \par \uc2 "Both sides reiterated the allocation of \uc2 IMF\uc2  quotas should be shifted towards emerging markets and developing countries," Lew said. ($1 = 6.5712 Chinese yuan renminbi) (Additional reporting by Samuel Shen, Lindsay Dunsmuir and Jason Lange; Writing by Elias Glenn and Sue-Lin Wong; Editing by Will Waterman and Dan Grebler) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-7T16:23:45.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160607ec6700veq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
