\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China \highlight\uc2 's yuan eases as corporate dollar demand offsets suspected  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Juni 2016\par 06:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, June 7 (Reuters) - The yuan edged lower against\par the dollar on Tuesday, as companies bought the greenback \par hovering near four-week lows, offsetting suspected state-bank\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to shore up the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Easing expectations of an imminent U.S. interest rate\par increase will likely reduce pressure on the yuan in coming\par weeks, but traders said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue to hold a\par firm line on the renminbi to discourage speculators.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  set the midpoint rate \par at 6.5618 per dollar prior to market open, 0.18 percent weaker\par than the previous fix 6.5497. \par     Spot yuan opened at 6.5655 per dollar and was\par changing hands at 6.5718 at midday, easing 0.12 percent from the\par previous close.\par     Traders noted that state-owned banks were offering dollars\par at 6.57 to combat surging corporate dollar demand in early\par trade, and then retreated to 6.5720 around where the market\par dollar supply and demand reached a rough equilibrium.\par     State-owned banks typically \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market on\par behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , having offered dollars since early\par January to shore up the yuan when the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is faced\par with depreciation pressure, traders said.\par     The yuan is expected to weaken around 2.5 percent over the\par coming year, a Reuters poll showed last week, pressured by\par cooling economic growth and expectations for U.S. rates to trend\par higher. \par     The offshore yuan was trading 0.07 percent softer than the\par onshore spot at 6.5761 per dollar. \par \par     The yuan market at a glance: \par     \par     ONSHORE:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  PBOC midpoint      6.5618   6.5497    -0.18%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.5718   6.5642    -0.12%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    0.15%              \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change YTD                       -1.19%\par  Spot change since 2005                25.94%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument            Current   Difference\par                                  from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    6.5761    -0.07%\par         *                        \par  Offshore              6.73      -2.50%\par  non-deliverable                 \par  forwards                        \par                **                \par  \par *Premium for offshore spot over onshore \par **Figure reflects difference from \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's official midpoint,\par since non-deliverable forwards are settled against the midpoint.\par . \par     \par     \par \par     \par  (Reporting by the Shanghai Newsroom; Editing by Jacqueline\par Wong)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-6-