\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 China Intervention \highlight\uc2  in Offshore Yuan Market Unlikely -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 203 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2016\par 12:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0907 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Market chatter has it that \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in the offshore yuan market to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against speculators. But Patrick Wu, head of renminbi trading for ANZ, says there is small chance of that happening, at least not to the extent of earlier this year. That is because funding costs for taking short bets against CNH--the offshore yuan--are still low. If the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  had indeed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively--by buying offshore yuan likely through proxy Chinese banks in Hong Kong--liquidity would have dried up, making it expensive to bet against the CNH. Last Friday, funding costs were slightly more expensive--the only sign of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 --but now costs have gotten cheaper again. Now, it's mostly Chinese companies hedging against yuan depreciation, which could send the offshore yuan weaker, especially if the U.S. dollar rebounds, he says. The offshore yuan was as weak as 6.5588 per U.S.\par \par \uc2  dollar Monday morning but has slightly strengthened to 6.5447.(dominique.fong@wsj.com; @dominiquefong)\par \par \uc2 Editor: KLH\par \par \uc2 (Delayed by 1 hour)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 16-05-16 1027GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020160516ec5g0009n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
