\par\par \b \uc2 IFR Markets ForexWatch Asia Regional Daily Briefing\b0\par\par\uc2 3,624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2016\par 02:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SYDNEY, May 9 (IFR) - North America news and data\par * Smallest U.S. job gains in 7 months temper rate hike expectations; NFP 160k v\par 202k Rtrs f/c, 208k pvs\par * US avg earnings steady at 0.3%, workweek on target 34.5 v 34.4 pvs;\par participation rate/U6 lower\par * Canada employment -2.1k v 0k rtrs f/c, unemp rate steady at 7.1% v 7.2% Rtrs\par f/c\par * Fed's Dudley: Fed's Dudley says reasonable to expect two rate hikes this year\par (\f28 \uc2 \f2 NYT\f28 \uc2 \f2 )\par * Oil rises 1% \f28 \uc2 \f2 Baker Hughes\f28 \uc2 \f2  sees rig count fall fot seventh week in a row to Oct\par '09 low\par * Gold jumps after U.S. payrolls data misses forecasts\par * \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2  sees 1 Fed hike in '16, Blackrock sees 1 maybe none, Goldman/BofA\par expects next Fed hike in Sept\par * Brazil senate cmte votes for Rousseff impeachment trial, president likely\par suspended from office next week\par * Brazil's Barbosa: policies to fight inflation generating results, paves way\par for CVB rate cut in H2 '16\par * S&P revises Turkey's sovereign credit outlook to stable; rating BB+\par  \par News from the weekend\par  \par China trade data shows exports and imports weaker than expected\par * April exports minus 1.8%y/y vs minus 0.1 pct expected\par * April imports minus 10.9% y/y vs  minus 5.0 pct\par * April trade surplus $45.56 bln vs f'cast $40 bln\par  \par China trade data released on Sunday was disappointing, as both exports and\par imports were worse than expectations. The poor import and export data reversed\par the pleasant surprises to the upside that were contained in last month's China\par trade data. The data out of China follows last weekend's weak South Korea trade\par data and is sure to add to global growth concerns. The 10.9% fall in imports\par marks the 18th consecutive month of declines and is especially concerning; as it\par indicates domestic demand remains tepid despite government efforts to transition\par to a consumer based economy through heavy infrastructure spending.\par  \par Likely market reaction \par The markets haven't been overreacting to China data in recent weeks and that is\par due to reduced concerns over China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve position (see next story), but\par the global growth fears that saw most major commodities fall last week will\par likely increase after the weak China trade data. EM assets and commodity\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as the AUD, CAD and NZD fell last week and will likely open the\par Asian market under some pressure again on Monday morning. John.Noonan@tr.com\par     \par China \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves increase to $3.23 TLN vs expectations of dip to $3.20 TLN\par China released their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves data on Saturday and it was better than\par expected. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves increased to $3.23% TLN in April from $3.21 TLN in March\par with the market expecting a dip to $3.20 TLN. The second straight month of\par improving \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves indicates that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hasn't had to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as much - as\par capital outflows have tailed off from the rapid pace of late 2016/early 2016.\par The large falls in global equities, commodities and EM assets at the start of\par 2016 were tied to fears China's reserve position would continue to deteriorate\par and force China authorities to devalue the Yuan. Those fears have receded and\par the better \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve data released on Saturday should keep those concerns on\par the back burner.  John.Noonan@tr.com\par     \par Alberta fires explode across forests and could double in size - Global Mail\par * Weather still warm and dry; blaze spreads to 1,500 square km\par  \par * Blaze will take months to extinguish, Alberta fire official says\par * \f28 \uc2 \f2 Syncrude Canada\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shut down, staff evacuated\par * Fire could double in size by end of day, officials say\par  \par Alberta's wildfires took a severe turn for the worst over the weekend - with\par officials saying the fire could double in size within 24 hours. Reuters reports\par that at least 10 oil/sand operators have cut production due to the fire and\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Syncrude Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  is the latest to shut down. Also Saturday, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Husky Energy Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par said it had shut down oil production at its Sunrise joint venture with \f28 \uc2 \f2 BP PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par According to the Globe and Mail Syncrude, a joint venture of several energy\par companies led by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Suncor Energy Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Imperial Oil Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 ., produces up to\par 350,000 barrels a day of light synthetic crude derived from the oil sands.\par Already this week, as much 1 million barrels a day - about a quarter of Canada's\par oil production had been shut off.\par  \par The fall in Canada energy production could push the price of crude higher in the\par short-term. The huge costs to the Canadian economy due to the enormous fire will\par weigh on the Canadian Dollar even if the Crude price moves higher.  \par John.Noonan@tr.com\par     \par The week ahead - All eyes on Japan return from holiday\par It will be a relatively quiet week for key data and events. The Asian markets\par eagerly await the return of Japan following the Golden Week holidays. The\par USD/JPY has formed a base above 105.00 for the time-being and this may calm\par Japanese official concern over the one-way JPY move higher that occurred\par following the surprise "no change" decision by the BOJ. Bank of Japan Governor\par Kuroda will speak at an event hosted by Jiji news agency at 22:55 GMT on Monday.\par  If Japan returns and pushed the Nikkei higher due to fading fears over the\par possibility of the USD/JPY breaching 105.00 it could set a positive tone in\par Asian markets.\par  \par The BOE meets this week and while no one is expecting a change in policy - but\par there are reports the BOE may lower their growth forecasts and possibly issue a\par warning on the UK economy if a BREXIT was the result of the June 23 referendum.\par     \par Key data in the week ahead\par It will be a very quiet start to the week for US data. Most of the key data will\par be out on Friday when Retail Sales, PPI and Univ of Mich consumer sentiment is\par released. Key data from the Euro zone includes SENTIX and German Industrial\par Orders on Monday; German Trades and Indutrial Output on Tuesday; EZ IP on\par Thursday and EZ Q1 GDP flash estimate and German inflation on Friday. UK Trades\par will be out on Tuesday and IP will be released on Wednesday. Japan Current\par Account is out on Wednesday and wilol the main economic release in Japan next\par week. Australia will release some second tier data including ANZ job ads on\par Monday and housing finance on Wednesday. The key event in China next week will\par be China CPI and PPI out on Tuesday.  John.Noonan@tr.com\par  \par Best to worst performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week\par CCY   APR029       May04      %Change\par EUR   1.1455      1.1406      -0.43%\par JPY   106.45      107.12      -0.63%\par GBP   1.4608      1.4435      -1.18%\par CHF   0.9595      0.9724      -1.34%\par NZD   0.6976      0.6837      -1.99%\par CAD   1.2555      1.2908      -2.81%\par AUD   0.7603      0.7370      -3.06%\par Comment\par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market built up large USD shorts coming into last week and this helped\par the USD move broadly higher. The IMM data released on Friday showed net USD\par shorts at USD 6.46 BLN - the highest in over 3 years. The commodity \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par suffered the most - led by a 3.06% fall in the AUD/USD last week. The RBA rate\par cut was flowed up with a dovish RBA SOMP - while the huge fall in the iron ore\par price added more weight. The CAD didn't fare much better than the AUD - with the\par USD/CAD rising 2.81% last week. The fall in the CAD looks overdone, as crude oil\par only lost a small bit of ground. The uncertain economic impact of the Canadian\par wildfires might be causing potential CAD buyers to hesitate.  John.Noonan@tr.com\par Themes for Friday's trading\par  \par * The main theme for trading on Friday was a muted reaction to the headline miss\par on US non-farm payrolls, as the details of the data were mixed and didn't\par significantly alter Fed expectations.\par  \par * While the headline plus 160 K new jobs missed the expectation of plus 202K -\par the plus 0.3% in the average hourly earnings would have pleased the Fed and the\par average work week edged higher to 34.5 hours.\par  \par * The futures market has virtually priced out a June Fed rate hike with the\par pricing indicating just a one in 10 chance the Fed will ease next month.  The\par market is pricing in just over a 40% chance they will hike in September while\par the chances of a December Fed hike remain around 60%.\par  \par * Wall Street had a choppy reaction to the US payroll data, but by the end of\par the day modest gains were achieved.\par  \par * The Dow closed up 79.92 points or 0.45% higher at 17,740.63; the S&P closed up\par 8.51 points or 0.32% higher at 2,057.14 and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NASDAQ\f28 \uc2 \f2  closed up 19.06 points,\par or 0.4 % higher at 4,736.16.\par  \par * For the week the Dow eased 0.19%; the S&P fell 0.40%, and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NASDAQ\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell\par 0.80%\par  \par * The VIX fell sharply on Friday - closing down around 7.5% at 14.72. For the\par week the VIX fell 6.24% despite Wall Street, Emerging markets and most key\par commodities posting weekly losses.\par  \par * Most key commodities close the day higher after a tough week.\par  \par * \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude edged 0.77% higher to 44.66, as the Canadian wildfire halted\par production of some large Canadian oil sands producers. For the week \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude\par closed down 2.74%.\par  \par * Lon Copper rose 0.53% on Friday, but for the week it suffered a steep 4.7%\par loss. Gold rose 0.86% on Friday and for the week it edged 0.39% lower.\par  \par * Iron ore continued to plunge - falling 3.03% to 57.70. Iron ore suffered a\par huge 11.50% fall from last week's close at 65.20.\par  \par * Emerging markets stopped falling on Friday with the EM Equity ETF (EEM) edging\par 0.15% higher. For the week it fell 4.54% and it is down over 7% from the April\par 19 high.\par  \par * Despite the gains on Wall Street, most commodities and steady EM trading - the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market continued to trade with a "risk-off" tone.\par  \par * The market tried to sell the US dollar in the wake of the weak US jobs\par headline number. The EUR/USD jumped to 1.1482 from 1.1430 before quickly falling\par back to 1.1400/20. The intraday moves in the EUR/USD and USD/JPY were influenced\par by the moves in \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields.\par  \par * The 2-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 US Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yield fell to 0.68% at one stage following the jobs\par report - before closing at 0.73% - up 1 \f28 \uc2 \f2 BP\f28 \uc2 \f2  on the day. The 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par yield traded as low as 1.70% before jumping to 1.77% on the close - up 3 BPs on\par the day.\par  \par *  The recovery in the \f28 \uc2 \f2 US Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields was due to the improvement in average\par hourly earnings, as the Treasury markets sees the trend in wages growth as the\par most important in determining the Fed's flight path/\par  \par * The EUR/USD closed the day unchanged at 1.1406 while the USD/JPY eased 0.13%.\par The AUD was the biggest lower on the day, but all of the AUD selling was in Asia\par following the dovish RBA SOMP.\par  \par * The AUD/USD closed down 1.30% at 0.7370 while the EUR/AUD also fell 1.30% and\par the AUD/JPY fell 1.43% on the day.\par  \par * The NZD and CAD also underperformed with the NZD/USD falling 0.65% and the\par USD/CAD rising 0.43%.\par  \par * The USD/CAD gained despite the steady to higher oil price, as sluggish Canada\par jobs data and fears over the economic impact of the Canadian wild fires weighed\par in CAD sentiment.\par  \par * The GBP/USD fell to an 11-day low on Friday, as BREXIT concerns continue to\par weigh as the June 23 referendum draws nearer.  The GBP/USD closed the day down\par 0.33% at 1.4435.\par     \par Wrap-up  \par The weak China import and export data could weigh on sentiment when the markets\par open on Monday morning. They feed growing global growth concerns and could\par result in further weakness in commodities, commodity \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and some EM\par equities and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The market haven't been reacting to misses on China\par data of late, as CNY devaluation fears have faded and the better China \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par reserves released on Saturday should see those concerns continue to dissipate.\par  \par Nevertheless, commodity market bears have been saying for a couple of weeks that\par the moves higher in iron ore, crude and copper were overextended to the upside\par and needs better global growth and trade data for the moves to be sustainable.\par While there is a strong consensus there is a bottom in place in \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude\par ahead of 26, there is a lot of doubt the price will reach 50 in the short-term\par at least. A break below the trend line around 41 and the 200-day MA around 40\par would suggest a short-term top is in place in \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude and consolidation\par between 30 and 40 lies ahead. If this proves to be the case - it could lend\par support to the USD while stirring up volatility in EM, equities and commodity\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par  \par Japan returns from an extending holiday and Tokyo flows should have a\par significant impact on Monday. There is a growing view on the market that USD\par weakness over the past couple of months looks a bit stretched and the USD is\par ready to stage a decent recovery. The fact that the USD made some ground last\par week despite a slightly dovish turn in Fed expectations supports the USD rebound\par thesis.  This view may encourage USD/JPY demand out of Tokyo, if Japanese market\par participants believe   the USD/JPY is ready to settle in a 105/110 range for\par awhile. If the USD/JPY does manage to move higher it should underpin the Nikkei\par - much to the relief of Japanese officials.\par  \par The AUD/USD should remain under pressure, as the dovish turn in RBA expectations\par along with the heavy fall in iron ore forces out weak longs and encourages fresh\par selling. The RBNZ (along with the BOJ and ECB) would be very envious of the RBA.\par The AUD/USD was tracking higher before the RBA cut rates and the complexion of\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has dramatically shifted to bearish since they decided to act. It\par breaks a recent trend where \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  easing did nothing to stop upward\par momentum of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and in some cases increased it. That could be one\par of the reasons AUD longs didn't worry too much about what the RBA might do in\par the lead up to last week's RBA cut.  The AUD/USD is poised to break below the\par 100-day MA at 0.7335/40, but will face stiffer support at the 200-day MA, which\par came in around 0.7260 on Friday.   John.Noonan@tr.com\par     \par Technical view\par  \par Equities\par  \par S&P The S&P looks vulnerable in the short-term, as the 5, 10 and 20-day moving\par averages are now aligned in a bearish formation. The trend lower hasn't yet\par started, as the 20-day MA continues to point higher and needs to turn lower to\par signal the trend lower has commenced. The S&P traded below the 50-day MA (2,045)\par on Friday, but managed to close above that reading. A close below the 50-day MA\par targets the 200-daay MA at 2,013. Key support is found at   ,1995 where the\par 100-day MA and 38.2 of the 1,810/2,111 move converge. A move back above the\par descending 10-day MA (2,072 on Friday) would relieve the downward pressure.  \par \{Last 2,057.14\}\par  \par ASX According to the short-date moving average study - the ASX is trending\par higher, but the price action isn't convincing. The 5, 10 and 20-day moving\par averages are aligned in a bullish formation and they all point higher.  The late\par bounce and strong close on Friday was encouraging, as the morning dip to 5,200\par stayed well above the ascending 20-day MA - which came in at 5,177 on Friday.\par For the trend higher to be sustained and gain momentum - the price needs to\par cross above the Oct 26, 2015 high at 5,384 within the next two weeks - while\par holding above the 20-day MA.  A fall below 5.175 would be a strong indication\par the short-term trend higher is over before it really started.  \{Last 5,292.04\}\par  \par Commodities\par  \par Gold The short-term moving averages remain in a bullish formation, but the 5-day\par MA is starting to turn lower - a warning that upward momentum is waning. The\par pullbacks continue to hold above the ascending 10-day MA (1,271 on Friday) and\par while that reading holds the trend higher could reignite at any time. A break\par below 1,245 (61.8 fibo of the 1,208/1,303 move) would confirm the short-term\par trend higher is over and a short-term top is in place at 1,303. The medium-trend\par higher remains in place, as the 50, 100 and 200-day moving averages are aligned\par in a bullish formation and pointing higher. A break below the ascending 100-day\par MA (1,190 on Friday) would warn that upside momentum is exhausted. Key\par resistance is found at the 2015 high at 1,306, which was validated by the\par pullback from 1,303.    \{Last 1,289\}\par  \par Iron Ore The short-term trend higher ended last week, as the 5-day MA has\par crossed below the 10-day MA. The next level of support is found at the ascending\par 50-day MA (57.20 on Friday) and a break below that level targets major support\par at 49/51 where the 100-day and 200-day moving averages are converging. A break\par above the ascending 20-day MA (61.12 on Friday) is needed to shift the pressure\par back to the upside.  \{Last 57.70\}\par  \par Lon Copper \par The short-term trend higher ended last week when the 5-day MA crossed below the\par 10-day MA. Lon Copper isn't trending in either direction in both the short and\par medium term. The next level of support is found at the ascending 100-day MA\par (4,716 on Friday) and a break below that level targets the quadruple bottom\par formed between 4,630/4,640. Resistance has formed between 4,870/4,880 where the\par 10 and 50-day moving averages converge. Major resistance is found at the 2016\par high at 5,131. \{Last 4,810.15\}\par  \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par Crude The short-term trend higher in \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude is showing signs of exhaustion,\par as the 5-day MA is pointing lower and the 10-day MA has flattened out. The price\par has held above the ascending 20-day MA (43.09 on Friday) and while it continues\par to do so - the trend higher can resume at any time. A break below 42.50 targets\par key support around 40.00 - where the 50-day and 200-day moving averages\par converge. Technical analysts are also looking at an ascending trend-line from\par 26.05 that come in around 41.20 on Friday. Key resistance is found at the 61.8\par fibo of the 62.58/26.05 move at 48.65.  \{Last 44.66\}\par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUR/USD  The short-term trend higher is showing signs of exhaustion, as the\par 5-day MA has started to point lower. The 5, 10 and 20-day moving averages remain\par in a bullish formation and while the 20-day MA (1.1357 on Friday) holds - the\par trend higher can resume at any time. The medium trend higher looks solid with\par the 50, 100 and 200-day moving averages aligned in a bullish formation and\par pointing higher. A break below the 20-day MA could see a drift back top support\par around 1.1215. A break back above former resistance at 1.1465 would shift the\par pressure to the upside.  \{Last 1.1406\}\par  \par USD/JPY  The 5, 10 and 20-day moving averages are aligned in a bearish\par formation, but the 5-day MA is starting to point higher, which means it isn't\par trending in the short-term at least. The medium-term trend lower looks strong,\par as the 50-day, 100-day and 200-day moving averages are pointing lower and\par aligned in a bearish formation. . Resistance is found at the 38.2 fibo of the\par 111.90/105.55 move at 107.95. A break above that level targets 108.70/80 where\par the 50% of that move and the 20-day MA converge. Key support has formed at the\par 105.55 level. \{Last 107.12\}\par  \par AUD/USD The AUD/USD has started trending lower - with the 5, 10 and 20-day\par moving averages aligned in a bearish formation and all pointing lower. The next\par level of support is found at the ascending 100-day MA, which came in at 0.7336\par on Friday. A break below that level would warn the medium trend higher is about\par to end - and a break below the 200-day MA (0.7266 on Friday) would confirm that\par to be the case. A break above the 50-day MA at 0.7565 is needed to relieve the\par downward pressure. \{Last 0.7370\}  John.noonan@tr.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-12T00:21:28.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160509ec59001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
