\par\par \b \uc2 METALS-Copper sinks towards 1-month low as dollar stands tall\b0\par\par\uc2 451 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Mai 2016\par 03:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE, May 9 (Reuters) - London copper sank towards its lowest in a month on Monday after the dollar withstood a weaker U.S. jobs report that largely kept rate hike expectations intact.\par \par \uc2 FUNDAMENTALS\par \par \uc2 * Three-month copper on the \uc2 London Metal Exchange\uc2  slipped by 1.0 percent to $4,760 a tonne by 0108 GMT, reversing a small gain in the previous session. Copper shed 4.8 percent last week for its biggest weekly drop since November. Prices on Friday plumbed the lowest since April 12 at $4,755.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Shanghai Futures Exchange\uc2  copper eased 0.4 percent to 36,450 yuan ($5,658) a tonne.\par \par \uc2 * The U.S. economy added the fewest number of jobs in seven months in April and Americans dropped out of the labour force, but encouraging annual wage growth backed views that the U.S. could still raise rates twice this year.\par \par \uc2 * New York Federal Reserve President \uc2 William Dudley\uc2  said two U.S. rate hikes this year were still a "reasonable expectation", adding upward drive to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 * A run of Chinese data is expected to show activity moderated in April after a strong showing in March. A Reuters poll forecast a small drop in all-important exports last month.\par \par \uc2 * Hedge funds and money managers raised their net long positions in COMEX gold and copper contracts in the week to May 3, \uc2 U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission\uc2  data showed on Friday.\par \par \uc2 * China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose in April to $3.22 trillion, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Saturday, marking a second monthly rise this year and suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is easing off its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  as capital outflows ease.\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's imports of copper fell 21.1 percent from month ago to 450,000 tonnes in April, data from the General Administration of Customs showed.\par \par \uc2 * For the top stories in metals and other news, click or\par \par \uc2 MARKETS NEWS\par \par \uc2 * U.S. stocks ended slightly higher on Friday as investors warmed to data showing U.S. wage growth in April despite weaker-than-expected jobs growth, while the wages data also pushed longer-dated Treasury yields higher.\par \par \uc2 DATA AHEAD (GMT)\par \par \uc2 0600 Germany Industrial orders Mar\par \par \uc2 0830 Euro zone Sentix index May\par \par \uc2 1400 U.S. Employment trends Apr\par \par \uc2 PRICES\par \par \uc2 Three month \uc2 LME\uc2  copper\par \par \uc2 Most active ShFE copper\par \par \uc2 Three month \uc2 LME\uc2  aluminium\par \par \uc2 Most active ShFE aluminium\par \par \uc2 Three month \uc2 LME\uc2  zinc\par \par \uc2 Most active ShFE zinc\par \par \uc2 Three month \uc2 LME\uc2  lead\par \par \uc2 Most active ShFE lead\par \par \uc2 Three month \uc2 LME\uc2  nickel\par \par \uc2 Most active ShFE nickel\par \par \uc2 Three month \uc2 LME\uc2  tin\par \par \uc2 Most active ShFE tin\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 6.4970 Chinese yuan renminbi) (Reporting by Melanie Burton; Editing by Richard Pullin) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-5-9T02:12:59.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160509ec590038p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
