\par\par \b \uc2 IFR Markets ForexWatch Asia Regional Daily Briefing\b0\par\par\uc2 5,100 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 April 2016\par 03:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SYDNEY, April 11 (IFR) - North America news and data\par     * U.S. wholesale inventories fall sharply (-0.5% v Rtrs f/c -0.1%, -0.2%\par pvs), points to weaker growth\par     * Atlanta GDPNow model forecast for real GDP growth in Q1 is 0.1% down from\par 0.4% on April 5\par     * \f28 \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  cuts q1 GDP view to 0.2% from 0.7% after drop in Feb inventories\par     * Fed's Dudley sees risks lingering, cautious on U.S. rate hikes, Risks\par tilted 'slightly' to downside despite U.S. strength (Japan/Europe), Minimum wage\par rises could help inflation goal at margins\par     * Germany's Gabriel says \f28 \uc2 \f2 ECB\f28 \uc2 \f2  rate policy expropriates "little people",\par argues Europe needs a growth program\par     * CA employment rose 40.6 K vs expectations of a 10 K rise\par     * CA unemployment rate fell to 7.1% from 7.3% (unchanged expected)\par \par   News from the weekend\par  \par The week ahead - China data, Inflation data and MOF in focus\par The strong JPY is causing great concern at the MOF, as it is having a severely\par negative impact on the Nikkei and inflation expectations. The Nikkei fell over\par 2% last week and is down close to 7% over the past two weeks. The market views\par the BOJ as running out of options to cap JPY strength - so the MOF may try\par jawboning to help stem the JPY rise. The bar for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is set high; as\par the MOF knows there will be strong international disproval of any attempts to\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par  \par Key \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data will be released next week, which will likely bring China growth\par concerns back into focus. China concerns have shifted to the back burner in\par recent weeks even though it is well know that Fed Chair Yellen sees China growth\par as a key risk for the global economy in coming months.\par  \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2  decide on rates in the week ahead, but\par no-change from wither central banks is expected.\par  \par Central banks \par The recent gains in the oil price and the strong Canada jobs data should ensure\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  stands pat when they meet next week. The key will be the\par statement, growth forecasts and any shift away from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  easing bias. The BOE\par MPC meets, but that should be a non-event, as the BREXIT vote still dominates\par the outlook for the GBP.\par     \par Key data in the week ahead\par China data will be the main focus with China PPI and CPI to be released on\par Monday followed on Wednesday by China trade data. The key data events will be\par Friday when Retail Sales, IP, Urban Investment and Q1 GDP will be released. The\par latest Reuters poll calls for Q1 growth to slip to 6.7% Y/Y from Q3 growth of\par 6.8%.\par  \par US data in the week ahead includes March PPI and Retail Sales on Wednesday\par followed by CPI on Thursday. One of the reasons for the rise in risk aversion\par over the past week was expectations of a weak US corporate earnings season which\par kicks off next week. US Q1 earnings is expected to be the worst earnings season\par since 2009 with expectations of a decline of 7.9% Y/Y. The key data events in\par the Euro zone will be IP on Tuesday followed by EZ inflation data on Wednesday.\par UKI CPI and PPI are the main events for the UK this week. It will be a fairly\par busy week in Australia with the NAB Business survey out on Tuesday and the key\par Aus employment data out on Thursday. The market is looking for Aus jobs to come\par in at plus 20 K while unemployment is expected to tick up to 5.9% from 5.8%.  \par John.Noonan@tr.com\par Best to worst performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week\par CCY   APR01       APR08    %Change\par JPY   111.72      108.08      3.26%\par CHF   0.9590      0.9540      0.52%\par CAD   1.3009      1.2985      0.18%\par EUR   1.1383      1.1402      0.17%\par GBP   1.4233      1.4125      -0.76%\par AUD   0.7669      0.7549      -1.56%\par NZD   0.6910      0.6801      -1.58%\par    \par \par Comment\par The JPY was by far and away the best performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week, as long-term\par JPY shorts hit the exits due to the belief the BOJ has run out of policy options\par to prevent the JPY from strengthening - barring direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The market\par appears to be challenging the BOJ to opt for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with the belief the\par bar for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is still too high in the face of international pressure not\par to do so. Heightened risk aversion and broad JPY strength forced long-term carry\par trade investors to unwind - resulting in the AUD and NZD being the worst\par performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the G10 last week. The NZD and AUD were the best\par performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the previous week, but equity market weakness ahead of\par US earnings season led to heightened risk aversion and weighed on risk\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The AUD also lost ground following a dovish RBA statement. The CAD\par managed to hold its ground against the USD thanks to a 8% rise in \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude\par and strong Canadian jobs data. The EUR was sidelined last week, as the USD has\par stopped trending higher to support the EUR/USD while EUR/JPY selling caps the\par EUR/USD topside. John.Noonan@tr.com\par     \par Themes for Friday's trading\par * The main theme impacting all asset markets on Friday was a 6%-plus rise in\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude after a surprisingly large US stockpile drawdown. The miss in\par expectations was huge as crude stockpiles fell by nearly 5 million barrels last\par week versus analysts forecasts for a rise of 3.2 million barrels, government\par data showed.\par  \par * \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude closed the day 6.6% higher at 39.72 and for the week it gained\par close to 8.0%.\par  \par * The surging oil price supported other risk assets and broadly weighed on the\par USD.\par  \par * Wall Street gave back a chunk of early gains, as worries over US growth and\par expectations of a weak US earnings season encouraged selling at the highs.\par  \par * The Dow was up over 150 points early in the session before closing just 36\par points higher.\par  \par * Very weak US whole sale inventories data portends weak Q1 US GDP with the\par widely followed \f28 \uc2 \f2 Atlanta Fed\f28 \uc2 \f2  GDP tracker now forecasting Q1 real GDP growth at\par just plus 0.1% - that is down from the peak of 2.75% at one stage in Feb.\par  \par * The Dow closed the day 0.20% higher at 17,576.96; the S&P rose 5.69 points or\par 0.28% to 2,047.60 and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NASDAQ\f28 \uc2 \f2  closed just 2.32 points higher at 4,850.69.\par  \par * Despite the modest gains on Friday it was the worst week on Wall Street since\par the end of February. The Dow and S&P fell 1.2% last week while the \f28 \uc2 \f2 NASDAQ\f28 \uc2 \f2  slid\par 1.3%.\par  \par * The huge rise in oil helped support EM sentiment with the iShares MSCI EM\par Equity ETF rising 1.74% and the MSCI LATAM Equity Index soaring 3.22%.\par  \par * Other key commodities were mixed with NY Copper rising 0.58% while Lon Copper\par closed flat on the day. For the week NY Copper fell 3.45% while Lon Copper slid\par 3.83%.   Iron ore closed down close to 1.0% at 53.30 and for the week it was\par 1.30% lower. \par  \par * Gold closed the day flat at 1,240 and for the week it gained 1.47%.\par  \par * It was fairly quiet day FOR us Treasuries, as the lowered US GDP expectations\par capped moves higher in US yields while improved risk appetite underpinned.\par  \par * The 2-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yield closed unchanged at 0.69% while the 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par yield rose 3 BPs to 1.71%. For the week the 2-year yield fell 7 BPs while the\par 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yield fell 8 BPs.\par  \par * The Fed Fund futures are pricing in only a 23% chance the Fed will hike at\par their June meeting.\par  \par * The CAD received double support on Friday was the best performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by\par a long shot,\par  \par * The CAD benefitted from the surge in the oil price while also getting a strong\par lift from surprisingly strong Canadian employment data.\par  \par * Canada added 40.6K new jobs in March while the unemployment rate fell to 7.1%\par from 7.3%. The market was expecting jobs to come in at plus 10K and the\par unemployment to come in unchanged at 7.3%. The market was expect8ng the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of\par Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  meeting next Wednesday to convey a dovish message, but the strong\par employment data may see a change of tune for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par  \par * The USD/CAD fell 1.22% to 1.2985 and the fall may have been greater if it\par weren't for a number of option strikes around 1.3000 to make the price action\par sticky at that level. The CAD rose around 1.0% against the EUR and 1.1% against\par the JPY.\par  \par * The AUD/USD managed to bounce after testing sub-0.7500 where decent buying\par interest awaits. The bounce in oil and modest gains on Wall Street led to\par AUD/USD short covering and a net 0.60% gain on the day to close at 0.7549.\par  \par * The USD/JPY was choppy on Friday, as short-covering due to a modest risk rally\par sent it to 109.10 at one stage. Longer-term funds took advantage of the bounce\par to establish fresh USD/JPY shorts. Wall Street gave back a chunk of earlier\par gains and prompted USD/JPY selling n the US afternoon session. The USD/JPY gave\par back all of the short0 covering gains and closed the day at 108.09 - down 0.10%\par from Thursday's close.\par  \par * The EUR/USD stayed on the sidelines, as was the case for almost all of last\par week. The EUR/USD had an inside-trading day - only managing a 1.1349/1.1419\par range on the day. The EUR/USD closed at 1.1402 - 0.19% higher than Thursday's\par close. The EUR/USD has closed within 15 pips of 1.1390 for seven straight\par trading days.\par     \par Wrap-up\par There wasn't any news of note over the weekend - so it should be a quiet start\par on Monday morning. The strengthening JPY was the major focal point last week and\par will remain in focus in the week ahead. The strong JPY set a negative mood for\par market most days, as it led to selloffs in the Nikkei - which weighed on\par regional markets in Asia and gave poor leads to Europe. The market feels there\par is little Japanese authorities can do about the rising JPY - aside from verbal\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . If the USD/JPY slide towards 105 accelerates it is likely to\par prompt the MOF to clearly threaten \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but the market might see it as\par a bluff and call it.\par  \par The strong rise in the oil price on Friday brings the price of \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude\par closer to the key 200-day MA. The 200-day MA has held attempts higher since the\par price broke below 100 and started trending lower back in mid-2014. The\par descending 200-day MA came in at 41.23 and should fall to around 40.60 by the\par end of next week. A daily close above that reading would suggest the down-trend\par is about complete and a bottom is in place just above 26.00. If oil was to start\par making tracks towards 50 bucks it would have a positive impact in EM assets and\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as well as relieving pressure on the high-yield bond markets.  It\par would help increase risk appetite and add more weight to the US dollar in a\par broad sense.\par  \par China comes back into focus with a very busy calendar of China data that\par concludes with the closely watched Q1 GDP on Friday. China concerns have\par lightened up in recent weeks, as displayed by the increase in China reserves\par last week - suggesting the exodus out of Chinese assets has ceased for the\par time-being. If the data comes in close to expectations it will keep China\par concerns on the sidelines and likely boost EM assets and help to underpin key\par commodity markets. If the data is sluggish it would likely push China concerns\par back to centre-stage and dampen risk appetite. Weak China data could also have\par an impact on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  expectations including those of the US Fed.\par  \par US earnings season kicks off next week with analysts expecting the worst\par quarterly earnings since 2009. If Wall Street reacts negatively to US corporate\par earnings it will set a weak tone for risk assets and support safe-haven\par destinations such as the JPY.\par  \par Sticking to technical analysis for market direction is advisable for the week\par ahead, as there are a number of cross currents that could impact investor\par sentiment in either direction.  John.Noonan@tr.com\par  \par North America News and data\par     * U.S. wholesale inventories fall sharply (-0.5% v Rtrs f/c 