 US yields while improved risk appetite underpinned.\par  \par * The 2-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yield closed unchanged at 0.69% while the 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par yield rose 3 BPs to 1.71%. For the week the 2-year yield fell 7 BPs while the\par 10-year \f28 \uc2 \f2 Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yield fell 8 BPs.\par  \par * The Fed Fund futures are pricing in only a 23% chance the Fed will hike at\par their June meeting.\par  \par * The CAD received double support on Friday was the best performing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by\par a long shot,\par  \par * The CAD benefitted from the surge in the oil price while also getting a strong\par lift from surprisingly strong Canadian employment data.\par  \par * Canada added 40.6K new jobs in March while the unemployment rate fell to 7.1%\par from 7.3%. The market was expecting jobs to come in at plus 10K and the\par unemployment to come in unchanged at 7.3%. The market was expect8ng the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of\par Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2  meeting next Wednesday to convey a dovish message, but the strong\par employment data may see a change of tune for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\par  \par * The USD/CAD fell 1.22% to 1.2985 and the fall may have been greater if it\par weren't for a number of option strikes around 1.3000 to make the price action\par sticky at that level. The CAD rose around 1.0% against the EUR and 1.1% against\par the JPY.\par  \par * The AUD/USD managed to bounce after testing sub-0.7500 where decent buying\par interest awaits. The bounce in oil and modest gains on Wall Street led to\par AUD/USD short covering and a net 0.60% gain on the day to close at 0.7549.\par  \par * The USD/JPY was choppy on Friday, as short-covering due to a modest risk rally\par sent it to 109.10 at one stage. Longer-term funds took advantage of the bounce\par to establish fresh USD/JPY shorts. Wall Street gave back a chunk of earlier\par gains and prompted USD/JPY selling n the US afternoon session. The USD/JPY gave\par back all of the short0 covering gains and closed the day at 108.09 - down 0.10%\par from Thursday's close.\par  \par * The EUR/USD stayed on the sidelines, as was the case for almost all of last\par week. The EUR/USD had an inside-trading day - only managing a 1.1349/1.1419\par range on the day. The EUR/USD closed at 1.1402 - 0.19% higher than Thursday's\par close. The EUR/USD has closed within 15 pips of 1.1390 for seven straight\par trading days.\par  \par Wrap-up\par There wasn't any news of note over the weekend - so it should be a quiet start\par on Monday morning. The strengthening JPY was the major focal point last week and\par will remain in focus in the week ahead. The strong JPY set a negative mood for\par market most days, as it led to selloffs in the Nikkei - which weighed on\par regional markets in Asia and gave poor leads to Europe. The market feels there\par is little Japanese authorities can do about the rising JPY - aside from verbal\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . If the USD/JPY slide towards 105 accelerates it is likely to\par prompt the MOF to clearly threaten \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but the market might see it as\par a bluff and call it.\par  \par The strong rise in the oil price on Friday brings the price of \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude\par closer to the key 200-day MA. The 200-day MA has held attempts higher since the\par price broke below 100 and started trending lower back in mid-2014. The\par descending 200-day MA came in at 41.23 and should fall to around 40.60 by the\par end of next week. A daily close above that reading would suggest the down-trend\par is about complete and a bottom is in place just above 26.00. If oil was to start\par making tracks towards 50 bucks it would have a positive impact in EM assets and\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as well as relieving pressure on the high-yield bond markets.  It\par would help increase risk appetite and add more weight to the US dollar in a\par broad sense.\par  \par China comes back into focus with a very busy calendar of China data that\par concludes with the closely watched Q1 GDP on Friday. China concerns have\par lightened up in recent weeks, as displayed by the increase in China reserves\par last week - suggesting the exodus out of Chinese assets has ceased for the\par time-being. If the data comes in close to expectations it will keep China\par concerns on the sidelines and likely boost EM assets and help to underpin key\par commodity markets. If the data is sluggish it would likely push China concerns\par back to centre-stage and dampen risk appetite. Weak China data could also have\par an impact on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  expectations including those of the US Fed.\par  \par US earnings season kicks off next week with analysts expecting the worst\par quarterly earnings since 2009. If Wall Street reacts negatively to US corporate\par earnings it will set a weak tone for risk assets and support safe-haven\par destinations such as the JPY.\par  \par Sticking to technical analysis for market direction is advisable for the week\par ahead, as there are a number of cross currents that could impact investor\par sentiment in either direction.  John.Noonan@tr.com\par  \par Technical view\par  \par Equities\par S&P\par Trend higher in S&P ran out of steam last week. The 5-day MA has turned lower\par and is about to cross below the 10-day MA to confirm the short-term trend higher\par has run its course. Key support is found at 2,010/2,015 where the 200-day MA and\par the 38.2 fibo of the 1,810/2,075 move converge. Strong resistance has formed\par between 2,075/2,085 and a break above that window is needed to reestablish\par upward momentum. \{Last 2,047.60\}\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASX\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \uc2 \f2 ASX\f28 \uc2 \f2  is trending lower and showing no sign it is ready to turn around.\par Resistance is at the descending 10-day MA (4,995 on Friday) and a break above\par that reading would warn downward momentum is waning. The 61.8 fibo of the\par 4,710/5,216 move comes in at 4,900 and while it edged below that level on\par Wednesday- it is yet to close below. A daily close below 4,900 targets the Feb\par 26 low at 4,838.  (\{Last 4,937.60\}\par Commodities\par  \par Gold\par Before the price action last week - gold was setting up to trend lower. The\par down-trend never developed and it isn't trending in either direction. The close\par above the 20-day MA at 1,233 confirms that gold isn't trending lower. The 50%\par retracement of the 1,282/1.208 move at 1,245 has been solid resistance and the\par 61.8 of that move comes in at 1,253. Support is found at the ascending 50-day MA\par which came in at 1,224 on Friday. \{Last 1,240\}\par Iron Ore\par Iron ore has started a short-term trend lower, but it is at an early stage and\par not yet convincing. Key support is found at the descending 200-day MA (50.00 on\par Friday) and a break below that reading would likely see the trend lower\par accelerate. A break below the descending 20-day MA at 54.75 would negate the\par trend lower and suggest more consolidation in he short-term. \{Last 53.30\}\par Lon Copper \par London copper has started trending lower - with the 5, 10 and 20-day moving\par averages aligned in a bearish formation and all pointing lower. The 61.8 of the\par 4,318/5,131 move comes in around 5,630 and it has tested that level on both\par Thursday and Friday. A break and close below 4,630 targets the Feb 10 low at\par 4,430. A move back above the descending 10-day MA (4,801 on Friday) would ease\par the downward pressure and warn the trend lower was losing momentum. \{Last 4,650\}\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude \par Crude isn't trending in either direction according to the short-date moving\par averages, as price action is very choppy. Major resistance is found at the\par 200-day MA (41.23 on Friday) and a break above that reading would strongly\par suggest the long-term trend lower is over and a bottom is in place. The 200-day\par MA has been approached three times since \f28 \uc2 \f2 NYMEX\f28 \uc2 \f2  Crude moved below 100 and it was\par rejected each time. Support has formed around 35.25 and a break below that level\par will likely see the short-term trend lower resume. \{Last 39.72\}\par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par EUR/USD  The 5, 10 and 20-day moving averages are aligned in a bullish formation\par and are pointing higher. This confirms a short-trend higher is in place, but\par there isn't a lot of upward momentum. A close below the ascending 10-day MA\par 1.1354 on Friday) would warn upward momentum is waning. Key resistance is found\par at the Oct 15, 2015 high at 1.1495.    \{Last 1.1402\}\par  \par USD/JPY \par The USD/JPY is trending lower and the trend is accelerating. The USD/JPY has\par made a lower daily high for eight straight days - so a move above Friday's\par 109.10 would warn that downward momentum is starting to wane. Only a break above\par the sharply descending 10-day MA (111.06 on Friday) would suggest the trend\par lower was mature. Key fibo support is found around 106.50. This level is the\par 38.2 of the 75.30/125.86 move. A break below that level should see a test/break\par below the psychological support at 105.00. \{Last 108.08\}\par  \par AUD/USD \par The AUD/USD stopped trending higher last week, as the 5-day MA crossed below\par both the 10-day and 20-day moving averages. Support is found at the Feb 24 low\par at 0.7477 and a break below that level opens the way for a test of the 38.2 fibo\par of the 0.6827/0.7723 move at 0.7380. A break back above a double top at 0.7640\par would likely restore upward momentum.  \{Last 0.7549\}  John.noonan@tr.com\par \f28 \par \par \p