\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-China's deep pockets give it edge in yuan fight\b0\par\par\uc2 591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2016\par 06:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are her own.)\par \par \uc2 By Rachel Morarjee\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, April 8 (Reuters Breakingviews) - \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained the upper hand in its fight with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators. The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves nudged up in March as fears that the renminbi would slide receded. Though the fight is far from over, China's deep pockets give it an edge.\par \par \uc2 Sentiment is all-important in the struggle over the direction of the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Repeated promises by the country's leaders that the yuan would remain broadly stable - combined with aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a crackdown on loopholes used to take cash out of the country - have slowed outflows. In March, reported \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves actually rose by $10.3 billion to $3.21 trillion - the first increase since last autumn.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  has helped. The American \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to tone down future interest rate increases has weakened the dollar. The yuan is now worth slightly more against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than at the beginning of the year, before the latest devaluation scare started. The shift also boosts the reported value of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, because assets denominated in yen and euros are now worth more in dollar terms.\par \par \uc2 The battle is not over yet, though. Any pickup in the U.S. economy could prompt the Fed to change course, pressuring the yuan again. Moreover, many of China's economic fundamentals remain gloomy. Growth is slowing and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is responding by pumping money into the system. The tide of cash will push against the dam of China's increasingly tight \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls.\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  has probably also carried out some maneouvres in the derivatives markets. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on March 31 disclosed its derivative positions for the first time. Though its reported nominal short U.S. position of $28.9 billion is lower than economists and investors had expected, this does not include positions held by state-owned banks.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, the \uc2 PBOC\uc2  can keep up this struggle longer than most investors can maintain a short position. Authorities can use the country's banking system and numerous \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls to wage war on speculators. Though China may eventually be forced to devalue the yuan, there's little solace for hedge funds in being right at the wrong time.\par \par \uc2 On \uc2 Twitter{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/morarjee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/morarjee}}}\par \par \uc2 CONTEXT NEWS\par \par \uc2 - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves defied market expectations and rose in March, in the first monthly gain since November, as cooling expectations of U.S. interest rate hikes eased pressure on the yuan.\par \par \uc2 - Chinese authorities have taken a raft of measures to curb outflows sparked by concerns over the slowing economy and U.S. interest rate rises, and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to support the yuan.\par \par \uc2 - \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves - the world's largest - climbed $10.26 billion in March to $3.21 trillion, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on April 7. The reserves beat a Reuters poll forecast of a drop to $3.18 trillion from $3.20 trillion in February, but are still down sharply from a peak of $3.99 trillion in June 2014.\par \par \uc2 - Reuters: China's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves post surprise rise in March on more stable yuan\par \par \uc2 RELATED COLUMNS\par \par \uc2 Stop and flow\par \par \uc2 - For previous columns by the author, Reuters customers can click on\par \par \uc2 (Editing by Peter Thal Larsen and Katrina Hamlin)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-4-11T04:31:59.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160408ec48006hd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
