\par\par \b \uc2 Markets happy to play, if not believe, idea of G20 dollar accord\b0\par\par\uc2 886 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2016\par 16:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LONDON, April 8 (Reuters) - Even if there was no secret deal to weaken the dollar and stabilise world markets at a meeting of \uc2 Group of 20\uc2  finance chiefs in Shanghai six weeks ago, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets seem happy to play as if there was.\par \par \uc2 Few if any major investment houses believe - as conspiracy theorists and speculative writers have posited - that there was a formal accord, agreed quietly by U.S. and other \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs on Feb. 27.\par \par \uc2 Reuters reporting around and since the meeting backs that up.\par \par \uc2 But as the same players prepare to reconvene in the United States next week, many investors see the policy leanings and price moves of the past month as the logical outcome of discussions in Shanghai on a traumatic start to 2016.\par \par \uc2 In the weeks since, the three big headaches of the past year - China's yuan, falling oil prices and the threat of a stronger dollar and higher U.S. interest rates - have eased.\par \par \uc2 Fed chief \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  promised caution last week on any further rises in rates, and the resulting sell-off has left the dollar down 5 percent on the year against the euro and 10 percent against the yen.\par \par \uc2 In response, emerging markets have steadied, stocks and oil are not falling as fast and China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, the cause of a huge global sell-off in early January, are rising again.\par \par \uc2 It looks almost as if someone planned it.\par \par \uc2 "I absolutely do not think there was an agreed accord," says Simon Derrick, Head of Global Market Research at \uc2 Bank of New York Mellon\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 "But as a tactical play all of this makes perfect sense. The U.S. had a huge interest in stabilising China. They have done so and if there is collateral damage in the hit the Europeans and the Japanese have taken (in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation), they can deal with that later."\par \par \uc2 Equally, say others, there seems to be greater consensus among policymakers on what they can and cannot do within an increasingly complicated international financial system.\par \par \uc2 "These recent moves are consistent with a rising understanding of international spill-overs and spill-backs, as well as with a new assessment of which policies are more akin to beggar-thy-neighbour policies than others," says Martin Enlund, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Swedish bank \uc2 Nordea\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 As \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  faced a flurry of attacks on the yuan before the Shanghai meetings, some analysts raised the prospect of a grand public bargain in the style of the 1980s Plaza Accord to weaken the dollar.\par \par \uc2 In the event the only official message was familiar: competitive devaluations are bad and we will refrain from them - a commitment that hasn't stopped central banks running ultra-loose policies that weaken \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 2008.\par \par \uc2 "It's unthinkable (there was a deal on the dollar)," said one senior G7 official who attended the meeting.\par \par \uc2 China's vice finance minister last month also denied the existence of a secret agreement with Washington.\par \par \uc2 "The more conspiracy oriented sides of this are fairly unlikely," former U.S. Treasury chief \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  told an event in Washington this week. "It is just not the way that they (central banks) work."\par \par \uc2 Yet even if no such deal was done, events since have largely played in Beijing and Washington's favour.\par \par \uc2 By weakening the dollar and calming global markets, the Fed hopes to lay the ground for it to raise rates. It also avoids another hit to U.S. companies from a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which a year ago had gained a quarter in value in just over six months.\par \par \uc2 In turn, pressure on the Chinese yuan's de facto dollar peg has eased.\par \par \uc2 The big question mark, then, is Europe and Japan.\par \par \uc2 By printing money and slashing interest rates into negative territory, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hoped to get prices and exports rising and deflect the threat of deflation. That should founder if the euro and yen gain.\par \par \uc2 "This idea that we've had a hiatus in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action probably won't stand the test of what's going on in Japan and Europe," said Michael Metcalfe, head of macro strategy at \uc2 State Street Global Markets\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But if, as he and others argue, the BOJ and \uc2 ECB\uc2  are both likely to act again, why are the yen and euro so strong?\par \par \uc2 For ECB chief \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2 , it may be that markets no longer believe he can weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through policy.\par \par \uc2 Many say Japan, by far the most pro-active on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets historically, will not be so shy. But they also say Tokyo will find it hard to do so in the midst of G20 and G7 gatherings over the next month.\par \par \uc2 "My guess is the Europeans and the Japanese may not want to participate in this fight unless we see further moves," said Athanasios Vamvakidis, Head G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategy for \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  in Europe.\par \par \uc2 "But that may change if we see dollar-yen at 100 and the euro above $1.15." (Additional reporting by Leika Kihara in TOKYO, David Chance in WASHINGTON, Jamie McGeever, Anirban Nag and Mike Dolan in LONDON; Editing by Toby Chopra) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-4-8T15:54:19.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160408ec480129u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
