\par\par \b \uc2 China's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Victory Hides Scars of War -- Heard on the Street\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anjani Trivedi   \par \uc2 417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2016\par 10:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Beijing may want to declare "mission accomplished" in its war over the yuan. But the tactics it used may obfuscate the price it paid to get there.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose $10 billion in March to $3.22 trillion, breaking a disconcerting four-month depletion streak. Part of that can be chalked up to valuation effects. A rise in the euro rose made China's holdings in that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  look larger when reported in dollars. Still, the numbers seem to show that--for now--Beijing no longer has to spend heavily to keep the yuan steady.\par \par \uc2 A closer look, though, raises questions about how Beijing has managed to glaze over worries about outflows. It had long been extremely difficult to quantify Beijing's maneuvers in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The best estimates came from stringing together disparate sets of data related to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including reserves figures.\par \par \uc2 Since December, however, at the behest of the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , China has been reporting its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards positions--in theory, a crucial piece to the multibillion-dollar puzzle that is China's yuan management. Many expected to see evidence of massive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yet the net U.S.-dollar short position in February, the latest data available, was only around $26 billion, a quarter of what some economists had penciled in. So where did the rest go? The data show most of the forwards were for a year's duration, so they couldn't have all matured.\par \par \uc2 One possibility is that this figure reflects only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's restricted onshore market. But that doesn't square with the onshore forwards market's daily trading and volume data, which were much lower than the disclosed forwards contracts.\par \par \uc2 That leaves another place where the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have happened: the balance sheets of state-owned banks. In the past these banks have been conduits for spot-market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , both onshore and in the freer offshore market. The risk is, such exposure may not be showing up in the reserves data, and could surprise investors down the road should the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  be forced to absorb those positions.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately for investors, this relatively new data raises more questions than it answers. The more circuitous the routes to propping up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the less conviction investors may have in its stability.\par \par \uc2 Write to Anjani Trivedi at anjani.trivedi@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 08-04-16 0809GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020160408ec480006r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
