\par\par \b \uc2 China's yuan firms, state banks seen smoothening volatility\b0\par\par\uc2 490 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2016\par 06:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, April 7 (Reuters) - China's yuan firmed against\par the dollar on Thursday, after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set a stronger\par guidance rate and traders suspected state-owned banks \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par to hold down volatility.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set the midpoint rate \par at 6.4707 per dollar prior to market open, 0.07 percent firmer\par than the previous fix of 6.4754. \par     In the spot market, the yuan opened at 6.4727 per\par dollar and was changing hands at 6.4714 by midday in range-bound\par trade, strengthening 0.16 percent from the previous late session\par close.\par     Traders believed strong demand for dollars was in part\par tempered by the presence of state-owned banks.\par     "There was strong dollar demand in early morning trade,"\par said a trader at a Chinese commercial bank in Shanghai. "But\par state banks were offering prices around 6.4760, which limited\par movement in the spot rate."\par     Traders said the yuan still faces long-term depreciation\par pressure against the dollar given varying economic fundamentals\par in the United States and China, but the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's\par short-term volatility will hinge on how the dollar index \par moves.\par     The dollar index, which tracks the greenback against a\par basket of major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , stood at 94.434 around midday on\par Thursday in Asian trade, after plumbing to 94.239 in the\par previous session, its lowest since October last year.\par     Minutes from the Fed's March 15-16 policy meeting suggested\par that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  appears unlikely to raise interest rates\par before June due to widespread concern among policymakers over\par their limited ability to counter the blow of a global economic\par slowdown. \par     On Wednesday, a Reuters poll showed that China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves - the world's largest - likely fell for a\par fifth straight month in March, easing to $3.18 trillion from\par $3.2 trillion in February. The data may be released on Thursday.\par  \par     The offshore yuan was trading 0.18 percent softer\par than the onshore spot at 6.4829 per dollar.\par         \par     The yuan market at a glance: \par     \par     ONSHORE:\par  Item               Current  Previous  Change\par  PBOC midpoint      6.4707   6.4754    0.07%\par                                        \par  Spot yuan          6.4714   6.4819    0.16%\par                                        \par  Divergence from    0.01%              \par  midpoint*                             \par  Spot change YTD                       0.34%\par  Spot change since 2005                27.89%\par  revaluation                           \par  \par *Divergence of the dollar/yuan exchange rate. Negative number\par indicates that spot yuan is trading stronger than the midpoint.\par \f28 \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China (PBOC) \f28 \uc2 \f2 allows the exchange rate to\par rise or fall 2 percent from official midpoint rate it sets each\par morning.\par \par     OFFSHORE CNH MARKET   \par   \par  Instrument            Current   Difference\par                                  from onshore\par  Offshore spot yuan    6.4829    -0.18%\par         *                        \par  Offshore              6.6733    -3.04%\par  non-deliverable                 \par  forwards           