\par\par \b \uc2 HK Bourse: Results Announcement From \uc2 Guangzhou R&F Properties Co. Ltd\uc2 .\b0\par\par\uc2 13,479 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2016\par 05:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     For full details, please click on the following link: \par \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2016/0311/LTN20160311138.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2016/0311/LTN20160311138.pdf}}}\uc2 \f2  \par  \par  \par  \par                            2015 ANNUAL RESULTS ANNOUNCEMENT \par  \par                 Turnover up 28% to RMB44.3 billion \par                 Profit for the year rises to RMB6.7 billion \par                 Revenue from sales of property up 29% to RMB40.7 billion \par                 Gross profit margin and net profit margin from sales of property \par                  remained steady at 34% and 15%, respectively \par                 Cash and cash equivalents as at 31 December 2015 amounted to \par                  RMB21.3 billion \par                 Proposed final dividend of RMB0.90 per share -- full year dividend \par                  payout ratio of 68% \par  \par  \par The board of directors (the "Board") of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guangzhou R&F Properties Co., Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (the "Company" or \par "R&F") are pleased to announce the consolidated results of the Company and its subsidiaries (the \par "Group") for the year ended 31 December 2015. The annual results have been reviewed by the audit \par committee of the Company. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                             -1- \par  \par CHAIRMAN'S STATEMENT \par  \par Within a transformational period which saw a slowdown in China's economic growth, in 2015 the \par Group engaged in a strategic shift aimed at achieving long-term sustainability. This involved placing \par emphases on long-term profitability and lowering the cost of funding, two important factors in the \par Group's performance for the year. As it became apparent that the recovery of China's property \par market was likely to be gradual, during the year we revised our contracted sales target for 2015 (from \par RMB60 billion) to achieve contract sales of RMB54.4 billion. Whilst contracted sales were flat overall \par as compared to 2014, the Group's profitability increased against that year. The recovery in China's \par property market has been gradual, with the strongest recovery coming in core first- and second-tier \par cities in which the Group generates a significant proportion of its sales. Despite an expected slowdown \par in China's economic growth, unforeseen fluctuations in China's domestic A-share market during the \par year led to some volatility across many areas. These fluctuations, which prompted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Central Government on multiple occasions, may have had an indirect effect on the property \par market and on investment sentiment towards China. This period of heightened volatility has prompted \par the China Central Government to accelerate certain financial reforms and introduce alternate forms \par of liquidity for corporates; these have included reopening A-share IPO applications and accelerating \par the approval process for the issuance of domestic corporate bonds. These reforms have opened up \par new financing opportunities and have been a positive to allow the Group to shift focus to longer \par term goals. The following paragraphs highlight recent market trends and financial reforms that have \par had an effect on our Group's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the year. \par  \par Growth in sales volumes against economic weakness \par  \par In 2015, China's economy recorded Gross Domestic Product ("GDP") growth of 6.9%. This \par represented its lowest growth rate in the last 10 years, as the China Central Government continued \par to focus on making structural reforms to boost the long-term sustainability of the economy. Against \par this backdrop of lower economic growth was a concurrent progressive liberalization of the RMB \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which gradually placed pressure on the value of the RMB \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  -- particularly in the \par second half of the year. That pressure had accelerated further at the beginning of 2016. Under a \par slower economic backdrop in 2015, our listed peers experienced considerable growth in contracted \par sales in terms of gross floor area ("GFA") sold. This reflected factors such as an increasing stability \par in the underlying property market, reasonable average selling prices, and active destocking of built- \par up inventory from 2014. \par  \par Another notable trend in contracted sales in 2015 was a shift in demand to higher-tier cities. \par Fundamental drivers such as demographics, business activity, GDP per capita and existing \par infrastructure all helped create a preference for property in higher- rather than lower-tier cities for \par long-term living and investment. This demand, coupled with the scarcity of new land available for \par acquisition at historical price levels, has meant that developers like ourselves who hold a high \par concentration of land in first- and second-tier cities enjoy a slight advantage if economic conditions \par become less favourable. The greater demand for property in higher-tier cities has been a factor in \par the decision by management to term out resources for sale over subsequent years that will enable us \par to moderate the pace at which our property resources in prime locations are depleted, and allow us \par time to replenish our land bank appropriately. \par  \par Larger financing pools at lower interest rates \par  \par Historically, the Group has primarily relied on debt financing and various forms of borrowing for \par the liquidity it has needed to facilitate property development, capture investment opportunities, and \par fulfil its strategic objectives each year. In recent years, an offshore high yield market and large pools \par of high cost short-term onshore financing products (such as trust loans and perpetual capital \par  \par                                                   -2- \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10 Mar 2016 23:15 ET \par HK Bourse: Results Announcement From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guangzhou R&F Properties Co. Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par securities) have become available, and provided the primary source of capital for significant \par acquisitions made by the Group in 2013 and 2014. As a result, our financial gearing has deteriorated \par significantly, from a net gearing of 85% for the financial year ending 2012 to 124% for the financial \par year ending 2015. We do believe that the investments made during this period will provide valuable \par contributions in the future, but in the near term this borrowing has had the effect of increasing \par certain leverage metrics and adding to the financial burden of the Group. \par  \par However, the weakening economy has prompted the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  to cut its one-year \par lending rate on six occasions, beginning in late 2014. The rate had fallen from 5.60% at the end of \par 2014 to 4.35% by the end of 2015. In addition, the reserve requirement ratio for Chinese banks and \par large financial institutions was lowered from 20% to 17%, with the aim of mitigating some of the \par financial risks and burdens that corporates and local governments have faced due to a slowing Chinese \par economy. Another key stimulus that has been directly beneficial to property developers was the \par opening up of the onshore corporate bond market. An amended promulgation issued in January 2015 \par significantly simplified the approval requisites and accelerated the issuance process, enabling \par developers to issue onshore corporate bonds at historically low interest levels. Subsequently in 2015, \par an estimated RMB22.3 trillion worth of domestic corporate bonds were issued domestically in China, \par up 87.5% from 2014. The Group took advantage of the depth of the issuance market in 2015 to issue \par RMB6.5 billion at an interest rate of 4.95%, and a further RMB9.6 billion early 2016 (January) at \par an interest rate of 3.95%. A further unused quota of RMB2.9 billion has been approved and can be \par issued when the timing is appropriate. \par  \par The monetary policy stimuli described above, together with the opening of the domestic bond market, \par has provided and is continuing to provide the Group with significant additional sources of funding \par at interest rates that compare favourably with our previous high funding costs. This additional source \par of funding enhances our financial flexibility and liquidity, while allowing the Group to reduce its \par existing high interest burden and to term out borrowings that are nearing maturity. \par  \par Business highlights of 2015 \par  \par In 2015, the Group's contracted sales were largely flat by comparison with its contracted sales for \par 2014. The 2015 contracted sales amounted to RMB54.4 billion, a figure that met the revised sales \par target set mid-year. This figure was scaled down from the original figure of RMB60 billion once it \par became clear that the recovery of the property market was slower than expected, with the Group \par deciding to shift its focus to maintaining long-term profitability as against simply achieving year-on- \par year sales growth. Contracted sales, in terms of GFA sold in square metres, increased slightly by 2% \par to 4.11 million sq.m. Turnover (for the accounting income statement) increased significantly by 27.6% \par over 2014. In terms of GFA construction, there was a reduction in new starts compared with the \par preceding year, as resources were refocused on achieving a higher completion schedule. As a result, \par 3.51 million sq.m. of GFA was sold and delivered in 2015, an increase of 11% over 2014. \par  \par Despite 2015 contracted sales being flat, revenue and absolute gross profit rose by comparison with \par 2014, indicating that the Group was meeting its goal of maximizing profitability with its available \par resources. In 2015, we launched sales in our second overseas project in Brisbane, Australia; the result \par was relatively successful and, equally important, has deepened our overseas execution experience. \par Overall revenue was significantly higher on the basis of a larger scale of completions that were sold, \par but even taking into consideration the larger revenue base, the gross profit and net profit margin \par remained robust at 32.1% and 15.2% respectively. The Group's ability to maintain consistently higher \par margins when compared to the overall sector was especially significant in the context of declining \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                  -3- \par  \par margins in recent years, and the impact of such external factors such as the macroeconomic slowdown \par and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange losses. This demonstrates the Group's ability to manage external factors and \par navigate challenging conditions to maintain a positive profit trend. \par  \par As in 2014, the Group continued to be selective about land acquisitions, as conserving capital was \par a key strategy going into 2015. Acquisitions made in the previous two years had adequately replenished \par the Group's land bank in line with our sales growth targets, and we consider that our current land \par is of sufficiently high quality to meet the needs of the next few years. Any further acquisitions will \par involve strategically replenishing our supplies in key areas. Land bank acquisitions in China amounted \par to GFA of 3.6 million sq.m., bringing the Group's total land bank to 39.8 million sq.m. of saleable \par area. In addition to existing land owned in 27 cities and areas, land was added in 2 new cities on a \par small scale in order to broaden our city exposure and explore new markets at a controlled pace. \par However, over 70% of GFA acquired was in cities and areas where we have an existing presence, \par and our intention is to stay firmly focused on those cities in which we have extensive market \par knowledge and experience. \par  \par In 2015, the Group opened a number of small hotels including Pullman Taiyuan R&F Hotel, \par Doubletree Resort by Hilton, Haikou-Chengmai and Holiday Inn Guangzhou Airport Zone. More \par notably, in December, the Group officially opened the Park Hyatt, Guangzhou, a five-star luxury \par hotel with 208 generously sized contemporary guestrooms (including 36 suites) that offers world-class \par service. The hotel is situated in the heart of Pearl River New Town's central business district ("CBD") \par and reflects the unparalleled elegance of the Park Hyatt brand. Adding to the city's growing skyline, \par the Park Hyatt, Guangzhou is situated above the 53rd floor of the R&F Yingkai Plaza, soaring above \par the CBD and offering breathtaking views across the city. The Group now has a number of significant \par landmark grade A office and 5-star hotel developments (under the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ritz-Carlton\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Grand Hyatt and \par Park Hyatt brands) situated in the prime central district of the Pearl River New Town area, which \par between them are reinforcing our Group's reputation as a top tier developer. \par  \par Apart from hotels, the Group also added to its list of investment properties an additional shopping \par mall in the City Centre of Guangzhou, R&F Haizhu City. We soft-launched the first Guangzhou \par shopping mall R&F Haizhu City in October 2015. It is located at Jiangnanxi shopping rim which is \par the most developed and mature shopping area in Haizhu District. Total GFA of the shopping mall \par is 50,000 sq.m. with a MTR station No. 2 access underground. \par  \par On the financing front, 2015 was an active year for the Group both in terms of repayments and \par refinancing. Heading into 2015, the Group had substantial short-term liabilities which were either up \par for renewal or due for repayment. With local interest rates declining as the year proceeded and the \par onshore domestic bond market opening up to privately owned companies, the Group was able to \par actively manage its financing needs by issuing in July RMB6.5 billion of domestic bonds at a 4.95% \par coupon. Furthermore, despite a tightening trust financing market, the Group continued its strategic \par partnership with Ping An Asset Management by extending RMB1.6 billion of the RMB3.2 billion \par trust financing due in August. With a disciplined land bank acquisition strategy and strong cost \par controls in place, the Group was able to allocate excess funds to retire RMB7.6 billion of perpetual \par capital securities. As a result, the Group's overall effective cost of borrowings was reduced to 7.83% \par for 2015, as against 8.22% in 2014. \par  \par Whilst 2015 was a year dominated by debt financing activity across the sector, the Group achieved \par a significant milestone on the equity front. In June, it submitted an A-share application to the China \par Securities Regulation Committee ("CSRC") for a planned initial public offering of A-share to be \par listed on the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Shanghai Stock Exchange\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The submission marks our second attempt in the last five \par years to seek a listing in China; the previous application was discontinued in 2013. Whilst the volatility \par  \par  \par                                                   -4- \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10 Mar 2016 23:16 ET \par HK Bourse: Results Announcement From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guangzhou R&F Properties Co. Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . -3-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par in China has created some uncertainty about the timing for completion, the Group remains optimistic \par given numerous statements issued by the CSRC indicating its support for A-share reform. In \par November 2014, the CSRC announced that it was removing the ban on A-share offerings; and in \par January 2015, it further stipulated that the process of offering shares and bonds for real estate \par developers will not be precluded from onshore fundraising. If and when successful, our A-share \par offering will provide the Group with significant funds for refinancing existing debt and investing in \par future projects. An A-share listing would also have the added effect of adjusting the financial profile \par of the Group by enlarging the proportion of its long-term equity capital, representing a move away \par from its historical reliance on debt financing. \par  \par The outlook for 2016 \par  \par With uncertainties in the overall economy persisting in 2016 and the real estate sector continuing to \par play a significant role in direct investment, financing conditions are expected to remain favourable \par to companies in order to help them ease into the 'new normal'. It is also anticipated that certain \par austerity measures targeted at the sector may be loosened or relaxed in ways that could stimulate \par end-user investment and achieve long-term stability. In February 2016, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 China \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par announced reductions in the minimum down-payment in non-home purchase restriction cities for first \par home buyers to 20%, and for second home buyers to 30%. After significant domestic fundraising \par activity undertaken by the sector at historically low rates, and further policy easing, developers are \par now able to afford to participate more actively in the land market. This is enabling them to plan \par strategically for the longer term, although they still need to be cautious of the risk of over-investing \par as the economy adjusts from a high growth rate to a more moderate one. \par  \par Inventory levels and stock clearance will continue to remain a focus in 2016 as developers look to \par monetize inventory as a means of unlocking cash flow for reinvestment. Inventory destocking is \par beneficial in two main ways; first as a means of generating immediate cash flow, and second as a \par way of achieving same-year recognition of earnings in which no back end completion time is required. \par With a lower level of new starts in 2015, the Group's existing inventory will provide us with additional \par resources for meeting our 2016 contracted sales target. \par  \par Plans and projects for 2016 \par  \par The Group has delivered robust contract sales and profitability in recent financial periods, while \par maintaining an emphasis on cash conservation. However, with sufficient financing and a stronger \par bank balance, in 2016 the Group will look to continue maintaining profitability while also strategically \par replenishing its land bank for long-term sustainability. Our lower financing cost structure should \par offset margin declines that may arise from factors such as rising land prices versus stagnant selling \par prices, pricing pressure from inventory destocking, higher construction costs, and macroeconomic \par factors such as depreciation of the RMB. As a result, the Group has set its contracted sales target \par for 2016 at RMB60 billion, a modest 10% increase over the actual sales achieved in 2015, and a \par figure which takes into account both the outlook for the sector as a whole and the quality of our \par saleable resources. \par  \par Having a strong land bank and strategic footholds in first- and second-tier cities gives the Group a \par strategic advantage when it comes to weathering difficult markets, and also places us in a good \par position to take advantage of any significant sector recovery. In addition to looking at acquiring \par land predominantly in first- and second-tier cities, we will also focus on quick asset turn projects in \par which the time from acquisition to sale and then delivery is shorter. This is a good way of \par supplementing the slower turn of first- and second-tier city projects, which can be harder to replicate \par and replenish given scarcity of land and higher costs. In 2015, the Group's strategy in Taiyuan fulfilled \par  \par  \par                                                   -5- \par  \par a number of important criteria; it is a second-tier city with strong rigid demand, relatively low cost \par land, reasonable margins and scope for quick asset turn. In 2016, Taiyuan and other projects of a \par similar profile will form part of the Group's land acquisition strategy for achieving a good balance \par between cash flow and profitability. \par  \par As speculative investment is harder to foresee and the China Central Government's focus is on \par affordable rigid demand, the Group's projects will continue to be weighted towards residential projects \par targeted at the mass market, where rigid demand and upgrade demand are in line with government \par policies targeting long-term sustainability in the sector. Now that the one-child policy has been \par abolished, population growth and the needs of ordinary family households will be a factor in driving \par end user demand in first- and second-tier cities. In addition, the Group will continue to leverage its \par past successes in developing commercial projects and seek further investment opportunities in prime \par CBD locations and higher-tier cities for sale and long-term investment return. \par  \par In 2016, management will remain focused on achieving sustainable long-term profitability by \par implementing a balanced expansion plan involving higher sell-through rates and careful management \par of overall costs. After emerging from a period of tight liquidity and a high cost funding environment, \par management intends to remain disciplined on expenditure and continue to term out or lower the \par amount of its high cost debt over time. This should further improve cash flow and reduce the impact \par on margins. Overall, with improving market conditions and the further relaxation of austerity \par measures, we are confident of meeting our targets. Lastly, a successful A-share listing will definitely \par be a significant factor in determining the direction of the Group going forward. \par  \par Acknowledgements \par  \par We are coming to the end of a transformational period that has involved significant corporate \par adjustments against a backdrop of major economic and sector challenges. Management continues to \par appreciate the loyalty shown by our shareholders and other investors throughout this period, and \par their support as we have developed and implemented new longer-term strategies and directions. \par Achieving our contracted sales is key to achieving our overall strategy, so on behalf of the Group I \par would like to thank our customers for their support in investing in our products, and placing \par confidence in our market expertise. Within the Group, I would like to express my sincere thanks to \par the directors, whose experience and oversight have provided important guidance. Finally, I wish to \par extend my sincere appreciation to our thousands of staff members, whose dedication and commitment \par have been vital in communicating our core values to our shareholders, customers and investors. \par Without the support of all these groups, management could not have confidently overcome the \par challenges and developed the forward-looking strategies designed to continue the successes of the \par past into the years to come. \par  \par  \par  \par  \par                                                  -6- \par  \par  \par  \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10 Mar 2016 23:16 ET \par HK Bourse: Results Announcement From \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guangzhou R&F Properties Co. Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . -4-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CONSOLIDATED INCOME STATEMENT \par (All amounts in RMB Yuan thousands unless otherwise stated) \par                                                                          Year ended 31 December \par                                                                                 2015        2014 \par                                                                   Note \par  \par                                                                           44,290,924 \par Revenue                                                            2                     34,705,410 \par                                                                          (30,083,853) \par Cost of sales                                                      4                    (22,391,431) \par  \par Gross profit                                                              14,207,071    12,313,979 \par  \par                                                                            1,518,092 \par Other income and other gains -- net                                 3                      2,030,304 \par                                                                             (896,657) \par Selling and marketing costs                                        4                       (896,059) \par                                                                           (2,409,572) \par Administrative expenses                                            4                     (2,220,501) \par  \par Operating profit                                                          12,418,93