\par\par \b \uc2 BREAKINGVIEWS-China hasn't yet won fight against  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  flight\b0\par\par\uc2 579 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2016\par 12:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (The author is a Reuters Breakingviews columnist. The opinions expressed are his own.)\par \par \uc2 By Peter Thal Larsen\par \par \uc2 HONG KONG, March 7 (Reuters Breakingviews) - China has clawed back some ground in the battle to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. The country's pile of dollars and other international \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  got only slightly smaller in February, shrinking by less than $30 billion to a still-sizeable $3.2 trillion, after two months of much bigger outflows. That suggests efforts to talk up the yuan and prevent capital from leaving the People's Republic are having some effect.\par \par \uc2 Losing nearly 1 percent of its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  hoard looks like a relatively good result compared with 3 percent declines in December and January. Several external factors helped: the U.S. dollar weakened slightly against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in February, lifting the reported value of China's non-dollar holdings. The Chinese New Year holiday also interfered with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculation.\par \par \uc2 It's important not to extrapolate from one month of data: in 2015 China's reported reserves rose in the months of April and October yet still ended the year $513 billion smaller. And the scale of the decline matters less than what is causing it. A recent analysis by the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2  points to overseas depositors shifting out of renminbi and into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . This accounted for about half the $175 billion decline in banks' cross-border exposures to China in the third quarter of last year, the BIS reckons. Chinese companies repaying foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt explains another chunk. These factors - which both drain Chinese reserves - should ease as the yuan stabilises.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that fundamental factors still point to more devaluation. China's slowdown contrasts with the improving U.S. economy. The ruling Communist party's promise to boost GDP by 6.5 percent a year until 2020 requires it to pump in even more credit, which means looser monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 China's reserves are still substantial: at most, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably needs $2.5 trillion to insulate the economy against external shocks, according to a Breakingviews analysis based on \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  guidelines. But large outflows can quickly become self-fulfilling. China hasn't yet won the fight against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flight.\par \par \uc2 CONTEXT NEWS\par \par \uc2 - China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell $28.57 billion in February, slightly less than expected and easing from a sharp slump in January, suggesting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is scaling back its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan as capital outflows ease.\par \par \uc2 - Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign reserves declined for a fourth straight month, and the $3.2 trillion at the end of February was the lowest level since December 2011, data from the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  showed on March 7.\par \par \uc2 - Economists polled by Reuters had predicted reserves would fall $30 billion from $3.2 trillion at the end of January.\par \par \uc2 - They had sunk $99.5 billion in January and $107.9 billion in December, which was the biggest monthly drop on record.\par \par \uc2 - China's reserves fell $513 billion in 2015, the largest annual drop in history.\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 PBOC\uc2  announcement {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://bit.ly/1pajTWv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://bit.ly/1pajTWv}}}\par \par \uc2 - Reuters: China Feb \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fall to $3.20 trln, lowest since late 2011\par \par \uc2 RELATED COLUMNS\par \par \uc2 Off target\par \par \uc2 Taking flight\par \par \uc2 - For previous columns by the author, Reuters customers can click on\par \par \uc2 (On \uc2 Twitter{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/peter_tl"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://twitter.com/peter_tl}}}\uc2  Editing by John Foley and \uc2 Sarah Bailey\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-3-10T11:17:57.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160307ec3700x99\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
