\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian shares head for best week in 5 months, US job data eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 840 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2016\par 07:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Asia ex-Japan shares, Nikkei have solid weekly gains               \par \par \uc2 * European markets poised for positive open               \par \par \uc2 * Emerging markets recovering, led by Brazil               \par \par \uc2 * Oil prices set to end week with strong gains               \par \par \uc2 By Hideyuki Sano and Nichola Saminather               \par \par \uc2 TOKYO/SINGAPORE, March 4 (Reuters) - Asian shares looked set on Friday to post their strongest week in five months as global investors returned to riskier assets after a string of positive U.S. economic data and a bounce in oil and commodity prices.               \par \par \uc2 That optimism is likely to flow through to European shares, with financial spreadbetters expecting Britain's FTSE 100 , Germany's DAX and France's CAC to all open 0.5 percent higher.               \par \par \uc2 The rebound could continue if the February U.S. employment report later in the session shows job gains but remains weak enough to discourage \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  rate increases in the near term.               \par \par \uc2 MSCI\uc2 's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan  rose 0.6 percent to the highest in almost two months. That put in on track for a 5.5 percent gain for the week, its strongest weekly performance since October.               \par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei closed up 0.3 percent, producing a weekly gain of 5.1 percent.               \par \par \uc2 "Globally, markets are rolling back the extreme risk-off trading they did in January and February," said Norihiro Fujito, senior investment strategist at \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities\uc2 . "Part of the reason is that the Fed seems to be easing its insistence on raising rates."               \par \par \uc2 Chinese shares, reversed earlier losses, with the Shanghai Composite index climbing 0.2 percent, heading for a weekly gain of 3.5 percent. The CSI 300 advanced 1 percent, extending gains this week to 4.7 percent.               \par \par \uc2 Investors are awaiting the start of the annual meeting of China's parliament on Saturday, which will map out economic goals for the next five years.               \par \par \uc2 The MSCI World equity index covering 46 markets  held near its two-month high touched on Thursday.               \par \par \uc2 The rally was led by emerging markets, with a measure of emerging-markets shares rising 0.4 percent on Friday for a sixth day of gains, its longest winning streak since October.               \par \par \uc2 The biggest move on Thursday came from Brazil's Bovespa index, which rose more than 5 percent, its biggest gain in six years, on news that President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  could be implicated in a sweeping corruption scandal.               \par \par \uc2 That encouraged investors who blame her administration's policies for driving Brazil into deep recession.               \par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, the S&P 500 Index rose 0.35 percent to a two-month high of 1,993.4.               \par \par \uc2 U.S. data on Thursday was positive on the whole, with factory orders rising and the service sector index showing continued expansion.               \par \par \uc2 Somewhat dimming the optimism, however, the services survey showed employment in the sector fell in February for the first time in two years.               \par \par \uc2 But that was not necessarily bad for U.S. stocks, as it helped to reduce expectations for a Fed rate hike this month, and pushed the dollar lower.               \par \par \uc2 The dollar's index against a basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2    slipped 0.6 percent on Thursday. It held steady at 97.636 on Friday.               \par \par \uc2 The dollar's weakness also helped \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan strengthen for a fourth day, while suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also lent support.               \par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  set the midpoint rate  at 6.5284 per dollar prior to market open, 0.20 percent firmer than the previous fix.               \par \par \uc2 Spot yuan opened at 6.5280 per dollar. It hit an intraday high of 6.5099 around 11:30 a.m. local time (0330 GMT), apparently supported by state-bank dollar sales, before trimming gains to 6.5234.               \par \par \uc2 The dollar's weakness also helped push gold to a 13-month high of $1,268.30 per ounce on Thursday. The precious metal was last trading at 1,261.20.               \par \par \uc2 The euro jumped back to $1.0953 from Wednesday's one-month low of $1.08255.               \par \par \uc2 The yen traded at 113.83 to the dollar, recovering from Wednesday's two-week low of 114.56.               \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar stood at $0.7367, holding near a three-month high of $0.7374 hit on Thursday, helped by rising iron ore prices. It's on track for a weekly gain of 3.3 percent, the most in five months.               \par \par \uc2 Spot iron ore for immediate delivery to China's Tianjin port <.IO62-CNI=SI> hit a 4-1/2-month high on Thursday.               \par \par \uc2 Commodity prices have been on the mend, with oil recovering more than 30 percent from January's 12-year lows, most recently helped by data showing U.S. oil production fell to its lowest since November 2014.               \par \par \uc2 Brent futures touched a two-month high of $37.40 per barrel on Wednesday. They were back up to that level on Friday, heading for a gain of 6.6 percent this week.               \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-3-4T07:38:53.000Z \par \par \uc2 