\par\par \b \uc2 With G20 hopes low, beaten-down emerging nations go it alone to stop rot\b0\par\par\uc2 886 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Februar 2016\par 17:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 24 (Reuters) - With little chance of coordinated market action from G20 finance chiefs meeting on Friday, the developing economies most hurt by three years of attrition in markets have begun to win investor support for their own efforts to draw a line in the sand.\par \par \uc2 Major emerging market economies including Mexico, Brazil, South Korea and India have made fresh moves in the past month to defend their battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , coupling often aggressive open-market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with warnings against speculators.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  meanwhile spent almost $100 billion of hard cash reserves last month keeping its tightly controlled yuan stable.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2  said on Thursday that its surprise decision last week to raise interest rates and buy about $2 billion of pesos was intended to signal that a 40 percent fall in the peso since 2013 was enough.\par \par \uc2 "It was a way of showing our protest, our clear rejection of those levels," said Carstens, who called on big emerging powers last month to stem capital outflows in coordinated fashion.\par \par \uc2 Central bankers and finance ministers of the world's 20 richest countries will meet in Shanghai this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Little chance is seen of a grand plan to calm shaky markets even though many investors blame misaligned exchange rates and economic policies in host country \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  and elsewhere for this year's volatility.\par \par \uc2 After \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the tune of $2 billion in defence of the won last week, \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned sternly that "herd behaviour in the market has been intensifying".\par \par \uc2 While another bout of global jitters hurt many of those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  again on Wednesday, major investment funds and international banks say these efforts could help turn the tide.\par \par \uc2 "The interesting dynamic has been the number of emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In a week ahead of a G20 that is quite strange," said Richard Benson, co-head of portfolio management with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund Millennium in London.\par \par \uc2 "In a world of competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations, we've reached a point where people don't want any more."\par \par \uc2 BOTTOM?\par \par \uc2 Some big investors have turned positive on emerging market local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt in recent weeks, judging that a sell-off dating back to 2013 may finally be bottoming out.\par \par \uc2 "With oil and commodity prices stabilising you get stabilisation of fundamentals, and you can do selection," said Sergio Trigo Paz, head of emerging market debt at BlackRock, the world's largest asset manager.\par \par \uc2 A 25 percent-plus surge in the dollar index since 2013 -- and even bigger gains against emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- have been a key motivation for U.S. investors to flee emerging market trades.\par \par \uc2 But diminishing expectations of further U.S. interest rate rises have pushed \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the euro and yen higher against the dollar, Trigo Paz noted.\par \par \uc2 "Once this happens, people start to venture into emerging markets," he said. "When you see emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  flat to positive for the year it gives you a good signal on how the emerging asset class will fare when volatility starts to fade."\par \par \uc2 ACTION\par \par \uc2 The G20's mantra for a decade has been that countries should not devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost exports. But current crisis settings might make moves -- especially those to oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness -- more acceptable.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's decision to ditch piecemeal dollar auctions for more aggressive action has already secured a handful of recommendations from major banks to buy the peso.\par \par \uc2 Omni, a hedge fund which recently went public with its bets against China's yuan, says Mexico differs from many Asian countries in being able to back the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with higher interest rates.\par \par \uc2 As well as the big issue of Chinese dollar-denominated corporate debt, burgeoning household debt has forced policymakers in countries like South Korea and Thailand to keep borrowing costs low.\par \par \uc2 "Unlike China, Mexico does not have a core credit story that would create a downward spiral," Omni fund manager Chris Morrison said.\par \par \uc2 "So the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes out and says enough is enough, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is too cheap, and we're hiking 50 basis points to show you that we think it is too cheap. That for me is a credible policy."\par \par \uc2 COORDINATION?\par \par \uc2 With China having spent almost $700 billion in a year to stem a slide in the yuan, there had been speculation that the G20 summit might come up with a deal reminiscent of the 1985 Plaza Accord that would devalue the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Those expectations have largely evaporated, however, and the group is now expected only to back Chinese efforts to stabilise its markets and avoid a spillover to the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 But the language of the post-meeting communique is still seen as a potential risk, not least given the need of the emerging nations for some gesture of support.\par \par \uc2 Peter Marber, head of emerging market investment at Loomis Sayles, is another investor who has recently allocated more to emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds.\par \par \uc2 "It's tough to build a case to be super-long into this weekend's G20 meeting, but there is the possibility of some coordinated action to prop up EM \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. dollar," Marber said.\par \par \uc2 "Small, but it might happen." (Writing by Patrick Graham; Editing by Catherine Evans)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-2-27T16:49:33.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160224ec2o01n9d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
