\par\par \b \uc2 Year of the Monkey: Anxiety For China & EM Investors? -- Barron's Blog\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dimitra DeFotis   \par \uc2 576 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2016\par 23:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 As the Year of the Monkey beckons across Asia, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  has zodiac advice for investors: be clever like the monkey when navigating pitfalls in China.\par \par \uc2 Last week was a rather sour one for equities: The iShares China Large-Cap exchange-traded fund ( FXI) fell roughly 5% last week, while the \uc2 iShares MSCI Emerging Markets ETF\uc2  ( EEM) slipped nearly 2%. Among stocks, \uc2 Melco Crown Entertainment\uc2  ( MPEL) fell nearly 12% last week, while Baidu ( BIDU) and NetEase ( NTES) slipped 11%. \uc2 PetroChina\uc2  ( PTR) fell 3%, \uc2 Ctrip.com International\uc2  ( CTRP) slipped more than 8% and Bank of China ( BACHY) slipped 1.7%. Among the winners: \uc2 Aluminum Corporation of China\uc2  ( ACH) rose 6.4% and \uc2 Trina Solar\uc2  ( TSL) was up nearly 4%.\par \par \uc2 Barclays Andreas Kolbe, Mitul Kotecha and Aroop Chatterjee write:\par \par \uc2 "Emerging market investors are likely to look with some anxiety into what the Chinese New Year has in store. ... Concerns about China's growth prospects and the knock-on effects on commodity prices have been a driving factor for emerging market and global risky assets, at least since summer 2015. And recent data do not provide any reasons to think that these concerns may fade soon. China's NBS manufacturing PMI was 49.4 in January, below expectations, and we expect this Sunday's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves data release to show another large decline by $140 billion amid ongoing capital outflows. Given China's drag, we have lowered our growth forecasts for some Asian economies, including Korea and Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 The decline in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and the measures the Chinese authorities have taken, aimed at limiting \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  transactions, have raised doubts about the sustainability of the local bid for Chinese U.S.-dollar credit (which has remained much more resilient than global emerging market benchmarks.) We think that short-term demand may slow; hence, we recommend scaling back exposure to BBB-rated Chinese corporates ...\par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  is scheduled to report its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves for January on Sunday, 7 February. Our analysis of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's liquidity \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  implies that China's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves could have dropped by up to $140 billion ($130 billion after valuation adjustments) to around $3.19 trillion in January. If correct, this would be the biggest monthly drop in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves on record, beating the previous record fall seen in December 2015. Note, small modifications in our assumptions can change these estimates to a wide range of $80-180 billion ... China's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves shrank by $513 billion over 2015 and are down by $663 billion since hitting a peak in August 2014. The $108 billion fall in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in December is currently the largest drop on record. As we noted in RMB -- Loosening the reins, 7 January 2016, this was surprising given that China allowed a faster CNY adjustment against the USD during the month and was perceived to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  less ...\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves data may set a negative tone for emerging markets into the coming week, especially if our expectation of a sharp drop in reserves proves correct. That said, Chinese New Year holidays across much of Asia may dampen overall activity. There will also be plenty of attention on developments in oil prices and Fed rhetoric ... " \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par  More at Barron's Emerging Markets blog, \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://blogs.barrons.com/emergingmarketsdaily/}}}\par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 05, 2016 17:19 ET (22:19 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160205ec250048n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
