\par\par \b \uc2 As China  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Reserves Plummet, What's Beijing's Bottom Line?\b0\par\par\uc2 691 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Februar 2016\par 13:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 As China's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  hoard drops by hundred-billion-dollar chunks, a key question for economists and investors is what China's bottom line is and what Beijing can do to defend it.\par \par \uc2 China's net \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves have fallen by over $600 billion from their mid-2014 peak of $3.99 trillion as Beijing \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up its weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's capital outflow was as much as $1 trillion last year by some estimates if trade surpluses and inbound investment flows are included.\par \par \uc2 Some put Beijing's red line at $3 trillion, which may not be far off if reserves fall another $200 billion by early March, as some forecast. China's January figures are due out on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Others cite a $2.6 trillion figure, roughly the amount needed to safeguard liquidity under \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  metrics that take into account China's short-term external debt, exports, external portfolio liabilities and M2, a measure of money supply, according to \uc2 DBS Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still others believe China has no definite bottom line and, by implication, that it doesn't buy the \uc2 IMF\uc2  guidelines. Beijing is willing to spend another $400 billion this year and $300 billion in 2017 to prop up the yuan, also known as the renminbi, says RHB Research economist Zhang Fan. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability will be the priority," he said.\par \par \uc2 Trying to assess what Beijing is thinking takes on added importance as China's problems batter global markets, commodity producers and confidence. \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  watchdog didn't immediately respond to questions.\par \par \uc2 Whether Beijing can stem the outflow soon is an open question. Economists say Beijing is working particularly hard to keep ordinary households from losing confidence in the yuan. Recent state media articles criticizing billionaire investor \uc2 George Soros\uc2  for his negative comments on China's economy are aimed at bringing the home crowd on side, some say, in a country where investors aren't always swayed by fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 "Chinese even hoard garlic and onions," said OCBC Bank economist Dongming Xie. "They could start hoarding U.S. dollars."\par \par \uc2 China faces difficult options as it approaches any red line, analysts say. With top leaders in the one-party state endorsing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability, it's difficult to backtrack and let the yuan find market equilibrium. A sharp depreciation, meanwhile, could spark a global "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" and increase bad bank loans in China. And tighter capital controls could blunt already-weakened financial reforms.\par \par \uc2 All the while, the cost of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  grows as national wealth slips away. "If the day comes when \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are used up, the renminbi will become waste paper overseas," wrote a user identified as MeiWenMeiLe_489 on the Sina news website.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, Beijing has sought to entice more capital into China and stepped up efforts to prevent it from leaving, an exercise at times resembling a game of whack-a-mole as investors find creative ways around controls that limit individuals to $50,000 in annual foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases. "Their attempts at stricter \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls are in tatters," said IG Markets Ltd. analyst Angus Nicholson.\par \par \uc2 There are some potential silver linings, economists say. Once Chinese companies repay most of their offshore debt, outflow pressure could ease. And Chinese investors may start repatriating capital if they see signs of stability given generally higher returns in China than abroad.\par \par \uc2 Still, China's economic mandarins are on the defensive in a game that's largely about confidence, analysts say, leading to a pattern of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bad economic news, further yuan depreciation and more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "It's a vicious cycle," said Chen Long, an analyst with research firm Gavekal Dragonomics. "Each bit of stability is temporary."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials hoping for a break over the coming Lunar New Year holiday may find it cut short by global markets that rarely rest. "The Chinese government faces some big decisions," said IG's Mr. Nicholson. "And it's coming faster than many Chinese leaders want."\par \par \uc2 --Mark Magnier. Follow him on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @markmagnier.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 05-02-16 1245GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020160205ec25000c2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
