\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Bets against China's yuan build as traders eye G20 deal\b0\par\par\uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2016\par 15:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Updates with graphic)\par \par \uc2 By Patrick Graham and Anirban Nag\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Feb 3 (Reuters) - Billions of dollars in new bets against China's yuan have been placed on derivatives markets this week as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers weigh the chance of an official devaluation around this month's G20 finance chiefs' meeting in Shanghai.\par \par \uc2 Yuan volatility and the bias towards a weaker yuan in options markets have surged to record highs in the past week and dealers say billions of "low delta puts", which pay out only when the offshore yuan rate gets above 7.20 per dollar, have been taken out.\par \par \uc2 The yuan is also back under pressure in the offshore spot market, falling to a three-week low of 6.6510 yuan as London opened on Wednesday. Onshore rates, which China controls tightly, were steady at 6.5778.\par \par \uc2 One-month volatility on the offshore yuan jumped from below 8 percent to almost 10 percent, a record high, versus 8.5 percent on the euro-dollar equivalent.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://tmsnrt.rs/1P6bkUm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://tmsnrt.rs/1P6bkUm}}}\par \par \uc2 Traders said option volumes - difficult to track because most of the market is conducted over-the-counter rather than on traceable platforms or exchanges - reached $12 billion on Monday and almost $17 billion on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In morning trade in London, when dealers in the world's biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading centre are operating alongside their counterparts in Beijing and Shanghai, the bias toward a weaker yuan - essentially a net measurement - on 1-, 2-, 3- and 6-month contracts all surpassed record levels hit in August.\par \par \uc2 "Clearly, the market sees that the intensive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from PBoC (\uc2 People's Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is not sustainable, and therefore the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  go at some point," said Hao Zhou, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Much talk centres around how much more China will have leaked in reserves in January, in data due by the end of this week.\par \par \uc2 Reuters polling of more than a dozen banks puts the fall at a record $130 billion, reducing China's warchest to combat yuan weakness to $3.2 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Some hedge funds betting against the yuan have speculated the drop will be $200 billion or more. The sales desk of one large international bank in London was circulating an estimate of $262 billion to selected clients on Tuesday in an email seen by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 WARNINGS\par \par \uc2 Analysts from \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  called on Friday for G20 financial leaders to agree next month in Shanghai to joint steps that would include a one-off devaluation of the yuan and a commitment to a stable dollar to prop up flagging growth and head off another financial market panic.\par \par \uc2 Against that, China has repeatedly warned "speculators" in the run up to the week-long Lunar New Year starting this weekend that it will keep the yuan steady.\par \par \uc2 China launches its 12-month presidency of the G20 group of developed and developing economies with the Shanghai meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors on Feb. 26-27.\par \par \uc2 Another big report this week on the yuan, from analysts at French \uc2 bank Societe Generale\uc2 , gave a one-in-three probability of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sliding to 7.50 by the end of 2016.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  (\uc2 PBoC\uc2 ) may insist that it has no intention to devalue the yuan, but capital flows are putting significant downward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . China's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are large but far from unlimited, or even sufficient, if large capital outflows persist.\par \par \uc2 "Our central scenario (65 percent probability) envisions USD/CNY reaching 6.80 in 2016 in a largely gradual and controlled manner, but there is a large and growing risk that USD/CNY trades up to 7.50 this year." (Graphic by Nigel Stephenson; Editing by Ruth Pitchford) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-2-3T15:07:47.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160203ec2300krq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
