\par\par \b \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  Sees China Stock Markets Improving -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,921 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2016\par 02:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0135 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Analysts at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  say they expect the Chinese stock market to improve late in the first quarter and early second quarter. That's after shares on the mainland had their worst first-month performance in history. But investors should "expect market conditions to improve" as domestic growth accelerates and volatility in the yuan market subsides, the firm writes in a report earlier today. The latter, it says, will be "possibly provoked by a more dovish Fed," that is, a scenario where the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signals it is less likely to raise interest rates. Goldman singles out a list of companies trading at historically low valuations, including \uc2 Fosun International\uc2  (0656.HK), the Chinese private conglomerate's main listing in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2  The firm concludes, as many other analysts have, that Chinese stocks are cheap on average measures: The forward P/E and trading P/B of Chinese stocks both onshore and offshore are close to or already below the historical troughs in 2008, taking into account market caps of firms. Still, in the onshore market, small and mid-caps are still a lot more expensive than large caps. That means the median P/E of the mainland stock market sits at more than 18 times, compared with 11 times at the bottom of the financial crisis.(chao.deng@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0128 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's surprise decision to adopt a negative interest rate may be aimed at positively affecting wage increases and capital expenditure plans for next fiscal year starting in April, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  economists say. "The move can also be expected to slow down the flow of capital into Japan and hold back yen appreciation," they say. "Other effects, such as a pickup in housing investment, capex, and M&As, through a decline in financing costs and downward pressure on long-term yields overseas are not necessarily certain but could be noteworthy," they add. A negative rate will be adopted, starting Feb. 16. (megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0128 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] South Korea's weaker-than-expected exports, which sank to nearly a seven-year low in January, were due to fewer working days and soft global demand, the trade ministry said. The first month of this year has one fewer business days than last year. Despite the data distortion, the latest reading was disappointing enough to dash hopes for a quick recovery in exports in 2016. Exports shrank 18.5% from a year earlier in January--the worst print since in August 2009 when the financial crisis battered the country's trade--missed the market expectation of a 12.3% drop. The January read marks the 13th straight month of declines. Last year, Korean shipments overseas declined every single month on sluggish global trade, lower oil prices and China's slowdown. The Korean economy is heavily geared towards exports and the health of its trade data is an indicator of the robustness of the global economy. (kwanwoo.jun@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0125 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The impact on Australia's inflation rate from a falling Australian dollar is beginning to become more visible, says Annette Beacher, chief economist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 TD Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Asia-Pacific. The effect of the lower exchange rate is in every indicator now, including terms of trade data, producer price indices and headline inflation, she says. Still, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's ability to cut interest rates again in the near-term remains in place, as all inflation indicators are muted overall, she adds. (james.glynn@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0123 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysia's financial markets are closed for a holiday, but in the round-the-clock offshore market, the ringgit fell as much as 0.7% against the U.S. dollar Monday. The implied value of the ringgit derived from the offshore-traded non-deliverable forwards market fell to as low as 4.1700 from around 4.1400 Friday. The ringgit's weakening comes in the wake of gloomy economic data released earlier. China's manufacturing PMI for January was worse than expected, falling to 49.4 from December's 49.7, and below the median forecast of 49.6. Earlier, South Korea's export data disappointed as well, with a steep drop of 18.5% on top of the previous month's 14.1% slide, making it the 13th straight fall. The risk-off tone has crippled other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and tamed a fledgling stock market rally in the region. USD/MYR is now trading at an implied spot level of 4.1650 from its Friday closing of 4.1400. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0122 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's official manufacturing purchasing managers' index, a gauge of the nation's factory activity, fell to 49.4 in January, the lowest level since August 2012 when the reading hit 49.2. The index, released by the National Bureau of Statistics on Monday, marks the sixth-straight month of contraction in the measure of manufacturing activity. A PMI reading above 50 indicates an expansion in manufacturing activity, while a reading below points to a contraction.\u8203\'20\'0B The subindices of new orders and productions all dropped in January, pointing to continued sluggishness in the world's second largest economy. (grace.zhu@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0120 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] USD/KRW is higher Monday, trading at 1,210.0 from its Friday close of 1,199.1, representing a 0.9% drop for the won versus the U.S. dollar. A much poorer-than-expected reading of South Korea's exports for January may have been responsible for the won's decline. Exports fell by a steep 18.5% compared to the 14.1% drop in December, and disappointed against the median forecast for a smaller drop of 12.3%. This was the 13th straight negative reading. The gloomy export sector data bodes poorly for South Korea's manufacturing PMI data due at 0130 GMT. If that number disappoints as well, it would corroborate the export data's bleak outlook, not just for South Korea, but also for the global economy. USD/KRW could then spike into the Bollinger uptrend channel at 1,212 and thus confirm a bullish technical bias which would imply a greater chance of won weakness versus the greenback ahead. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0120 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Evergrande Real Estate\f28 \uc2 \f2  (3333.HK), which is one of the most active issuers in the offshore bond market, is seeking consent from holders of its U.S. dollar bonds due in 2018 and 2020 to bring the covenants into conformity with those of the bonds due 2019 which it issued last month. The Chinese property developer didn't elaborate details of the proposed amendments, but says the terms are more flexible which will enable it to "pursue additional business opportunities and further business growth". Evergrande will pay a consent fee of US$3.50 for each US$1,000 in principal amount if bond holders agreed to the amendments. The consent solicitation will be closed on February 19. Last month, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2  downgraded Evergrand's rating to B2 from B1 with negative outlook on the view that its debt-funded expansion has increased its financial risk. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  has a B+ rating on Evergrande with a negative outlook, while Fitch has BB- rating on Evergrande with a negative outlook. (carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0119 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The weakness in China's official purchasing managers' index in January--which declined to a lower-than-expected 49.4 last month from 49.7 in December--suggests that China's corporate sector needs to deleverage in the face of weak demand, and that China is starting to see more aggressive cuts in production capacity, said Commerzbank economist Zhou Hao. The weakening services PMI last month also bears watching, he added. "China's economy still faces strong head winds," Zhou said. "China still requires aggressive fiscal and monetary policy to counter the weakness." (mark.magnier@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @MarkMagnier) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0116 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Hong Kong investors could react negatively to key China manufacturing data that came in lower than expected. Manufacturing activity fell to 49.4 in January, much lower than the 49.6 forecast and the 49.7 in December, according to official China data. Investors are still digesting the news, as Hang Seng Index futures were trading flat before suddenly plunging 0.6% before the market open. An unofficial gauge, the Caixin manufacturing PMI, is due soon after the open and any surprises on the downside could also sway the mood of investors as they try to get a sense of the pace of China's economic slowdown. Any reactions from China to the data, in the form of more stimulus policies, or interest rate cuts, will also be crucial bellwethers for the Hong Kong market. (dominique.fong@wsj.com; @dominiquefong) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0111 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Asia's credit markets are facing a challenging year in 2016 due to heightened market concerns over China's growth slowdown and the pace of interest rate normalization in the U.S., says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "It is becoming evident that the Chinese government's primary objectives of maintaining growth in excess of 6.5%, implementing reform and economic rebalancing, and ensuring macroeconomic and financial stability cannot be achieved in unison," says Michael Taylor, Moody's chief credit officer for Asia Pacific. "China's policy trilemma is creating uncertainty for regional credit," says Taylor, adding that recent stimulus measures in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  suggest that growth and stability in the country will be prioritized at the likely expense of a slower pace of reform and delays to system deleveraging. As for Asia credit overall, the flat or slower growth for most Asian economies in 2016 and capital flow volatility will weigh on the region's rated debt issuers, particularly in the corporate sector, the rating agency says. Moreover, weak growth and commodity price declines will interact with foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility to raise credit risk across the region, it adds. (carol.chan@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 31 Jan 2016 20:35 ET \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sees China Stock Markets Improving -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0111 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China's January manufacturing PMI fell to 49.4 from December's 49.7, and was also worse than The Wall Street Journal's consensus forecast for a reading of 49.6. The sub-50 number signifies a contraction of the manufacturing sector and was the weakest it has been in more than three years. There was an immediate risk-averse reaction across asset classes after the data were released. Japan's Nikkei eased to a 1.2% gain from a 1.4% rally, Brent crude oil slipped from close to $36 per barrel to its current $35.49, and the Singapore dollar and the South Korea won weakened versus the safe haven U.S. dollar. The Australia dollar also slid, while the euro rallied, adding to signs of risk-aversion. Earlier, South Korea's export data was a disappointment, showing a contraction of 18.5% from an earlier 14.1% drop, thus adding to a downbeat mood in Asian markets. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 31, 2016 20:35 ET (01:35 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160201ec2100047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
