\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  War: U.S. Hedge Funds Mount New Attacks on China's Yuan\b0\par\par\uc2 By Juliet Chung and Carolyn Cui   \par \uc2 1,321 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Februar 2016\par 02:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires Chinese (English)\par \uc2 RTNW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Some of the biggest names in the hedge-fund industry are piling up bets against China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , setting up a showdown between Wall Street and the leaders of the world's second-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 Kyle Bass's Hayman Capital Management has sold off the bulk of its investments in stocks, commodities and bonds so it can focus on shorting Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the yuan and the Hong Kong dollar.\par \par \uc2 It is the biggest concentrated wager that the Dallas-based firm has made since its profitable bet years ago against the U.S. housing market. About 85% of Hayman Capital's portfolio is now invested in trades that are expected to pay off if the yuan and Hong Kong dollar depreciate over the next three years--a bet with billions of dollars on the line, including borrowed money.\par \par \uc2 "When you talk about orders of magnitude, this is much larger than the subprime crisis," said Mr. Bass, who believes the yuan could fall as much as 40% in that period.\par \par \uc2 Billionaire trader Stanley Druckenmiller and hedge-fund manager \uc2 David Tepper\uc2  have staked out positions of their own against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also known as the renminbi, according to people familiar with the matter. David Einhorn's Greenlight Capital Inc. holds options on the yuan depreciating.\par \par \uc2 The funds' bets come at a time of enormous sensitivity for China's leaders. The government is struggling on multiple fronts to manage a soft landing for the economy, deal with a heavily indebted banking system and navigate the transition to consumer-led growth.\par \par \uc2 Expectations for a weaker yuan have led to an exodus of capital by Chinese residents and foreign investors. Though it still boasts the largest holding of foreign reserves at $3.3 trillion, China has experienced huge outflows in recent months. Hedge funds are gambling that China will let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken further in a bid to halt a flood of money leaving the country and jump-start economic growth.\par \par \uc2 The effort is a lot riskier, though, than taking on a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  whose value is set by the market. China's state-run economy gives the government a number of levers to pull and tremendous resources at its disposal. Earlier this year, state institutions bought up so much yuan in the Hong Kong market where foreigners place most of their bets that overnight borrowing costs shot up to 66%, making it difficult to finance short positions and sending the yuan up sharply.\par \par \uc2 The situation grew more tense after billionaire investor \uc2 George Soros\uc2  predicted at the \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2  gathering in Davos, Switzerland, recently that "a hard landing is practically unavoidable" for China's economy. He said he is betting against commodity-producing countries and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a result.\par \par \uc2 Days later, a commentary appeared in China's state-run \uc2 Xinhua News Agency\uc2  warning that "radical speculators" trying to short sell, or bet against, the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would "suffer huge losses" as the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes "effective measures to stabilize the value of the yuan."\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Soros Fund Management, Mr. Soros's family office, declined to comment on the firm's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  positions.\par \par \uc2 The show of force has scared off some fund managers from adding to their wagers. Some traders have scaled back or even exited from their short bets, saying they have little appetite to go up against the Chinese government. Some say they are looking with new interest at shorting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of other Asian countries that they expect would fall if the yuan keeps depreciating.\par \par \uc2 The standoff harks back to big battles such as Soros's bet against the British pound a quarter-century ago. In 1997, Malaysia's prime minister blamed Mr. Soros for a run on the ringgit during the Asian financial crisis. Mr. Druckenmiller, then chief investment officer for Soros Fund Management, said at the time that while the main Soros hedge fund had earlier shorted the ringgit, it bought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the crisis, cushioning its fall.\par \par \uc2 Hayman Capital began betting against the yuan last year after studying China's banking system and being stunned at its rapid expansion of debt. The firm's analysis suggested that past-due loans, which currently stand at about 2% of the total, would rise sharply and eventually require an injection by the central government of trillions of dollars of yuan to recapitalize the banks. An expansion of the \uc2 Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet would lead its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken, just as the dollar depreciated when the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  bailed out U.S. banks during the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Broader market bets against the yuan began growing last August, when the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  unexpectedly devalued the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by 2% against the U.S. dollar. The move fueled speculation that Beijing eventually would have to decouple the yuan from the strengthening dollar and follow other countries to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a way to buoy growth.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Druckenmiller, who now invests his own wealth, and one of his former prot\u233\'00\'E9g\u233\'00\'E9s, Zach Schreiber, who runs the roughly $10 billion hedge-fund firm PointState Capital LP, also have had sizable shorts against the renminbi since last year, people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 The wager helped PointState gain about 15% last year, said a person familiar with the firm, and has contributed to gains of more than 5% through mid-January.\par \par \uc2 Traders who remain bearish now are shorting China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in several ways, say people familiar with the trades. Some are betting that the gap between the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's onshore exchange rate and more market-sensitive offshore rate will diverge further. Early in January, the spread hit a record of 0.1367 before government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  narrowed it sharply.\par \par \uc2 Two days before \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  devalued its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last August, William Ackman's \uc2 Pershing Square Capital Management LP\uc2  began to put on a "large notional short position in the Chinese yuan through the purchase of puts and put spreads" to hedge against "unanticipated weakness in the Chinese economy," according to Mr. Ackman's annual investor letter, disclosed this past week.\par \par \uc2 But, demonstrating how difficult it can be to profit from such bets, Mr. Ackman said in the letter the wager had generated only a modest profit for Pershing Square and had been insufficient to offset the firm's larger losses, as China continued to defend the exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ackman wrote that he continued to hold the position because of its importance as a hedge.\par \par \uc2 Other firms that have profited from shorting China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  include the $2 billion Scoggin Capital Management and \uc2 Carlyle Group LP\uc2 's Emerging Sovereign Group, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 For 2016, ESG's short-China fund, Nexus, was up more than 20% partway through January, according to people familiar with the fund, thanks in part to a large short position against the yuan. Much of Nexus's position is made up of options, one of the people said.\par \par \uc2 It was unclear how much exposure Greenlight and Mr. Tepper's \uc2 Appaloosa Management LP\uc2  have, though Mr. Tepper was outspoken last year in calling the yuan overvalued.\par \par \uc2 Since August, China has been imposing various rules to stabilize the exchange rate and stem the outflows, including a 20% reserve requirement applied to onshore yuan-derivative trades. That move makes it more expensive for funds to keep shorting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through swaps.\par \par \uc2 Chinese officials have indicated they aren't seeking to devalue the yuan in order to gain advantage over their trading partners, citing the need to avoid a damaging spiral of competitive devaluations as others follow suit. The country still has room to stimulate its economy through fiscal policies, economists say.\par \par \uc2 Write to Juliet Chung at juliet.chung@wsj.com and Carolyn Cui at carolyn.cui@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 01-02-16 0135GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNW000020160201ec210001z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
