\par\par \b \uc2 China's money rates slump for week as c.bank injects huge liquidity\b0\par\par\uc2 750 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2016\par 05:38\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SHANGHAI, Jan 29 (Reuters) - China money rates dropped\par sharply this week as the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pumped massive amounts of\par short-term liquidity into the markets to avoid any cash crunch\par ahead of the long Lunar New Year holiday, which falls in early\par February this year.\par     Repeated injections by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  might\par delay expected cuts in banks' required reserve ratios (RRR),\par which would increase long-term base money in the system and so\par add pressure on the yuan to depreciate further, a view that\par authorities are battling to discourage.\par     The volume-weighted average rate of the benchmark seven-day\par repo traded in the interbank market, considered\par the best indicator of general liquidity in China, was at 2.25\par percent by midday, down 9 basis points from 2.34 percent at the\par close last Friday.\par     The overnight rate was down 7 basis points at \par 1.96 percent by midday, while the 14-day repo \par plunged 87 basis points to 2.82 percent.\par     Amid increasing worries over the health of the world's\par second-largest economy, the yuan staged a sharp\par depreciation of more than 2 percent from late November to early\par January, when the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  began \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aggressively to prevent\par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from falling further.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's stock market has also tumbled more than 20\par percent so far this year on concerns about big share sales, the\par cooling economy and worries over the negative impact from the\par government's clampdown on irregular market practices.\par     "Money markets have become a battle ground for the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  to\par try to keep the yuan stable and prevent the stocks from slumping\par further," said a senior trader at a Chinese state-owned bank in\par Shanghai.\par     "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tries to tell stock investors that there\par is enough money and you need not worry, while it does not\par explain the fact that it is not pumping long-term base money\par into the system amid concerns over the yuan's depreciation."\par     In a surprise move, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  announced on Thursday that it\par would increase the frequency of open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and could\par conduct the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  every working day between Jan. 29 and\par Feb. 19 to maintain liquidity during the Lunar New Year.\par     It typically conducts such \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  only twice a week\par during non-holiday periods.\par     In a rare extraordinary open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Friday, it\par injected 100 billion yuan into the money markets, bringing total\par net injections this week to 690 billion yuan, the biggest weekly\par injection on record by Reuters calculations. \par     The \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  has injected 1,235 billion yuan ($188 billion) on a\par net basis via the open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  so far in 2016 and the\par Lunar New Year, which falls on Feb. 8, is still a week away.\par     In comparison, it injected only 400 billion yuan via open\par market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the run-up to the Lunar New Year in early\par 2015.\par     Together with Short-term Lending Facilities, Medium-term\par Lending Facilities and other liquidity tools, the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2  has\par pumped more than 2 trillion yuan ($304 billion) short-term funds\par into the markets over the past two weeks. (For a FACTBOX,\par click )\par     \par     Key money rates at a glance:\par   \par                   Volume-wei  Previous    Change (bps)\par                   ghted       day (%)     \par                   average                 \par                   rate (%)                \par  Interbank repo market\par  Overnight        1.9538      1.9675      -1.37  \par                                           \par                                           \par  Seven-day        2.2339      2.3128      -7.89   \par                                           \par                                           \par  14-day           2.8056      2.9574      -15.18  \par                                           \par                                           \par  Shanghai stock exchange repo market\par  Overnight        2.7900      0.6550      +213.50\par                                           \par                                           \par  Seven-day<CN7DR  3.5750      1.7550      +182.00     \par  PO=SS>                                   \par  14-day           2.6000      2.6700      -7.00    \par                                           \par                                           \par  PBOC Guidance Rates\par  Overnight        1.9800      2.0000      -2.00      \par  <CN1DRPFIX=CFXS                          \par  >                                        \par  Seven-day        2.4000      2.4000      +0.00      \par  <CN7DRPFIX=CFXS                          \par  >                                        \par  14-day           2.9500      2.9500      +0.00      \par  <CN14DRPFIX=CFX                          \par  S>                            