\par\par \b \uc2 George Soros\uc2  in China's Crosshairs After Predicting Tough Economic Times Ahead\b0\par\par\uc2 773 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Januar 2016\par 10:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China is putting a face on the economic pessimism it accuses of helping weaken the yuan and the economy: billionaire investor \uc2 George Soros\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A front-page commentary published in some editions of People's Daily on Tuesday appeared to warn Mr. Soros would lose any bets he made based on a recent prediction that hard economic times for China are "unavoidable."\par \par \uc2 Other state media followed suit. Denouncing "radical speculators," China's official \uc2 Xinhua News Agency\uc2  dismissed the famed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader's view as "the same prediction several times." The Global Times, in its English edition, asked, "So why are so many Western pundits and media outlets so intent on talking China down?"\par \par \uc2 The rhetorical shots come as \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  is making broader efforts with market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rule adjustments to offset the impact of its slowest growth rate in a quarter century, shore up grinding stock markets and stem surging capital outflows. China's state-run media regularly note concerns the economy is cooling, but they tend to highlight positive aspects of what the government describes as a broad economic restructuring.\par \par \uc2 The uniformity and prominent placement in government-run media of the challenges to foreign critics, including economists quoted by Western newspapers, appear to suggest growing concern in Beijing that negative sentiment is spreading.\par \par \uc2 State media warnings directed at private individuals like Mr. Soros are rare. But his legend as an investor stems from a career making profitable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bets -- both real and rumored -- that are widely studied in China. It comes just as \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is taking steps to limit flight from the Chinese yuan by its huge middle class.\par \par \uc2 Suspicion in China that Mr. Soros is now placing bets against the yuan follow comments he made last week at the \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2  in Switzerland. "A hard-landing is practically unavoidable," Mr. Soros told \uc2 Bloomberg Television\uc2 . "I'm not expecting it, I'm observing it."\par \par \uc2 "Declaring war on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Ha," said the People's Daily commentary, which appeared in the overseas edition, a newspaper aimed at Chinese living outside China. The paper serves as the official purveyor of Communist Party views, and the commentary was authored by a researcher at \uc2 China's Commerce Ministry\uc2 . It wasn't published in the domestic editions, though it did appear online.\par \par \uc2 Similar words appeared in other key state media. Multiple news items published by \uc2 Xinhua\uc2  raised concerns Mr. Soros would back up his view with powerful bets of a plunge in the yuan or the Hong Kong dollar. "If Soros really does that this time, he will definitely lose money," economist Li Daokui told \uc2 Xinhua\uc2 . "I am worried for him as a friend."\par \par \uc2 On social media, many commentators doubted even a rich investor like Mr. Soros could alter Chinese fundamentals, with one saying he "might be able to bully a small country." Others injected humor, including a widely circulated joke that cast doubt China has the firepower to fight a savvy billionaire following bloody losses in its stock market.\par \par \uc2 Blaming international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators isn't a surprise, according to another well-known investor, Jim Rogers, who says they "always" get faulted for market turmoil no matter where, despite evidence that domestic investors are typically a more powerful force. "Everybody looks for a scapegoat," he said by telephone from Singapore.\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Rogers, a one-time partner of Mr. Soros, says his concern is that China's slowdown is getting unfair blame for global difficulties, which he says stem from indebted governments in the U.S., Europe and Japan. "I see serious economic problems in the world, worse than we saw in 2008," said Mr. Rogers, noting that he remains an investor in China's yuan and listed stocks. "It's clear these problems didn't start in China."\par \par \uc2 Almost three years ago, Mr. Soros had predicted China was headed for economic hard times -- and much of the view was reported by the country's state media at the time.\par \par \uc2 Speaking in April 2013 at the Boao Forum, an event the Chinese government styles as its version of Davos, Mr. Soros said the country's banking system was troubled, its transition to the consumer-led economy wouldn't be easy and that slower growth could sap confidence, all of which could "precipitate a hard landing." Mr. Soros added, "The current growth model can continue for another year or two, but not for a decade."\par \par \uc2 --James T. Areddy, with contributions by \uc2 Yang Jie\uc2 . Follow James on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  @jamestareddy. \par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 27, 2016 04:27 ET (09:27 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160127ec1r0014q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
