\par\par \b \uc2 2016 Roundtable Part 2: Peering Into The Future -- Barron's\b0\par\par\uc2 9,617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2016\par 06:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par R2\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM BARRON'S 1/25/16) \par    By Lauren R. Rublin \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A funny thing happened last week at the local Trader Joe's. Your hungry Roundtable editor popped in for some dinner provisions, and got an unbidden earful of market talk from a cheerfully bearish cashier. Stocks will fall a lot further, he predicted. The Baltic Dry Index, which measures the cost to ship stuff like grain and steel around the world, has collapsed. Inventories are piling up, deflation is looming, and the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2  might weigh a fourth round of quantitative easing.\f28 \par \par \uc2 It was like deja vu all over again: The 2016 Barron's Roundtable, convened Jan. 11 in New York, was heavy on the same worrisome talk, and last week's frequently dismal stock action only confirmed the prescience of our assembled market seers.\par \par \uc2 While big-picture prognostications dominated the first Roundtable issue, published Jan. 18, they also feature prominently in this week's installment, the second of three. But, happily, the four experts showcased in the pages ahead also have plenty to say about specific investments that could yield rich returns in the year ahead, notwithstanding the troublesome backdrop.\par \par \uc2 Sharing the bear's lair this year are Roundtable veteran Felix Zulauf, the sagacious Swiss investor who helms Zulauf Asset Management, and newcomer \uc2 Jeffrey Gundlach\uc2 , co-founder of DoubleLine Capital, a mostly bond-focused shop whose returns have dazzled since its formation in 2009. Both riff at length on the potential calamity facing emerging markets due to China's cooling economy, and think U.S. stocks haven't seen their lows yet. Felix offers a bevy of short-selling ideas to profit from the pain, while Jeffrey combs the fixed-income universe for mispriced assets, such as closed-end funds and a mortgage REIT, that could reward investors in multiple ways. A consummate contrarian, he also sees opportunity in Puerto Rico's distressed debt.\par \par \uc2 Abby Joseph Cohen, a standout strategist and public policy guru at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , also shares her best bets for the new year, in the form of seven stocks favored by the firm's analysts that amplify her current investment themes. Cautious about the industrial economy, she is tilting toward health care, retailing, and housing plays that could benefit from the consumer's improved finances.\par \par \uc2 Mario Gabelli\uc2 , the master of \uc2 Gamco Investors\uc2  and a shrewd guide to corporate deal-making, rounds out this week's quartet with a look at media companies that could enrich shareholders by buying, selling, or rearranging valuable assets. There is a lot to chew on in his calculations and commentary and in the rest of this meaty Roundtable segment.\par \par \uc2 ABBY JOSEPH COHEN\par \par \uc2 Barron's: Abby, what are you recommending today?\par \par \uc2 Cohen: As we discussed earlier in this session, capex [capital spending] has weakened among U.S. companies. But there has been a great deal of capex in some industries. While spending is weak in energy and chemicals, and among some heavily industrial companies, it is quite robust in industries such as digital technology and medical technology. Philips, based in the Netherlands, is a capex beneficiary, but not in the usual cyclical sense. Philips is selling for 10 times 2016 estimated earnings, below the sector P/E [price/earnings ratio] of 12.5 times. Its European shares have fallen 7% in the past 12 months, to 22.15 euros [closing price on Jan. 8] and yield 3.6%. Our analyst expects Philips to earn 2.21 euros a share [$2.39] this year, well above the consensus estimate of 1.64 euros.\par \par \uc2 I started looking at Philips after thinking about one of my picks from last year's Roundtable \uc2 Acuity Brands\uc2  [ticker: AYI], a maker of LED lighting. New energy-efficient technologies, like lighting, have a long road ahead in terms of growth. Philips has been around since 1891 but is always reinventing itself, and has a history of innovation in many categories, from traditional lightbulbs to Blu-ray discs. While the company is typically viewed as an industrial, it has a robust consumer business. It also has a robust lighting business.\par \par \uc2 Philips has a first-mover advantage in providing equipment such as lighting and ultrasound services to its customers. Philips' lighting is used in public and industrial spaces, but the area that intrigues me is health care.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: How so?\par \par \uc2 Cohen: The company makes CT scanners and MRI scanners. It has used its digital expertise to build a pretty robust business in hospital management and information management in the health-care industry. The stock trades in Amsterdam [PHIA.Netherlands] and via American depositary receipts [PHG].\par \par \uc2 Barron's: Which security do you recommend buying?\par \par \uc2 Cohen: If you are dollar-based, buy the ADR.\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: Would you hedge the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Oscar Schafer: The company does a lot of business outside the \uc2 European Union\uc2 . If the stock is going to work, it will go up 50%. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't going down 50%, so you can afford to be wrong on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Cohen: My next two names are in the health-care sector, which performed well last year, but ran into hard times toward the end of 2015. There were significant outflows in the fourth quarter from health-care mutual funds, and from many stocks, particularly pharmaceuticals. \uc2 AbbVie\uc2  [ABBV] was one. It has a yield of 4%. In the 12 months ended Friday, the stock was down 18%.\par \par \uc2 The chief concern has been the high concentration of Humira, a treatment for arthritis, in its total business model. Humira has dominated \uc2 AbbVie\uc2 's revenue, and investors are concerned that a biosimilar drug will be launched, posing competition. Our analyst believes the launch of a biosimilar probably won't happen until 2020, and, if that is the case, \uc2 AbbVie\uc2  still has good earnings growth ahead. Earnings could grow at a compound annual rate of 13% from 2015 through 2020. [Subsequent to the Roundtable, \uc2 AbbVie\uc2  had a major patent win on Humira.]\par \par \uc2 AbbVie\uc2  has some promising things in its product pipeline. It is working on a number of vaccines for HIV and hepatitis C, although the hepatitis product is off to a slow launch.\par \par \uc2 William Priest: Abby, we own \uc2 AbbVie\uc2 . It has been in a negative trend for a few weeks, and I can't explain its significant underperformance versus many other names. Maybe it is concern about potential competition to Humira.\par \par \uc2 Scott Black: The \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  has warned that the company's hepatitis C treatments could cause liver toxicity in people with cirrhosis. This is a big plus for \uc2 Gilead Sciences\uc2  [GILD], which makes the leading hepatitis C treatments. The news took down \uc2 AbbVie\uc2 's stock.\par \par \uc2 Cohen: Our second name is Mylan [MYL] which was hurt because of its failed bid last fall for \uc2 Perrigo\uc2  [PRGO]. Both are makers of generic drugs. Mylan's stock fell 13% in the past 12 months. It is trading for 9.8 times 2016 estimated earnings, below the rest of the industry.\par \par \uc2 Although investors are upset about the failed bid, more consolidation is likely in the generic-drug industry. Mylan is likely to be an acquirer, and seems to be benefiting from previous deals. The company bought some generic products from \uc2 Abbott Laboratories\uc2  [ABT], and is working them through its sales and distribution system. Also, a competitor had a problem with its own version of Mylan's EpiPen [an epinephrine auto-injector used to treat life-threatening allergic reactions], so Mylan is benefiting from that.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: How large is the EpiPen market?\par \par \uc2 Cohen: It could be as big as 24 million people, larger than Mylan previously thought. Mylan's application to sell a generic version of Copaxone [a multiple sclerosis treatment] is now before the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 ; it doesn't seem to have any problems. The company also has a generic version of Advair [an asthma treatment] on track, and has built a new manufacturing facility to produce it. Mylan has some temporary issues, but our analyst sees good value in the stock.\par \par \uc2 Next, the U.S. consumer had a tough time for a while, but is doing far better now. That brings me to \uc2 Signet Jewelers\uc2  [SIG], which operates stores in the U.S., the U.K., and Canada under a variety of names. Signet acquired \uc2 Zale\uc2  in 2014. In the U.S., Signet operates 1,400 stores under the \uc2 Kay Jewelers\uc2  and Jared the Galleria of Jewelry brands. The \uc2 Zale\uc2  division has 1,600 stores in the U.S. and Canada. The company had a good holiday season. Maybe people weren't buying a lot of coats, but they were buying jewelry.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: How has the stock done?\par \par \uc2 Cohen: In the past 12 months Signet's shares have been flat. The stock closed Friday at $126.93, and yields 0.6%. For the fiscal year ending in January 2017, our analyst has an earnings estimate of $8.07 a share, roughly in line with the consensus. The company has had some issues: It was sued for gender discrimination. But Signet is held in high esteem by customers, and has a good distribution network.\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: Are they selling upscale products, or a range of products?\par \par \uc2 Cohen: They cater to middle- and upper-middle-income buyers, and sell a lot of diamonds.\par \par \uc2 Lowe's\uc2  [LOW] also could benefit from a stronger consumer, and a strong housing market. It benefits from the renovation market, in particular. The stock was flat in the past 12 months, and yields 1.5%. We are optimistic about the housing market. Demographics favor a pickup in household formation, which will help \uc2 Lowe's\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Barron's: Why do you prefer \uc2 Lowe's\uc2  to \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  [HD], another housing beneficiary?\par \par \uc2 Cohen: \uc2 Lowe's\uc2  has a cheaper valuation. It sells for 18 times this year's expected earnings.\par \par \uc2 23 Jan 2016 00:09 ET  2016 Roundtable Part 2: Peering Into The Future -2-\par \par \uc2 Bharti Airtel\uc2 , based in India, provides mobile telecom services in India and elsewhere. It trades on the \uc2 Bombay Stock Exchange\uc2  [BHARTI.India]. The company is spending to expand its 3G and 4G telecom networks. The stock was down about 10% in the year ended Jan. 8, but our analyst, based in India, has an earnings forecast for this year that is dramatically above consensus. He expects the company to earn 19.89 rupees [29 cents] versus the consensus forecast of INR16.91. The stock is trading for 16 times expected earnings for the fiscal year ending in March 2017, and yields 1.4%.\par \par \uc2 Lastly, \uc2 Ocado Group\uc2  is an online supermarket company based in the U.K. The shares trade in London [OCDO.UK]. The stock did poorly in the past 12 months, falling 29%. The company was formed in 2000 and has invested heavily in distribution and inventory-control systems. There have been concerns that \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  [AMZN], which has launched in the U.K., will hurt them. But Amazon is based in London, which is \uc2 J Sainsbury\uc2 's [SBRY.UK] territory more than \uc2 Ocado\uc2 's.\par \par \uc2 Brian Rogers: Does the company do business as \uc2 Ocado\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Cohen: Yes. It also provides deliveries for Morrisons, a well-known supermarket chain in the U.K. \uc2 Ocado\uc2  has a lower-cost model than other supermarkets in the U.K. It has very little product waste, which keeps its costs down. Here, too, our analyst has above-consensus estimates 3.43 pence [five cents] for the fiscal year ending in November, versus the consensus estimate of 2.98p.\par \par \uc2 This isn't a new industry, but an example of an old industry trying to take advantage of some newer technologies. \uc2 Macy's\uc2  [M], which Brian recommended today [Rogers' picks were featured in last week's Roundtable issue] is also building a significant Internet platform. To the extent that old-line retailers can adapt successfully to the Internet, they should do well.\par \par \uc2 Barron's Thank you, Abby. Let's hear from Felix.\par \par \uc2 FELIX ZULAUF\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: As most of you know, I am a macro guy. I invest based on my analysis of macroeconomic trends, and try to figure out where we are in the economic and financial cycle. My base case is this: The developed economies have had a disappointing recovery since the financial crisis, but the emerging economies had a big boom until a few years ago. That boom went bust in 2012-'13. Now we are in a down cycle that will end with crisis and calamity. China's troubled economy is to the global financial market what the U.S. housing market was in the prior cycle. Many people don't understand this. While the U.S. is widely analyzed, China often is misunderstood, as the economic data published by the Chinese government are of poor quality.\par \par \uc2 In the past 12 months, China tried to reignite its economy. Debt has risen dramatically, to more than twice gross national product, with almost no impact on the real economy. China has reported annual growth of nearly 7%, but the industrial complex is in a recession, and I estimate the service sector is growing by only 4% a year. In reality, then, growth is probably around 2%, and slowing.\par \par \uc2 Priest: Doesn't China have a huge percentage of the world's debt?\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: It does. China has doubled its debt in a few years' time. In boom times, the corporate sector in China and other emerging markets made all sorts of mistakes. Companies overexpanded, took on too much debt and staff, and raised labor costs excessively. Officially, Chinese corporations have issued $1 trillion in dollar-denominated debt. Unofficially, it could be as high as $3 trillion.\par \par \uc2 When wealth is created during a boom, there is no need to diversify into other economies. Once the boom is over, however, the wealthy want to diversify outside the country. By looking at what happened in other economies when boom times ended, we can estimate that about $3 trillion in wealth wants to leave China. That is a huge amount, and outflows have been increasing.\par \par \uc2 Most economists and strategists think China is growing by 5% or 6%. They look at the country's current-account surplus of $300 billion and think everything is fine. But China is running a capital account deficit of $1 trillion. It doesn't publish that number, although you can figure it out.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: How did you figure it out?\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell to $3.3 trillion last year from $4 trillion. The current-account surplus is $300 billion, so that means $700 billion has flowed out of the country on a net basis. The gross outflow is about $1 trillion. If we assume $1.3 trillion of China's reserves are illiquid, only $2 trillion remains. If money continues to leave the country at the current rate, China could lose all its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves in a little more than a year.\par \par \uc2 To prevent that from happening, \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  eventually will stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to prop up the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, and let it fall. The government has other options: It could raise interest rates, which would be dreadful for the Chinese economy, or tighten capital controls, which would postpone the problem of capital flight, but cause bigger problems in the future. That is why China will have to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall. China has a balance-of-payments crisis, which usually ends with a recession in the real economy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  overshoots on the downside, and interest rates rise to extreme levels. That is what lies ahead.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: That is some base case. What are the implications for the rest of the world?\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: China's Asian trading partners will also let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall. It is conceivable that Singapore, which has attracted a lot of foreign capital over the years because of its image as a strong-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  state, will be extremely exposed to the situation in China. Singapore's banking-sector loans have grown dramatically in the past five or six years. Singapore is now losing capital, which means the banking industry is losing deposits. When that happens, carry trades go awry, which is usually a prescription for disaster. I expect a banking crisis to develop in Singapore and to spread eventually to Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: What will happen to the Hong Kong dollar?\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: \uc2 The Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it will defend the Hong Kong dollar. During the first stage of the crisis, capital flowed from mainland China to Hong Kong, which depressed interest rates in Hong Kong. In the past few days, the reverse has happened; capital has flowed out of Hong Kong and shorter rates [interest rates on shorter-term government debt] have gone up. The Hong Kong dollar is pegged to the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Rising interest rates will be deadly for the Hong Kong stock market and real-estate market. Eventually, as their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lose value, China and the emerging-market countries will reduce their imports, which will exert a contractionary force on the world economy. They will try to export more by cutting prices, which will influence the pricing mechanism throughout the industrialized world.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: Are you talking here about a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war?\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: Some call it a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war.\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: Government-owned enterprises in China are already dumping steel.\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: In Asia, as well as the U.S., there has been a large buildup of inventories, and inventory levels are too high. The corporate sector in both regions overestimated final demand. Now it has to cut production. Companies also overestimated their debt-carrying ability. Remember that cutting costs is cutting someone else's revenue. Thus, the global economy will continue to weaken.\par \par \uc2 Investors are pretty fully invested in equities around the world, because equities have been the only game in town. The slogan was TINA: There is no alternative to stocks. Among U.S. households and institutional investors, equity allocations are approaching the 2000 and 2007 peaks. Investors are extended and vulnerable, and don't have a lot of cash.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the monetary backdrop is changing. The \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  hiked interest rates in December, and at the margin is shrinking its balance sheet. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  has shrunk its balance sheet dramatically because it is losing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. The \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't engage in further quantitative easing [buying assets to drive down interest rates] beyond its current round, which means QE is finished as a policy tool for now. [\uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2 , president of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , suggested Thursday that \uc2 the ECB\uc2  might ease monetary policy further in March.] The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  will continue with QE reluctantly, but is unhappy with the government of Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  because it isn't delivering on reforms. The BOJ will increase QE when bond yields rise, buying more bonds to push yields down. In sum, the big monetary push we have seen in years past is over. Global liquidity is deteriorating. To complete the picture, the geopolitical backdrop will deteriorate further.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: Is there any hope for investors under your scenario?\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: Investors should stay as defensive as possible. If you have to stay invested, stick with sectors such as consumer staples and utilities. My first recommendation is in money markets. I don't expect the Fed to hike rates further this year. Instead, Fed officials will bring their dots down. [Members of the Fed's policy-making committee publish their expectations for the federal- funds rate on a chart called the dot plot.] The Eurodollar futures market is predicting two more rate hikes. They won't be executed because the world economy will turn much softer. The U.S. dollar remains too strong versus emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It doesn't make sense to hike rates and make the dollar even stronger.\par \par \uc2 23 Jan 2016 00:09 ET  2016 Roundtable Part 2: Peering Into The Future -3-\par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy will surprise on the downside. I recommend buying December 2016 Eurodollar futures. [Eurodollars are timed dollar-denominated deposits held outside the U.S. The futures contracts are traded on the \uc2 CME\uc2 .] Friday's closing price was $98.94. The implied yield is 1.06%, or 100 minus the price. The actual yield is 0.62%, which means a further rate increase of 44 basis points [hundredths of a percentage point] is priced in. That won't happen. If the Fed hikes one more time by 25 basis points, you would still earn 19 basis points on the contract, or 44 minus 25. The notional size of the contract is $1 million, and the term is three months. If you buy one contract and earn 44 basis points, that's $1,100. You can buy 100 or 1,000 contracts, and make your money that way.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: How would you lose money on this?\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: You would lose money if the Fed hikes rates by 25 basis points more than twice.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: Do you buy the futures or options on the futures?\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: We buy both, but I like the futures. In equities, I would short the EEM, or iShares MSCI Emerging Markets exchange-traded fund. [Short sellers sell borrowed shares in the expectation of repurchasing them later at a lower price.]\par \par \uc2 I recommended this in the June Midyear Roundtable, as well ["2015 Midyear Roundtable Top Stock Picks," June 15, 2015]. The ETF is selling for $29.50. The 2008 low was $18.24. I expect it to hit that low again.\par \par \uc2 Second, I recommend shorting S&P 500 futures. The U.S. corporate sector has a relatively weak balance sheet. Companies have loaded up on debt. The stock market is vulnerable, and prices could go much lower. The average bear-market decline is 22%-23% for U.S. indexes. I am prepared for a decline of more than 20%. People will be surprised by how low the market goes.\par \par \uc2 Cohen: What is the timing of your projected decline?\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: It started last spring with a stealth bear market. The first leg down occurred last summer, and a recovery attempt failed. Now the second leg down has begun. There might be a bounce, but then the selling will resume. I expect three down legs from last year's highs. I am not saying the S&P 500 will fall 50%, but 1600 is my minimum downside target, and I expect the market to hit it this year. This is a global affair; European markets will fall, as well. I would short the weakest market in Europe Spain, via IBEX 35 index futures. Spanish companies have a lot of exposure to Latin America, Brazil in particular, and Spanish banks will suffer badly.\par \par \uc2 I mentioned the potential for a banking crisis in Singapore. I don't recommend shorting Singapore bank stocks, but rather the EWS, or iShares MSCI Singapore ETF. In this case, an investor will benefit from both declining local stock prices and a decline in the Singapore dollar against the U.S. dollar. Finally, in Europe, I recommend shorting the German DAX index and the Euro Stoxx 50, both via futures. My minimum downside target for the DAX is 7500.\par \par \uc2 Schafer: Where is the DAX trading now?\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: It is around 9000. I also would short emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I would buy the dollar against the CNH, the Chinese yuan offshore. The yuan offshore is priced at about $6.60, and could shoot up to $8.\par \par \uc2 Cohen: What is your time frame for that?\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: I expect it to happen this year. This will be an extremely bad year for investors. Hopefully, it will yield many investment opportunities in 2017. You will hear me sounding bullish then.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: We don't believe you.\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: I would buy the U.S. dollar against the Singapore dollar, which is trading for $1.43-$1.44. I would also buy the dollar against the Korean won and Taiwan dollar. South Korea is the most highly leveraged industrial economy in Asia.\par \par \uc2 Schafer: Where does gold fit into your investment portfolio?\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: Gold will be a tremendous trade at some point this year, but we are in a deflationary environment, which isn't good for gold. When systemic risk thrives, gold could become an important investment, but gold isn't entering a new bull-market cycle. The price could break below $1,000 an ounce first, and then run to $1,400, so I don't want to recommend it today. You'll have to time your entry during the year.\par \par \uc2 The same is true for bonds. There will be a safe-haven run into U.S. Treasuries, pushing the yield on the 30-year \uc2 Treasury\uc2  bond down to 2.50% or so from 2.91%. Then yields will rise again, as the markets assume the authorities will expand the deficit once the economy turns sour. Governments will try to support the system, but we need a calamity to occur first.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: Thanks for your thoughts, Felix. Mario, you're next.\par \par \uc2 MARIO GABELLI\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: We have talked today about the impact of China's weakening economy on the commodities market, and how problems in the industrial sector will hurt \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500 earnings. I am going to discuss something unrelated: a company I have followed for some time that has grown more exciting [puts on \uc2 New York Knicks\uc2  cap]. I am referring to Madison Square Garden [MSG], which owns the greatest sports arena in the world Madison Square Garden, in New York. The company has 25 million shares outstanding, selling at $154 each, for a market capitalization of $3.8 billion. It has $1.5 billion of net cash, and has committed to buying back $500 million worth of stock.\par \par \uc2 Madison Square Garden owns several sports franchises, including the \uc2 New York Knicks\uc2 , \uc2 New York Rangers\uc2 , and New York \uc2 Liberty\uc2  women's basketball team. As a group, its sports franchises could be worth $1.7 billion. In addition to the Garden, the company owns properties such as the Forum in L.A., and the Chicago Theatre. It leases Radio City Music Hall and the Beacon Theatre in New York.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: And it is a real-estate play.\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: That's right. From Manhattan's West 34th Street south along the High Line, there has been a construction renaissance. There have been many discussions about renovating Penn Station. The air rights over Madison Square Garden, which sits atop Penn Station, could be worth as much as $500 million, and the company is starting to look at ways to monetize that asset. The combination of cash flow, asset values, and stock buybacks allows me to put a value on the stock of $200 to $300 per share. Another way to look at it is, if you buy the stock, you get the \uc2 Knicks\uc2  for free.\par \par \uc2 Through its 4.5 million B shares, the Dolan family controls the company. There are a lot of ways to make money here by monetizing the company's assets.\par \par \uc2 Next, \uc2 Griffon\uc2  [GFF] is a small-cap stock. The company has 48 million shares. The stock sells for $16.60. \uc2 Griffon\uc2  is involved in several industries. Through its home and building-products division, the company is involved in the U.S. garage-door business. It is a $1.8 billion market, and \uc2 Griffon\uc2 's Clopay unit has a 25% market share. It competes with Overhead Door, and others. It sells mostly through \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 . This is a good business, and it is improving. The company has added a few product lines in its home division, including True Temper garden and snow-removal tools.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: What are \uc2 Griffon\uc2 's other businesses?\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: The company is a component supplier for diapers. \uc2 Procter & Gamble\uc2  [PG] accounts for half of \uc2 Griffon\uc2 's diaper business, and is gaining share. \uc2 Griffon\uc2 's costs have fallen as resin prices have come down, and these cost savings are passed on to customers. But, on balance, this too is a good business for the company.\par \par \uc2 Griffon\uc2  also develops and sells equipment for the military through its Telephonics unit. It makes submarine-monitoring hardware for helicopters, and is a first-tier supplier to \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2  [LMT]. Military spending will increase in 2016, '17, and '18, given China's more aggressive posture in the South China Sea, North Korea's reported test of a hydrogen bomb, and the events in the Middle East. The U.S. has underspent on its military, and we'll have a new president next year, which means spending will rise.\par \par \uc2 Here are the numbers. \uc2 Griffon\uc2  generated revenue of about $2 billion for the fiscal year ended Sept. 30. Ebitda, or earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation, and amortization, was about $171 million. In the current fiscal year revenue could rise to $2.05 billion, and Ebitda, to $180 million. \uc2 Griffon\uc2  earned 73 cents a share in its latest fiscal year. It could earn 85 cents a share this year, and $1 in fiscal 2017. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  owns more than five million shares.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: Is there any chance \uc2 Griffon\uc2  might break itself up or spin off businesses?\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: We would prefer that they don't break things up. We'd like management to focus on using the company's cash flow to grow the businesses it is in, and make a few tuck-in acquisitions. To me, \uc2 Griffon\uc2  is in the right businesses.\par \par \uc2 Kaman\uc2  [KAMN], based in Connecticut, ties into two themes: higher military spending, and the struggles of the energy, metals, and mining industries. \uc2 Kaman\uc2  has about 30 million fully diluted shares. The stock is trading for $38. Pro forma, with two acquisitions, net debt is $435 million. \uc2 Kaman\uc2  acquired Timken Alcor Aerospace Technologies from \uc2 Timken\uc2  [TKR] for $45 million.\par \par \uc2 Kaman\uc2  sells JPFs, or joint programmable fuzes, used to program precision-guided bombs aboard military planes. The \uc2 U.S. Air Force\uc2  has placed orders for \uc2 Kaman\uc2 's JPFs, as have other nations. One small but important concern is that the \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  just awarded a fuze contract to another company, which will be phased in starting in 2021.\par \par \uc2 Meryl Witmer: Which company is that?\par \par \uc2 23 Jan 2016 00:09 ET  2016 Roundtable Part 2: Peering Into The Future -4-\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: It's \uc2 Orbital ATK\uc2  [OA]. \uc2 Kaman\uc2  also is a subcontractor to \uc2 Lockheed Martin\uc2  for cockpits for the Black Hawk helicopter. It has an aerospace-structures business. But the crown jewel is a business that manufactures tapered rolled bearings for commercial aircraft. As the ramp rate rises on new planes from \uc2 Boeing\uc2  [BA] and \uc2 Airbus Group\uc2  [AIR.France], \uc2 Kaman\uc2  benefits. This business has been particularly strong. Including recent acquisitions, bearings revenue could reach about $300 million this year, and $320 million in 2017. Ebitda could rise from $105 million in 2016 to $120 million in 2017. The JPF business is operating at capacity. Growth can be accommodated by machinery and labor.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: What is happening on the industrial side of the company?\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: That business faces challenges. \uc2 Kaman\uc2  generates about $1.2 billion in annual revenue from its distribution segment, which mainly includes the sale of MRO [maintenance, repair, and overhaul] supplies. About 20% of distribution revenue is tied to EMM energy, mining, and metals. The company acquired a business with \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Pennsylvania, near the Marcellus Shale. That has become an air pocket: Revenue in the distribution segment declined at an accelerating rate in the fourth quarter, as energy prices fell, and will probably continue deteriorating.\par \par \uc2 Kaman\uc2  generates a lot of cash. It is reasonably well-managed, and the defense side of the company will get more traction in coming years. Charles "Chuck" \uc2 Kaman\uc2  founded the company in the 1940s. As an aside, the first time I visited his office, the guy's dog, Otto, came over and put his head on my lap. I didn't ask any aggressive questions after that.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: Chuck Kaman was a smart man.\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: I expect \uc2 Kaman\uc2  to earn $2.90 a share this year and $3.10 in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Auto-parts stocks were a tale of two cities last year. \uc2 O'Reilly Automotive\uc2  [ORLY] was up 31%. \uc2 Pep Boys Manny, Moe & Jack\uc2  [PBY] was up 87%. \uc2 Genuine Parts\uc2  [GPC] was down about 20%. \uc2 Genuine Parts\uc2  is trading for $78 and has 150.8 million shares. The company has raised the dividend annually for the past 59 years. It has four operating segments. The automotive business, conducted under the Napa name, contributed $8 billion out of a total $15 billion in 2015 revenue. We expect revenue of closer to $16 billion this year and $16.5 billion in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: Why has the stock underperformed peers?\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: About 25% of automotive revenue comes from Canada, Australia, New Zealand, and Mexico. Revenue was up nicely in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year, but down significantly when translated into dollars. At some point, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  head wind will flatten out and the negative impact will disappear.\par \par \uc2 Genuine Parts\uc2  also has an MRO business, which sells repair parts. It competes with \uc2 Fastenal\uc2  [FAST] and \uc2 W.W. Grainger\uc2  [GWW]. Annual revenue for this business is about $4.5 billion. It has been declining sequentially in the past few months, but the business is OK. I expect \uc2 Genuine Parts\uc2  to earn $4.50 a share for 2015, $5 this year, and $5.50 next year. The company pays an annual dividend of $2.46 a share, which it is likely to raise again this year.\par \par \uc2 Shifting gears [everyone groans], people are uncomfortable with the outlook for emerging markets, but I like Kinnevik [KINVB.Sweden], a Swedish company that has several emerging-market subsidiaries. I recommended it last January. If you give $1 billion to a private-equity fund, they take fees and 20% of profits, and you have no liquidity. In Kinnevik's case, you can invest in a basket of companies selling at a discount to net asset value; management doesn't take two-and-20 [2% in fees and 20% of profits], and you can get your money out.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: What is the stock price?\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: Kinnevik has 277 million A- and B-class shares. The stock was priced at 238 Swedish kronor [$28] last Friday. Kinnevik has investments in e-commerce \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The business I am focused on is \uc2 Millicom International Cellular\uc2  [MIICF].\par \par \uc2 Millicom has 60 million wireless customers in Africa and Latin America. In Africa, it is cash break-even. At some point, Millicom will be able to monetize its African business. The Latin America business operates in Costa Rica, Honduras, Guatemala, El Salvador, Bolivia, Colombia, and Paraguay. The company already has sold off its Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Barron's: How will it monetize the Latin American portion?\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: \uc2 John Malone\uc2  [chairman of Liberty Media/LMCA] recently merged \uc2 Columbus International\uc2 , a telecom provider in the Caribbean and Central and South America, into \uc2 Cable & Wireless Communications\uc2  [CWC.UK], in which he has a stake. Last summer \uc2 Liberty Global\uc2  [LBTYA], which he controls, created a tracking stock called Liberty LiLAC Group [LILAK] to track its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Latin America and the Caribbean. Next, Malone is likely to use Liberty LiLAC to further consolidate \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in that part of the world. Millicom is likely to be sold at the right price. The current CEO of Millicom previously ran the Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Liberty LiLAC.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: What could Millicom be worth in a break-up and sale?\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: Let me put it this way: If I were running Millicom, I would first sell off the African business for a couple of billion dollars. It has about $200 million in annual Ebitda. Then I would consolidate Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Malone's. This is going to be a big winner. Here's the math: Millicom has 100 million shares. The U.S. shares are trading for $52. The company could generate $7 billion in revenue this year, and $2.3 billion in Ebitda. Next year revenue could hit $7.4 billion, and Ebitda, $2.4 billion. Annual capex is $1.2 billion, so the business is cash-flow positive.\par \par \uc2 Next, \uc2 CBS\uc2  [\uc2 CBS\uc2 ] has 38 million class A shares, of which Sumner Redstone's National Amusements owns 30 million. There are eight million in public hands. Our clients own about four million of the A shares. The A shares are selling around $50, and the B shares, at $46. There are 474 million shares in all. \uc2 CBS\uc2  has an equity-market capitalization of $22 billion; $8 billion of debt, and an enterprise value of $30 billion. The company operates television stations and produces content for TV.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2  had $13.8 billion in revenue last year, going up to $14.6 billion this year. Election-related and Super Bowl 50 advertising will bolster revenue in 2016. Ebitda was $3.1 billion, and could rise to $3.4 billion. The company might be harvesting assets through the spectrum auction later this year. The prior spectrum auction generated proceeds of more than $40 billion, although this auction won't generate close to that, due to buyer exhaustion.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: What is \uc2 CBS\uc2  likely to generate in earnings?\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: The company earned $3.30 a share last year, and could earn $3.90 this year and $4.25 next year. The stock is selling for 11 times next year's estimate. The broadcasting business accounts for almost $9 billion of revenue. On the programming side, I like Ray Donovan better than Downton Abbey, and I like The Walking Dead.\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: That's a lot of TV.\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: In addition to big sources of advertising and the spectrum sale, there is \uc2 CBS\uc2  All Access, the company's "over-the-top" or streaming service.\par \par \uc2 Black: The stock is cheap statistically, but [CBS CEO] \uc2 Leslie Moonves\uc2  makes north of $50 million a year and hasn't delivered much top-line growth.\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: Come on, hedge-fund managers make a lot of money. This guy [points to Gundlach, sitting to his right] runs $63 billion in fixed-income portfolios, and makes a lot.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: Give us the range of outcomes at \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: \uc2 Redstone\uc2  is 92. When he passes from the scene, will his family hang on to \uc2 Viacom\uc2  [VIAB], which he also controls, and sell \uc2 CBS\uc2 ? A lot of organizations and individuals might like \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Lastly, \uc2 Discovery Communications\uc2  [DISCA] has 645 million shares outstanding: 223 million A shares have one vote, seven million B shares have 10 votes, and 415 million C shares don't get a vote. Malone is involved with this company, too; he owns about six million of the B shares. Discovery has $7.3 billion of debt, and a $25 stock price. It has a market cap of $16 billion and an enterprise value of $23.7 billion. The company's various cable-TV channels generated $6.4 billion of revenue in the latest year, about half outside the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Revenue could climb to $6.8 billion this year and $7.2 billion next year. We forecast Ebitda of $2.6 billion for 2016, and $2.9 billion for 2017. The company earned $1.80 last year. It could earn $2.15 this year and $2.55 next year. Neither \uc2 CBS\uc2  nor Discovery has much capex. They are great cash generators. The big issue for Discovery is how to migrate its content to global platforms, including smartphones. The CEO, \uc2 David Zaslav\uc2 , is terrific. Discovery won the non-U.S. broadcast rights for the Olympic Games from 2018 through 2024. That's it.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: In that case, thank you. Jeffrey, it's your turn at bat.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY GUNDLACH\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: Felix laid out a lot of facts that I have also been observing. I agree that the Fed won't be able to raise interest rates according to its dots, particularly if you follow the dot plot to the end of 2017. It suggests the Fed will hike rates eight times, by 200 basis points in all. Based on the deterioration in stock prices, commodities, and junk bonds, I have been waiting for the Fed to start dialing back its rate-hike rhetoric. We got a little of that today, when \uc2 Dennis Lockhart\uc2  [president of the Federal Reserve Bank of Atlanta] said the Fed might not have enough data to move rates up in March.\par \par \uc2 23 Jan 2016 00:10 ET  2016 Roundtable Part 2: Peering Into The Future -5-\par \par \uc2 According to some fixed-income managers, the step-function decline in junk-bond prices has created an overwhelmingly attractive opportunity. At DoubleLine, we are not of that opinion. While junk bonds tied to the commodities complex are due for a rally, it will be a rally within a correction. Like Felix, we believe China is growing at a much slower rate than the government claims and people want to believe. The \uc2 World Bank\uc2  recently dropped its global growth estimate for 2016 to 2.9% from 3.3%. Since China is 16% of the world economy, global growth might be only 2%.\par \par \uc2 In this environment, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation becomes the only tool to spur growth for many countries experiencing negative GDP. In Brazil, the largest economy in South America, GDP growth is negative 4%, even if the country's finance minister says it is negative 2%. Again, like Felix, I recommend shorting the iShares Emerging Markets ETF.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: We allow doubles.\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: The correlation between the emerging-markets ETF and commodity prices is incredibly high, and the gap today between commodity prices and the ETF price is remarkably wide. This suggests further downside in emerging-market stocks. The \uc2 MSCI\uc2  Emerging Markets Index, which the ETF tracks, is now around 700. If commodities prices stay at current levels, history suggests the index should be around 450. It would probably overshoot to the downside, so you could be looking at a 40%, or possibly 50% decline.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: How much worse could things get in the junk-bond market?\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: I expect it to remain challenged. It would require a big rally in commodities prices to avoid a debt-default cycle. However, a portion of the credit market has a safety cushion large enough to absorb another 200- or 300-basis-point widening in junk-bond spreads versus Treasuries. I'm referring to closed-end bond funds, which trade on the \uc2 New York Stock Exchange\uc2 . Closed-ends are one of the best plays on the Fed not raising interest rates. I like them better than the Eurodollar futures idea Felix discussed.\par \par \uc2 Closed-end funds are leveraged, and investors have been afraid to own them because they fear that the Fed has launched a tightening cycle. Also, based on daily data going back 20 years, they have traded at a 2% discount, on average, to net asset value. Recently, however, the sector traded at a 10% to 12% discount to NAV. It has traded at such a steep discount only 5% of the time. In the past 20 years, the discount has been wider than that only during the financial crisis in 2008-'09.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: Rogers: Are you referring only to fixed-income closed-ends?\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: Yes. If history is any guide, discounts would widen further only in a 2008-type scenario, which is possible, although doubtful so soon after the prior crisis. Under current circumstances, you have about two percentage points of downside, and 10 points of upside to return to the historical discount. That makes a basket of closed-ends attractive. If you bought a junk-bond-oriented closed-end trading at a 12% discount to NAV, some of the bonds would be trading at a 15% discount. This isn't a bad idea, but I prefer Brookfield Total Return fund [HTR]. It is trading just as poorly as some other closed-ends, but is vastly safer.\par \par \uc2 Brookfield's NAV is $25.75 a share. It is trading at $21.77, for about a 12% discount to NAV. It pays a monthly dividend of 19 cents, which it hasn't changed for more than seven years. While investors have been crushing anything that is interest rate-exposed, there is also little ability in the closed-end fund market for institutional investors to arbitrage the discounts. For example, at DoubleLine, we manage $85 billion. You thought it was $65, huh? $85, bro.\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: I said $63. My estimates are always low.\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: There is no way I could buy these institutionally without the discounts instantly disappearing, because the volume is so low, relative to the amount I would have to buy to make a difference on DoubleLine's $85 billion in assets. Closed-ends are a good opportunity for the retail investor.\par \par \uc2 Brookfield Total Return yields 10.47%. It is leveraged 35%; hence, the worry about interest-rate risk. Junk bonds and commodities are Ground Zero of the bond market's and the economy's problem, but only 8% of this fund's portfolio is corporate credits. Another 2% is preferred stocks, and the rest is mortgage-backed securities. MBS are immune to the problem of lower commodities prices. Lower oil prices actually help homeowners put extra cash in their pockets. That makes it more likely they will make mortgage payments.\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: Having seen The Big Short, I'm nervous about MBS.\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: Trust me, there aren't a lot of MBS from North Dakota [a major shale-oil region]. The MBS are non-government-guaranteed, so defaults could be an issue. Yet with housing prices having risen for four years and consumers getting a break on oil prices, the fundamentals are favorable. While I like HTR, an investor could put together a basket of closed-ends yielding 10% and 12% and selling at a 10% to 15% discount to NAV.\par \par \uc2 Priest: With respect to closed-end funds, if you assume that the management fee is a perpetual claim on net asset value, the present value of that stream of future claims will almost always lead to a market price below the funds' NAV. Historically, that discount can vary, but 8% to 10% is not uncommon.\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: Your argument makes mathematical sense, but historically that discount has been 2%. A reversion to the mean isn't unheard of; based on recent discounts, it would amount to a 10% gain in the price.\par \par \uc2 Witmer: With the yield, the return would be 20%.\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: I was getting to that. The S&P 500 yields 2%. Either the credit market has to improve, or stocks have to fall further, because there has been a yawning gap in the performance of these markets in recent months. If the S&P rises 10%, closed-ends could return 20%. If the stock market falls 30%, a decline is already priced into these funds. I look at closed-end funds as a good place to put your risk money.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: What is the best way to assemble a basket of quality closed-ends?\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: You have to decide which sectors you are interested in, and look up the discounts to NAV because they aren't homogenous. [Barron's publishes weekly data on closed-end funds; see the Market Data section of Barrons.com.]\par \par \uc2 Priest: In my prior life, we ran many closed-end funds. We decided to adopt a novel policy: Charge investors based on market value, rather than the net asset value. Yet the discount to net asset value didn't change at all.\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: I'm not surprised. Closed-end funds aren't well understood.\par \par \uc2 Priest: It seems logical to invest in liquid fixed-income closed-ends, rather than those equity closed-ends with a preponderance of illiquid private placements, where values aren't determined by the market or an independent third party. Moreover, for some funds, frequent rebalancing of the portfolio may cause large capital gains. Low-turnover equity closed-end funds make sense.\par \par \uc2 Witmer: Who lends closed-ends money?\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: A fund manager can establish a bank line of credit or do a reverse repo [reverse repurchase agreement].\par \par \uc2 Black: What happens to the mortgage portfolio if interest rates rise?\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: It is a bond portfolio, so it will go down. But priced at a 12% discount to NAV, it is trading at what it would be worth if rates were 150 basis points higher. That's the cushion of safety. You have to ask yourself, in what context would rates rise? The economy would be doing OK, and the backdrop would be less deflationary. The discount would absorb some of the impact of higher rates. The discount is the secret sauce here. If you can acquire a $100 bill for $85, I recommend that you do so, assuming you consider a $100 bill attractive to begin with.\par \par \uc2 It wasn't so long ago that people would do just about anything to get a 7% yield. Those who bought master limited partnerships lost a lot of money. Now there is plenty of yield out there, and people say they're afraid of the Fed. Fears that the Fed will raise rates significantly are overblown. This brings me to \uc2 Annaly Capital Management\uc2  [NLY], one of the largest mortgage REITs [real-estate investment trust]. It has an $8 billion market cap and has been trading at a 25% discount to book value for some time. It is probably riskier than HTR because it is leveraged about six times, so there is significant exposure to borrowing costs that could rise over time.\par \par \uc2 Schafer: What is the stock price?\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: It is selling for $9.41. A few years back, it sold for $18. These sorts of stocks have step-function moves. They don't move by a few percent; they go from $18 to $12 and from $12 to $9, and if the yield curve is inverted, and they have to cut their dividends, things get really bad. But a discount of 30% to book value is the widest ever for Annaly, and historically very wide for a mortgage REIT. Annaly is paying a dividend of 30 cents per quarter. It yields 12.75%. The environment for Annaly has improved.\par \par \uc2 Schafer: Didn't Annaly cut its dividend?\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: Management cut it in 2013, but it has been at this level for a while. When rates rose sharply during the "taper tantrum" in 2013, after the Fed hinted it was going to reduce asset purchases, Annaly appropriately got scared and reduced its leverage. When you reduce leverage, you reduce your earnings power, and then you have to cut your dividend. Annaly primarily owns government-guaranteed mortgages and tries to hedge the interest-rate risk. This is challenging, but they survived the credit crisis by doing so. You have to give them credit for that. The discount to net asset value got as wide as 30% back then, in a far more challenging environment. At today's discount, a lot of bad things are priced in. If the Fed doesn't raise interest rates much, the stock should go higher.\par \par \uc2 Black: At Annaly, there used to be a mismatch between funding the assets and liabilities.\par \par \uc2 23 Jan 2016 00:10 ET  2016 Roundtable Part 2: Peering Into The Future -6-\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: That is always a challenge. They are buying mortgages with an instability aspect to them, as they don't know exactly when the mortgage holders will pay them off. The hedges are largely \uc2 Treasury\uc2 - or swaps-based [interest-rate swaps], and they don't have the same instability. That is why these stocks can be volatile. But there are a lot of ways for this to go right, and if it doesn't, you'll just have to cry your way to the bank, clipping a 12.75% yield.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: Why buy Annaly, as opposed to another mortgage REIT?\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: It is highly liquid. I have even been able to buy some shares in our institutional business. Also, I know the company and have talked to management several times.\par \par \uc2 A third way of getting income is pretty risky, but I like it a lot. I'm talking about Puerto Rico GOs [general-obligation bonds] the 8% GOs due in 2035. The general-obligation bonds have the highest constitutional protections, which means borrowers must be paid first, ahead of other commonwealth debt holders. Puerto Rico has shown a willingness to default on some of its debt, but defaulting on this would be tough.\par \par \uc2 My thesis for owning Puerto Rico debt has a lot to do with Florida. There are many voters in Florida with ties to Puerto Rico, and Florida is a critical state to win in the presidential election. I started buying the bonds around 80, and bought more at 71. The bonds are triple tax-exempt, meaning interest payments to U.S. citizens aren't taxed at the federal, state or local level. In a high-tax state such as California, an 11% or 12% yield is equivalent to 23%, taxed. Maybe I won't get back 100 cents on the dollar. Maybe the government will let Puerto Rico go. But President Obama spoke in the Puerto Rico statehouse with the U.S. flag sewn together with the flag of Puerto Rico. It doesn't guarantee anything, but it is a reason to take a flyer. Even if the debt is restructured, you might get 70 cents on the dollar, and you're getting eight points of coupon per year. This particular issue is a few billion dollars.\par \par \uc2 Gabelli: If you were running the country, what would you do to help Puerto Rico?\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: I would extend them the ability to file for bankruptcy protection, and restructure what they can. I might extend them some sort of lifeline economically. I am not advocating this, and I don't care what shape a restructuring takes. But at 73 cents on the dollar, you're going to make decent money, particularly if you're a taxable investor. It is a speculative investment.\par \par \uc2 Next, investors often use diversification to justify buying things that are up a lot, but bond investors who haven't diversified beyond the U.S. might consider doing so. The DoubleLine Core Fixed Income fund [DBLFX] was 100% dollar-denominated since 2011, when the dollar was at a low. For the first time ever last week, I started to buy a diversified basket of non-dollar-denominated fixed-income assets of developed countries. The dollar is overvalued. It rose 25% in a year. People think a stronger dollar accompanies the start of a Fed tightening cycle, but history suggests otherwise. It is possible, even likely, that the Fed wants to dial back some of its rate-hike rhetoric. If that is the case, the dollar could weaken about 10% against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Schafer: Being long the dollar is a crowded trade.\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: Everyone is on one side of the boat, and they justify it by saying the Fed is going to tighten.\par \par \uc2 Zulauf: So you are buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of other industrialized economies?\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: Yes even the Canadian dollar, which looks like death because of the drop in oil prices. We are overdue for a commodity rally.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: What is the best way to execute this investment?\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: BWX [SPDR Barclays International Treasury Bonds ETF] would do the job. One reason \uc2 Treasury\uc2  yields bottomed in July 2012 was because Europeans were buying dollars like crazy. They did that by buying bonds. You had a monstrous move in the U.S. bond market that took the 10-year \uc2 Treasury\uc2  yield down to 1.38%. Now it is nearly 100 basis points higher. I am advocating diversification into developed-country \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Finally, while I regard U.S. equities as a much better bet than emerging markets, I recommend buying the India stock market through the iPath MSCI India exchange-traded note [INP]. Indian equities could have a hard time as emerging markets sell off, but the labor force is growing. India will be the China of the next generation, in terms of equity returns. Things are bad. India has a lot of corruption. That means things might get better.\par \par \uc2 Barron's: You made a high-profile negative call on Apple [AAPL] a few years ago. What are your thoughts now?\par \par \uc2 Gundlach: I recommended shorting Apple in April 2012 at $600 a share [prior to the company's 7-for-1 stock split]. The stock rose to $700. That's when everyone mocks you. But I insisted it would fall below $425. It did, and we bought it at $405. Then we sold it too soon. I don't have a position or a strong view now. The stock isn't expensive, but I have an allergy to high-market-cap stocks in general, because I don't understand how they can compound when they are already so big. [Apple's market cap is $533.9 billion.]\par \par \uc2 Barron's: Fair enough. Thanks for joining us, Jeffrey.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 2016 Roundtable Panelists\par \par \uc2 SCOTT BLACK\par \par \uc2 Founder and president,\par \par \uc2 Delphi Management,\par \par \uc2 Boston\par \par \uc2 ABBY JOSEPH COHEN\par \par \uc2 Senior Investment Strategist\par \par \uc2 and President,\par \par \uc2 Global Markets Institute,\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , New York\par \par \uc2 MARIO GABELLI\par \par \uc2 Chairman and CEO,\par \par \uc2 Gamco Investors\uc2 ,\par \par \uc2 Rye, N.Y.\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY GUNDLACH\par \par \uc2 Co-founder and CEO\par \par \uc2 Doubleline Capital\par \par \uc2 Los Angeles\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM PRIEST\par \par \uc2 CEO and co-CIO\par \par \uc2 Epoch Investment Partners\par \par \uc2 New York\par \par \uc2 BRIAN ROGERS\par \par \uc2 Chairman, T. Rowe Price\par \par \uc2 Baltimore\par \par \uc2 OSCAR SCHAFER\par \par \uc2 Chairman,\par \par \uc2 Rivulet Capital\par \par \uc2 New York\par \par \uc2 MERYL WITMER\par \par \uc2 General Partner,\par \par \uc2 Eagle Value Partners\par \par \uc2 New York\par \par \uc2 FELIX ZULAUF\par \par \uc2 President,\par \par \uc2 Zulauf Asset Management\par \par \uc2 Co-CIO and Partner,\par \par \uc2 Vicenda Asset Management,\par \par \uc2 Zug, Switzerland\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 To subscribe to Barron's, visit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.barrons.com/subscribe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.barrons.com/subscribe}}}\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 23, 2016 00:10 ET (05:10 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160123ec1n0002e\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Canadian Stocks Are Rallying As Oil Prices Surge -- Canadian Commentary\b0\par\par\uc2 1,527 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2016\par \uc2 RTT News\par \uc2 RTTNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2016 NoticiasFinancieras. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (RTTNews) - The Canadian stock market is up sharply in early trade Friday, adding to the gains of the previous session. Oil prices began to stabilize yesterday and have surged back to over $31 a barrel this morning. Today's rally is broad based, with strong gains in energy, mining and industrial stocks. Gold is the only sector lagging behind this morning, as investors exit safe havens.\par \par \uc2 The global rally in equity markets began with strong gains in Asia overnight. China's Vice President \uc2 Li Yuanchao\uc2  said on Thursday that market forces will guide the movement of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market to avoid excessive volatility.\par \par \uc2 Markets in Europe are surging Friday, adding to yesterday's gains. European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  said on Thursday that there were no limits on what policy tools the bank can deploy to achieve its inflation goal and to boost euro area growth, and hinted that more stimulus measures may come in March as the downside risks such as global uncertainty, market volatility and geopolitics have increased.\par \par \uc2 Markets in the United States are also solidly in the green this morning. The surge in crude oil prices coupled with all the various \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hints of further stimulus measures has investors in the mood to buy.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark S&P/TSX Composite Index is up 317.74 points or 2.64 percent at 12,353.60.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the index closed up 192.75 points or 1.63 percent, at 12,035.86. The index scaled an intraday high of 12,070.33 and a low of 11,772.19.\par \par \uc2 The Energy Index is surging 5.10 percent. Crude oil prices surged higher Friday morning as bargain hunters stepped in for second day in a row. Prices are approaching $32 a barrel this morning.\par \par \uc2 Canadian Natural Resources\uc2  (CNQ.TO) is advancing 5.16 percent and Enbridge (ENB.TO) is gaining 4.23 percent. \uc2 Tourmaline Oil\uc2  (TOU.TO) is rising 3.02 percent and \uc2 Encana\uc2  (ECA.TO) is adding 10.09 percent. \uc2 Cenovus Energy\uc2  (CVE.TO) is up 6.08 percent and \uc2 Crescent Point Energy\uc2  (CPG.TO) is climbing 7.30 percent. \uc2 Suncor Energy\uc2  (SU.TO) is increasing 4.91 percent and Canadian Oil Sands (COS.TO) is gaining 4.56 percent. \uc2 Husky Energy\uc2  (HSE.TO) is higher by 1.23 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Diversified Metal and Mining Index is advancing 4.98 percent. \uc2 First Quantum Minerals\uc2  (FM.TO) is climbing 8.46 percent and \uc2 HudBay Minerals\uc2  (HBM.TO) is adding 7 percent. \uc2 Teck Resources\uc2  (TCK-B.TO) is increasing 3.55 percent and \uc2 Sherritt International\uc2  (S.TO) is gaining 5.88 percent. \uc2 Lundin Mining\uc2  (LUN.TO) is surging 8.18 percent and \uc2 Capstone Mining\uc2  (CS.TO) is up 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Capped Industrials Index is climbing 4.07 percent. \uc2 Canadian National Railway\uc2  (CNR.TO) is gaining 2.98 percent and \uc2 Canadian Pacific Railway\uc2  (CP.TO) is increasing 8.39 percent. Bombardier (BBD-B.TO) is rising 0.92 percent and \uc2 Air Canada\uc2  (AC.TO) is adding 3.63 percent. \uc2 Finning International\uc2  (FTT.TO) is advancing 3.98 percent and AutoCanada (ACQ.TO) is climbing 5.09 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Capped Health Care Index is higher by 3.06 percent. \uc2 Concordia Healthcare\uc2  (CXR.TO) is gaining 3.42 percent and Extendicare (EXE.TO) is up 4.99 percent. \uc2 Valeant Pharmaceuticals International\uc2  (VRX.TO) is rising 1.51 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Capped Information Technology Index is up 2.26 percent. \uc2 Constellation Software\uc2  (CSU.TO) is higher by 2 percent and \uc2 Sierra Wireless\uc2  (SW.TO) is gaining 2.47 percent. BlackBerry (BB.TO) is increasing 0.71 percent and \uc2 Descartes Systems Group\uc2  (DSG.TO) is climbing 2.95 percent.\par \par \uc2 Avigilon (AVO.TO) is rising 2.88 percent. The company announced that Pradeep K. Khosla has been appointed to its Board of Directors.\par \par \uc2 The heavyweight Financial Index is rising 2.09 percent. \uc2 Canadian Imperial Bank of Commerce\uc2  (CM.TO) is gaining 2.71 percent and \uc2 Toronto-Dominion Bank\uc2  (TD.TO) is up 2.27 percent. \uc2 National Bank of Canada\uc2  (NA.TO) is advancing 3.06 percent and \uc2 Bank of Nova Scotia\uc2  (BNS.TO) is adding 2.22 percent. \uc2 Royal Bank of Canada\uc2  (RY.TO) is increasing 2.62 percent.\par \par \uc2 Bank of Montreal\uc2  (BMO.TO) is higher by 2.82 percent. The company announced an upgrade program of 2,200 full-service ATMs across Canada.\par \par \uc2 The Capped Materials Index is up 2.09 percent. \uc2 Silver Wheaton\uc2  (SLW.TO) is increasing 1.66 percent and \uc2 Agrium\uc2  (AGU.TO) is higher by 1.17 percent. \uc2 Potash Corp. of Saskatchewan\uc2  (POT.TO) is adding 2.19 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Capped Telecommunication Services Index is gaining 1.85 percent. \uc2 Manitoba Telecom Services\uc2  (MBT.TO) is rising 2.76 percent and \uc2 BCE\uc2  (BCE.TO) is advancing 1.56 percent. \uc2 TELUS\uc2  (T.TO) is climbing 1.58 percent and Sandvine (SVC.TO) is up 1.02 percent.\par \par \uc2 The Gold Index is up 0.25 percent. Gold prices were flat Friday morning, with the precious metal losing a bit of its safe haven appeal as markets rebound from severe recent losses.\par \par \uc2 Kinross Gold\uc2  (K.TO) is up 2.79 percent and \uc2 Yamana Gold\uc2  (YRI.TO) is adding 3.88 percent. \uc2 IAMGOLD\uc2  (IMG.TO) is climbing 5.75 percent and \uc2 Goldcorp\uc2  (G.TO) is higher by 1.64 percent. \uc2 Royal Gold\uc2  (RGL.TO) is rising 2.70 percent and Eldorado Gold (ELD.TO) is increasing 2.92 percent.\par \par \uc2 Barrick Gold\uc2  (ABX.TO) expects to take up to $3-billion in impairment charges. The stock is now up 1.34 percent.\par \par \uc2 On the economic front, Statistics Canada reported this morning that the Canadian consumer price index increased 1.6 percent from a year ago in December. Economists had expected an increase of 1.7 percent. The index declined 0.5 percent on a month over month basis, which was in line with expectations.\par \par \uc2 A separate report from Statistics Canada showed that Canadian retail sales climbed 1.7 percent in November. Economists had expected an increase of just 0.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Eurozone private sector growth eased more-than-expected to a 11-month low in January, suggesting that the slowdown in emerging markets affected economic activity. The Composite Purchasing Managers' Index, which combines manufacturing and services, fell to 53.5 in January from 54.3 in December, flash data from Markit Economics showed Friday. Economists had forecast a modest decline to 54.2.\par \par \uc2 Germany's private sector grew at the slowest pace in three months in January, flash data from Markit showed Friday. The composite output index fell to 54.5 in January from 55.5 in December.\par \par \uc2 The French private sector expanded at a slightly faster pace in January, flash survey results from Markit showed Friday. The flash composite output index rose marginally to 50.5 in January from 50.1 in December.\par \par \uc2 U.K. retail sales declined more than expected in December, figures from the \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  revealed Friday. Retail sales fell 1 percent in December from November, when it grew 1.3 percent. Economists had forecast a 0.3 percent drop for December.\par \par \uc2 The U.K. budget deficit narrowed in December, the \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  said Friday. Public sector net borrowing excluding public sector banks decreased GBP 4.3 billion to GBP 7.5 billion in December. The expected level was GBP 10.5 billion.\par \par \uc2 British households perceive that the value of their home increased at the start of the year, though the rate of growth eased from the prior month, a survey from Knight Frank and Markit Economics showed Friday. The house price sentiment index, or HPSI, fell to 58.7 in January from 59.4 in December.\par \par \uc2 After reporting a notable decrease in U.S. existing home sales in the previous month, the \uc2 National Association of Realtors\uc2  released a report on Friday showing that sales rebounded strongly in December. \uc2 NAR\uc2  said existing home sales jumped 14.7 percent to an annual rate of 5.46 million in December from a rate of 4.76 million in November. Economists had expected sales to climb to a rate of 5.20 million.\par \par \uc2 With a drop in housing permits and weak new orders in manufacturing leading the decline, the \uc2 Conference Board\uc2  released a report on Friday showing a modest decrease in leading U.S. economic indicators in the month of December.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Conference Board\uc2  said its leading economic index edged down by 0.2 percent in December after climbing by a revised 0.5 percent in November. Economists had expected the index to slip by 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 In commodities, crude oil futures for February delivery are up 2.15 or 7.28 percent at $31.68 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Natural gas for February is up 0.003 or 0.14 percent at $2.141 per million btu.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures for February are down $0.90 or 0.08 percent at $1,097.30 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 Silver for March is up $0.106 or 0.75 percent at $14.20 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 For comments and feedback: contact editorial@rttnews.com\par \par \uc2 Copyright(c) 2016 RTTNews.com. All Rights Reserved\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTTNEW0020160122ec1m004mp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares Rebound; Nikkei Soars 5.9%\b0\par\par\uc2 859 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 2016\par \uc2 RTT News\par \uc2 RTTNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2016 NoticiasFinancieras. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (RTTNews) - Asian shares rose across the board on Friday, thanks to a rebound on Wall Street overnight following an uptick in oil prices and dovish comments from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 ECB\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 ECB President \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  on Thursday signaled that the governing council may provide more stimulus at its next meeting in March amid growing concerns over faltering global economic growth, which have spooked investor sentiment worldwide.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 0.7 percent overnight and the S&P 500 added half a percent after oil futures rebounded sharply and Draghi hinted at additional stimulus measures.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil prices continued to climb in Asian deals after posting their biggest daily gain this year on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Worries about possible competitive devaluations also receded after China clarified it has no plan to pursue a policy of yuan devaluation.\par \par \uc2 China's Vice President \uc2 Li Yuanchao\uc2  said on Thursday that market forces will guide the movement of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the stock market to avoid excessive volatility. \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan was barely moved against the dollar after these comments.\par \par \uc2 Chinese shares swung between gains and losses before closing higher amid a global rally. The benchmark Shanghai Composite index closed up 36.08 points or 1.25 percent at 2,916.56 after losing 3.2 percent the previous day.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng index closed nearly 3 percent higher at 19,080.51, led by Chinese oil and coal producers after Premier \uc2 Li Keqiang\uc2  called for supply-side reforms and cutting overcapacity in the steel and coal industries. Chinese shares traded in Hong Kong rebounded from a six-year low.\par \par \uc2 Japanese shares soared, with sentiment buoyed by a weaker yen, the bounce in oil prices and speculation that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  may announce fresh monetary stimulus next week to fend off the threat of deflation. The benchmark Nikkei average closed up 941.27 points or 5.88 percent at 16,958.53, recouping the losses it had suffered in previous two sessions. However, the index fell 1.1 percent for the week.\par \par \uc2 Exporters \uc2 Sony\uc2 , \uc2 Panasonic\uc2 , \uc2 Toshiba\uc2  and \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  rose 4-6 percent as the dollar rose against the yen after comments from ECB President Draghi. \uc2 Sharp Corp\uc2  rallied 3.1 percent on talk of \uc2 Foxconn Technology Group\uc2  showing interest in taking over the company.\par \par \uc2 Energy explorer \uc2 Inpex Corp\uc2  soared 7.2 percent and \uc2 JX Holdings\uc2  advanced 5.8 percent. Market heavyweight \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  and mobile carrier \uc2 Softbank Corp\uc2  jumped about 8 percent each. Yaoko shares gained almost 6 percent after industry data showed supermarkets' existing-store sales increased in 2015 for the first time in 19 years.\par \par \uc2 In economic releases, manufacturing activity in Japan continued to expand in January, albeit at a slightly lower pace, the latest survey from Nikkei revealed with a PMI core of 54.2.\par \par \uc2 Australian shares extended gains from the previous session as commodities saw a brief reprieve from persistent selling. The benchmark S&P/ASX 200 climbed 52 points or 1.07 percent to 4,916 and the broader All Ordinaries rose 52 points or 1.06 percent to 4,969.60.\par \par \uc2 Mining giant \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2  jumped 7.5 percent and rival \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  added 3.4 percent. Oil & gas producer Santos soared 11 percent after reassuring investors over its financial health. Gold miner Newcrest dropped 1.5 percent as gold fell from a two-week high.\par \par \uc2 Medical device maker \uc2 ResMed\uc2  climbed 4.7 percent on reporting a 7 percent increase in second-quarter revenue. Insurance giant \uc2 Medibank Private\uc2  climbed 11.6 percent after upgrading its profit forecast. Winemaker Treasury Wine Estates soared 17.5 percent after its half-year earnings exceeded expectations.\par \par \uc2 Seoul shares gained the most in 4-1/2-mths and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained ground against the dollar on hopes for further easing of monetary policy in Europe and Japan. The benchmark Kospi average climbed 38.90 points or 2.11 percent to 1,879.43, but ended the week 2 percent lower. Market heavyweight \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  gained 3.3 percent and \uc2 SK Innovation\uc2 , the \uc2 SK Group\uc2  energy affiliate, advanced 3.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand shares joined a global rally after the oil price rebound. The benchmark NZX-50 index rose 40.71 points or 0.67 percent to 6,121.61. \uc2 Sky Network Television\uc2  rallied 4.7 percent to hit a three-week high, \uc2 Spark New Zealand\uc2  advanced 3.3 percent and \uc2 Genesis Energy\uc2  added 2.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, India's Sensex was up nearly 2 percent, Indonesia's Jakarta Composite index was gaining half a percent, Malaysia's KLSE Composite was rallying 1.4 percent, Singapore's Straits Times index was climbing 2.3 percent and the Taiwan Weighted added 1.2 percent.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's jobless rate rose to 3.2 percent in November from 3.1 percent in the previous month, official data showed. Taiwan's jobless rate rose more than expected to 3.88 percent in December from 3.84 percent in November.\par \par \uc2 For comments and feedback: contact editorial@rttnews.com\par \par \uc2 Copyright(c) 2016 RTTNews.com. All Rights Reserved\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTTNEW0020160122ec1m0025u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
