\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Davos -China has means to support economy, especially on fiscal side-regulator\b0\par\par\uc2 413 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Januar 2016\par 13:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quotes)\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Jan 21 (Reuters) - China has the means to ward off a sharper slowdown in the economy, especially on the fiscal side, Fang Xinghai, vice chairman of \uc2 China's Securities Regulatory Commission\uc2  (CRSC) said on Thursday at a session at the \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2  in Davos.\par \par \uc2 Concerns about China's policymaking ability have shot to the top of global investors' risk lists for 2016 after a renewed plunge in its stock markets and yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stoked worries that the economy may be rapidly deteriorating.\par \par \uc2 "In China, we cannot afford to let the growth rate drop too sharply, because that will ignite a lot of financial problems," said Fang.\par \par \uc2 "We have the means to do so, particularly on the fiscal side that we can expand quite a lot," Fang said in a video feed monitored by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 China's economic growth slowed to 6.8 percent in the fourth quarter, the weakest since the financial crisis, adding pressure on a government that is struggling to restore the confidence of investors.\par \par \uc2 Policy insiders expect China to lean more on the fiscal policy to support the slowing economy, probably widening this year's budget deficit to about 3 percent of GDP, while further cuts in interest rates and bank reserve ratios are anticipated.\par \par \uc2 China's stock market should do more to support the economy, Fang said, without giving details.\par \par \uc2 Fang reiterated that there was no basis for China to devalue the yuan, based on its economic fundamentals.\par \par \uc2 China will honour its commitment on reforms related to the yuan's entry into the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 's special drawing rights (SDR) basket, Fang said.\par \par \uc2 "Some people worry that once China entered into the SDR, it may not fulfill the commitment with respect to joining the SDR. I can assure you that worry is completely unnecessary," Fang said.\par \par \uc2 In November, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  admitted the yuan to the SDR basket, a symbolic win for Beijing's campaign for recognition as a global economic power.\par \par \uc2 Chinese officials have pledged to push forward economic reforms, despite recent heavy-handed steps to rescue the stock market and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yuan.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's structural reform will be a "massive undertaking," International Monetary Fund chief \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2  told the same session. (Reporting by Beijing Monitoring Desk and Kevin Yao; Editing by Kim Coghill and Clarence Fernandez) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-21T13:13:50.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160121ec1l00e48\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
