\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Paints\uc2 , \uc2 Wipro\uc2  Are the Only India Stocks in Green -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,845 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2016\par 06:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0528 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] \uc2 Asian Paints\uc2  (India) (500820.BY) and \uc2 Wipro\uc2  (507685.BY) are the only two stocks on the 30-stock S&P \uc2 BSE\uc2  Sensex with a 0.9% and 0.1% gain. \uc2 Asian Paints\uc2  enjoys a dominant position in the Indian paint market and on Monday reported a 25.8% on-year rise in third-quarter consolidated net profit at 4.63 billion rupees compared to 3.68 billion rupees a year earlier on a 13.9% on-year rise in total income at 419.58 billion rupees. \uc2 Wipro\uc2  is up despite reporting a modest 1.7% on-year rise in its fiscal third-quarter net profit as analysts find its valuation cheap relative to peers. (kenan.machado@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0525 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] Analysts have theories but few definitive answers to explain the Hong Kong stocks sell-off during its morning session. The Hang Seng Index is down 3.77% to 18,894, a 3-1/2 year low, and the SOE-heavy China Enterprises Index is down a thumping 4.89%. For once, the selling is not China driven: the Shanghai Composite was up when the Hong Kong hemorrhaging began. Will Leung, \uc2 Standard Chartered\uc2 's head of northeast Asia strategy, says Hong Kong's selloff is sentiment driven, with traders reacting to oil prices falling below $30 a barrel. Meanwhile, the Hong Kong dollar is weakening, falling close to the threshold requiring \uc2 Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rattling nerves. \uc2 Erwin Sanft\uc2 , head of \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Macquarie, says traders are concerned yuan weakening will diminish the value of HKD-denominated H-shares. This explains why the \uc2 China Enterprises\uc2  index fell further than the (yuan-denominated) A-share market Wednesday. But these are old dynamics, and the Leung and Sanft acknowledge there is little new in market to make traders sell. (jasper.moiseiwitsch@wsj.com; Twitter: @moiseiwitsch) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0520 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Japanese stocks extend falls, hitting lowest levels since Oct. 31, 2014, the day the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2  decided to bolster its quantitative easing. Masayuki Kubota, chief strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rakuten Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 , says continued falls in crude oil prices are stoking concerns about asset sales from oil producing countries, further deterioration of economic conditions in commodity producing nations, and worsening of U.S. energy industry, he says. In addition, there are some emerging concerns that more defaults of commodity projects could potentially lead to a financial crisis, he says. The Nikkei is down 3.3% at 16476.29. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0511 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Indonesia's rupiah is mildly weaker Wednesday, as crude oil prices threaten to print new multi-year lows, prompting a fresh round of risk aversion after Tuesday's relief rally. Spooked by a broad and deep slide of regional equity markets, the safe haven greenback is making headway versus most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . USD/IDR now trades at 13,905 from its Tuesday close of 13,875, representing a 0.2% depreciation of the rupiah versus the dollar. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's relatively small drop has raised speculation of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Indonesia\f28 \uc2 \f2  has recently been suspected of proactively buying the rupiah in the spot market, especially after last Thursday's terror attacks in Jakarta, to prevent sharp falls that might spark investor panic. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0455 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The chance of an imminent reserve ratio requirement cut by \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  can't be ruled cut despite the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 's move to inject more than $90 billion of funds into the banking system before the Lunar New Year holiday, economists say. The cash injections weakened speculation of an RRR cut before the holiday. "I think the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is also waiting to see how January's foreign reserves data look," says HSBC economist Ma Xiaoping. "Judging from the yuan rate's movements in January, I think [the \f28 \uc2 \f2 PBOC\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] may still have to lower the RRR to counteract capital outflows," Ma adds. Beijing's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, which have fallen every month but one since May, dropped by a record $107.9 billion in December. (liyan.qi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: PJK \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0450 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Malaysia's inflation rose less than expected in December partly due to government efforts to keep prices in check during the holiday season. Those efforts also offset upward pressure on the index from an unfavorable comparison with last year and more expensive imports as a result of a weaker local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The consumer-price index, the country's main gauge of inflation, rose by 2.7% in December from a year earlier. In November, the CPI rose 2.6% year-over-year. Economists had forecast on-year growth of 2.8% in December. The CPI in December remained unchanged from November and rose 2.1% in the January-December period. Inflation was 3.2% in 2014. Despite the slight acceleration in inflation, economists expect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  to keep the overnight policy rate unchanged. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Negara Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  will announce the outcome of its two-day policy rate review on Thursday. (saurabh.chaturvedi@wsj.com; @journosaurabh) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0447 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Taiwan December export orders--an important leading indicator for exports and industrial output--will likely register a 7.1% year-on-year decline, according to the median forecast of three economists polled by The Wall Street Journal. DBS expects Taiwan's 2015 exports orders to have fallen 3.9%, the first annual contraction since 2009 partly due to weak demand from China. The bank notes demand from China will likely remain weak, given the further slowdown in China GDP growth in the fourth quarter and the heightened volatility in its financial markets of late. Meanwhile, demand for electronics products may start to level off as the festive season has passed, the bank says. "In the short-term, cyclical headwinds will continue to dominate, presenting challenges for the outlook of exports and overall economy," the bank says. December Taiwan export orders data is scheduled for release on Wednesday at 0800 GMT. (chester.yung@wsj.com; Twitter:@chester_yung) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0443 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Capital flight from China will become the "new norm" in 2016 while the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ricochets between trying to maintain \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flexibility and keeping the yuan stable, says Citi. "Yuan depreciation will be structural, with increased volatility on both directions, and the growing uncertainty on the yuan's long-term performance will keep driving capitals outside," says Citi analyst Eric Tang during a 2016 investment outlook conference in Shanghai Tuesday. Growing demand for overseas assets from Chinese individuals, coupled with Chinese companies' drive to buy overseas firms, will lead to a sustained capital outflow. Citi expects the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to cut interest rate cuts twice this year, to cut banks' reserve rate five times. (Yifan.xie@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0439 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] India's rupee has slumped to a fresh two-year low of 67.95, surpassing Monday's low of 67.70 to the U.S. dollar, as risk aversion returns with a vengeance. After a brief respite Tuesday, the safe haven greenback is on the rise again as stocks retreat sharply. A broad shakedown in Asian equity markets is underway, as Indian stocks slide to their lowest since May 2014. The benchmark Sensex stock index is down 1.9%, while in other parts of Asia, South Korea's Kospi is down 3.1%, Hong Kong's Hang Seng is down 3.8%, and Singapore's STI is down 2.6%. The continued collapse of crude oil prices, which in better economic times would have been beneficial to Indian assets due to the country's chronic oil-related trade deficit, has been blamed for the ongoing malaise in global financial markets. USD/INR is now 67.93 versus its Tuesday close of 67.72. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0424 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The 3.0% decline in South Korean stocks is derailing the won, which is already down 0.7% versus its Tuesday close. The USD/KRW pair is hence climbing and back inside the Bollinger uptrend channel. Confirmation of this bullish chart cue, which hinges on a Wednesday closing above 1,210, would increase the chances that the won will continue to lose value versus the U.S. dollar in the near term. The resurgence of risk aversion was kicked off by the overnight drop of crude oil prices -- on a warning from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 International Energy Agency\f28 \uc2 \f2  about a worsening oil glut -- but it has been taken a notch higher with Asian equities getting dumped across the board. Hong Kong's benchmark equity index is down 3.8%, while Singapore's STI is down 2.7%. USD/KRW is now 1,214.4 versus its Tuesday close of 1,205.9. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: JFN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0409 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Indian shares fall to a level last seen before \f28 \uc2 \f2 Narendra Modi\f28 \uc2 \f2  became Prime Minister in May 2014. The benchmark S&P \f28 \uc2 \f2 BSE\f28 \uc2 \f2  Sensex is down 1.4% at 24,131.89 after falling as low as 24072.73 in early trade. The Sensex index was at 24121.74 before Modi came to power. The National Stock Exchange's Nifty 50 index also declines 1.6% to 7,318.30. Analysts say the fresh decline in Chinese shares and weak domestic macroeconomic data is weighing on the sentiment. Among the major losers, Adani Ports (532921.BY) falls 3.8%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Larsen & Toubro\f28 \uc2 \f2  (500510.BY) slips 3.7%, while \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tata Steel\f28 \uc2 \f2  (500470.BY) is down 3.2%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reliance Industries\f28 \uc2 \f2  (500325.BY) is also trading down 2.6% despite posting highest ever quarterly profit.(debiprasad.nayak@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 20 Jan 2016 00:28 ET \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Asian Paints\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wipro\f28 \uc2 \f2  Are the Only India Stocks in -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0406 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The Hang Seng Index plunges 3.8% to 18,896, a level last seen in 2012. One reason is speculation that the weakening Hong Kong dollar, a sign of capital outflows, will set in motion a chain of events leading to Hong Kong banks raising interest rates earlier than expected, analysts say. Local banks largely did not raise rates when the Fed did in December, because liquidity was abundant in the financial system. "Now there's more pressure" that rates will be raised to draw money back to the city, says Daniel So, strategist for \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Merchants Bank International\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "That would have a very big psychological impact on the housing market. Any rate hike would speed up the downturn." The next technical support level for the benchmark is 18,000, So says. "At 18,000 I think it would be rather safe to buy local Hong Kong stocks, especially Hong Kong telecoms, Hong Kong REITs and some utility stocks," he says. (dominique.fong@wsj.com; @dominiquefong) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 20, 2016 00:28 ET (05:28 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160120ec1k000a2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
