\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-South Africa's rand recovers, stocks dip as European shares fall\b0\par\par\uc2 504 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 2016\par 17:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2016 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 * Rand recovers on \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's c.bank yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Yields on government bonds hit one week high\par \par \uc2 * Stocks dragged lower as European shares slip (Adds latest prices, analyst comments)\par \par \uc2 JOHANNESBURG, Jan 18 (Reuters) - South Africa's rand firmed on Monday as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped ease global investor aversion toward emerging market assets in trade thinned by a holiday in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Stocks inched lower on Monday to end just in the red as a rebound in European shares fizzled after the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (\uc2 ECB\uc2 )said it would quiz euro zone lenders about high levels of bad loans.\par \par \uc2 By 1625 GMT the rand inched 0.09 percent firmer to 16.7670 per dollar, hanging on to earlier gains as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China\uc2 's efforts to stabilise the yuan saw demand for riskier assets in developing economies improve.\par \par \uc2 Lingering concerns over the local economy would however limit the rand's recovery, analysts said, more so with consumer inflation data due on Wednesday likely to show a spike in food prices owing to severe drought conditions.\par \par \uc2 "Expect the rand to battle to improve in the weeks and months ahead. We need quite a bit of water of pass under the bridge before the rand can start clawing back some of that improved sentiment," said economist at Credit Guarantee Insurance Luke Doig.\par \par \uc2 Bonds weakened, unable to shake the increased risk associated with South African assets, with the yield on the government's 10-year issue rising 15.5 basis points to 9.87 percent, its highest in one week.\par \par \uc2 On the bourse the benchmark Top-40 index slipped 0.03 percent to 42,093 while the wider All-share index finished 0.18 percent lower at 46,876.\par \par \uc2 Decliners included petrochemicals group \uc2 Sasol\uc2 , which shed 3.75 percent as Brent and U.S. WTI futures were last down 0.5 percent at $28.88 and $29.38 a barrel, respectively, just above 12-year lows.\par \par \uc2 Technical factors could, however, give the market some support this week, according to analysts.\par \par \uc2 "We are looking pretty oversold at these levels on traditional technicals," said Lloyd Priestman, a markets analyst at Caleo Capital in Johannesburg.\par \par \uc2 Momentum indicators such as the 14-day RSI suggest the Top-40, which has shed 8 percent to date in 2015, has strayed into oversold territory, making a support rally likely in the coming week.\par \par \uc2 Gainers on the day included steel-maker \uc2 ArcelorMittal\uc2  South Africa, which added 9.7 percent to 5.89 rand after it said that 68 percent of its 4.5 billion rand ($270 million) rights offer had been taken up and that its parent group would subscribe for the remainder.\par \par \uc2 Decliners pipped advancers 194 to 127 and over 240 million shares changed hands, a little over the 2015 daily average. (Reporting by Mfuneko Toyana and Ed Stoddard; Editing by Richard Balmforth) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2016-1-18T17:51:50.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020160118ec1i00k2c\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 European Stocks Seen Flat To Lower As China Concerns Linger\b0\par\par\uc2 435 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2016\par \uc2 RTT News\par \uc2 RTTNEW\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2016 NoticiasFinancieras. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 (RTTNews) - European stocks are seen opening flat to slightly lower on Friday despite strong overnight cues from Wall Street. Asian stock markets surrendered early gains to hit 3-1/2-year lows as oil prices fell in Asian deals and disappointing bank lending data added to gloom over the state of Chinese economy.\par \par \uc2 China's Shanghai Composite index was down as much as 3 percent on concerns a weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will add to record capital outflows. The yuan steadied amid \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 China\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yuan purchases surged to a record in December, data showed today.\par \par \uc2 The safe-haven Japanese yen edged higher, commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost ground, industrial metals retreated and gold firmed up after recent losses, indicating a more cautious investor sentiment.\par \par \uc2 The economic calendar remains light, with investors awaiting U.K. construction output and Eurozone trade balance figures later in the day for further direction.\par \par \uc2 In the New York session, traders will keep a close eye on reports on retail sales, producer prices, industrial production and consumer sentiment for further clues to the state of the economy.\par \par \uc2 On the earnings front, \uc2 Intel\uc2 's quarterly earnings and revenue topped estimates, but shares of the tech giant fell about 5 percent in extended trading on concerns about slowing revenue growth in its highly profitable data center business.\par \par \uc2 In domestic corporate news, brewer Anheuser-Busch InBev said it had launched a major bond issue to finance its deal to buy rival \uc2 SABMiller\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Salzgitter Group said it would be affected by the legislation of German Federal Finance Court published on January 13, 2016.\par \par \uc2 European stocks hit three-month lows on Thursday as investors continued to fret about oil price volatility and choppy Chinese markets. Stocks ended off their day's lows after the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  kept interest rates on hold and \uc2 ECB\uc2  minutes indicated that some members wanted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to act more aggressively in December.\par \par \uc2 The German DAX dropped 1.7 percent, France's CAC 40 index lost 1.8 percent and the FTSE 100 of the U.K. shed 0.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, stocks bounced back from an early side on Thursday as oil prices rebounded from 12-year lows, \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  reported better-than-expected quarterly earnings and statements from Fed officials indicated that interest rates will rise slowly. The Dow rose 1.4 percent, the tech-heavy \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  rallied 2 percent and the S&P 500 gained 1.7 percent.\par \par \uc2 For comments and feedback: contact editorial@rttnews.com\par \par \uc2 Copyright(c) 2016 RTTNews.com. All Rights Reserved\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTTNEW0020160115ec1f001rx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
