\par\par \b \uc2 China Shares Fall on Fear Banks Refuse Stock as Collateral -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,854 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2016\par 07:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 0626 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ] China's shares turned sharply lower in afternoon trade following a local newspaper report on certain banks refusing to accept company stock as loan collateral. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index is now down 2.6% at 2927.63, after falling more than 3% earlier. The abrupt selloff came after an article in the International Finance News reported earlier that an unspecified number of banks in Shanghai have stopped accepting stocks from companies as collateral for borrowing. The story raised concerns among already vulnerable investors about the outlook for the market, despite a rebound yesterday. However, the market may have over-reacted to the report, given that even yesterday, the more authoritative China Securities News ran an article that dispelled similar worries, saying that stock exchanges and brokerages are keeping a firm grip on risks associated with the so-called "share pledges" business.\par \par \uc2  Now all eyes are on the routine weekly briefing by the \uc2 China Securities Regulatory Commission\uc2  after the market closes. (hong.shen@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0622 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Credit rating agency \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  downgrades its long-term credit rating on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sumitomo Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (8053.TO) to negative from stable after the trading house said it expects a big impairment loss on its investment in a Madagascar nickel project. The Y77 billion ($654 million) write-down, which the company says may be followed by others, will mean Japan's fourth-largest trading firm will substantially miss its Y230 billion profit forecast for the year through March, S&P says. While the company's profits will likely recover in fiscal 2016, the rating firms says, "We expect accumulation of equity capital from profit recovery and growth to remain constrained." This will weigh on the company's ability to take risks in the future, as the company will need to free cash flow by speeding up asset disposal and rein in risk assets. Shares in Sumitomo are up 0.4% after touching their lowest since October 2014 on Thursday. (mayumi.negishi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0552 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Chinese startup \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xiaomi Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . said Friday it sold more than 70 million smartphones last year, missing its forecast of 80 million to 100 million units. The company cited China's smartphone market contraction as the reason for its sales growth slowdown. Xiaomi's slowing growth has prompted analysts to question its $46 billion valuation in recent months. (eva.dou@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0547 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] China share weakness will no doubt be on the radar of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Australia's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when it meets on Feb. 2, but not by as much as you might think. The conduit that links Shanghai to Sydney is the iron ore price, and throughout the crunch in China Shares that began late December, the price of the ore has not moved much. If you measure it from mid-December, it is actually higher. That overall resilience at around the US$40 per ton mark will temper any concerns the RBA has about China just now. The crunch in China shares that occurred mid-2015 saw the iron ore price linger between US$50 and US$55 per ton, giving the RBA scope to care less at the time. Interest rates in Australia have been on hold since May, 2015. The fact that the massive devaluation of iron ore over recent years has pretty much run its course, is another reason why the RBA will care less. To be sure, with some forecasters calling a drop toward the US$20 per ton mark near-term, the RBA might be forced to reevaluate. (james.glynn@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2  @JamesGlynnWSJ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0544 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] India's rupee is down a second day in a row, falling to a two-year trough of 67.44 to the U.S. dollar, extending from Thursday's historical low of 67.30. The weakness of the rupee is in sync with other emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  which have surrendered early gains Friday, and are now mostly weaker on an interday basis, due to a downturn in China's stock market. Shanghai composite index is currently down 2.4%, ignoring the overnight improvement in general risk appetite, and nullifying yesterday's 2.0% rally. USD/INR could keep heading higher while the bullish technical indicator of the Bollinger uptrend channel remains in effect. If the dollar ends Friday stronger than 66.95, the positive-USD chart signal might encourage dollar-buying that would relatively weaken the rupee. USD/INR is now 67.41 from its Thursday close of 67.30. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0534 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] World's fifth-largest wind turbine maker \f28 \uc2 \f2 Suzlon Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  (532667.BY) is up 2.2% to 20.65 rupees after it said in a filing Friday that lenders agreed to lend it $344 million so it can finish its orders. "This will enable Suzlon to scale up its volumes," the company said. The new line of credit is in addition to working capital of $644 million, it said. Investors who wanted a play on the global renewable energy space were enthused by Suzlon. The froth in the stock prompted the Indian company to buy Germany's RePower in 2007 for 1.4 billion euros, funded mostly by debt. But the global credit crisis of 2008 and cracks in the turbines of RePower and the resultant customer payouts forced Suzlon to renegotiate repayment with its bond holders. (kenan.machado@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0517 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The USD/JPY falls as low as 117.59 from around 117.67 after BOJ Governor Haruhiko Kuroda said during parliamentary testimony that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was not considering additional easing for now. "There still remains expectations for easing in January," says a senior Japanese bank dealer. But the comment was not strong enough to cause yen buying to bring down the pair to 117.50 or below, says the dealer. The pair now bounces back to 117.78. (hiroyuki.kachi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0508 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Following Thursday's attacks in Jakarta, the Philippines must confront the threat of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Islamic State\f28 \uc2 \f2  to prevent similar attacks on Manila or other cities, security experts have warned. Philippine military officials have always flatly denied that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Islamic State\f28 \uc2 \f2  has any foothold in the country, but they should now admit that the group has a small but dangerous presence in the Muslim south, said Zachary Abuza of the U.S. National War College, as revealed by militant videos posted online. A Jakarta-style attack would be "very much within the capabilities of militants in the southern Philippines," he said. In 2014 the Philippine government signed a landmark peace deal with Islamic rebels on the southern island of Mindanao, but the agreement has become bogged down in the Philippine Congress, where some lawmakers are wary of ceding too much power to former rebels. The peace process should now be given top priority, Abuza said, to avoid playing into the hands of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Islamic State\f28 \uc2 \f2  by giving disaffected young Muslims a reason to flirt with the Middle East group's extremist ideology. (trefor.moss@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0431 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] The offshore yuan is down 0.3% against the U.S. dollar Friday, and breaking stride with the onshore yuan--which might be cause for consternation among authorities. The yuan is weaker as stocks on the benchmark Shanghai composite index slide 1.5%, nearly nullifying Thursday's 2.0% rally, which took the index above the psychological watermark of 3,000. The index is now back under 3,000 despite an overnight Wall Street rally, which has given other Asian stock markets a boost. The offshore USD/CNH is now implying a weaker offshore yuan compared to the onshore USD/CNY. This price differential--which represents bearish bets on the yuan by offshore-based speculators--was closed on Monday when suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday sent the offshore yuan sharply higher. But the gap started opening up again yesterday, and if it widens further, the authorities might step in again to wrestle USD/CNH back down. USD/CNH is now 6.6161 from its Thursday close of 6.5995. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor JSM \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0430 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] South Korea's won is still a tad stronger versus the U.S. dollar than its last close, but that may not be true for long. USD/KRW is clawing its way back up to Thursday's July 2010 peak of 1,215.3 as the local stock market reverses its earlier 0.8% rise; the Kospi index is now down 1.1%. The inability of China stocks to build on yesterday's 2.0% rally, instead falling 1.5%, may have been the trigger for bearishness among equity investors elsewhere in Asia. Initial investor optimism, stemming from Wall Street's 1.7% rally on the back of oil prices recovering, has faded. USD/KRW could resume its upwards path, implying further weakness for the won versus the U.S. unit, in the near term if it ends Friday above 1,204.1 and thus keeps the daily Bollinger uptrend channel intact. USD/KRW is now 1,212.9 from its Thursday close of 1,213.4. (ewen.chew@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: MNG \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0427 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] OCBC reiterates its buy recommendation and S$1.24 fair value estimate on Keppel DC REIT (AJBU.SG) after the Singapore-listed real estate investment trust says its distribution per unit since its initial public offering exceeds its forecast by 1.9%. The cumulative distribution per unit since Keppel DC REIT was listed on Dec. 12, 2014, was 6.84 Singapore cents, compared with 6.71 Singapore cents forecast in its prospectus for its initial public offering. "Despite recent media reports of a large expected increase in supply of data centres in Singapore this year, we note that the fitting out period can take a very long time and hence the impact [on Keppel DC REIT] may not be as bad as it seems," OCBC says. Keppel DC REIT is up 1% at S$1.02, while Singapore's benchmark FTSE Straits Times Index is flat. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 15 Jan 2016 01:26 ET \par China Shares Fall on Fear Banks Refuse Stock as -2-\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0416 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] Fears of terrorist attack puts a brake on the FTSE \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bursa Malaysia\f28 \uc2 \f2  Index trending higher, says Hong Leong Investment Bank. "Further rebound would be capped at 1674 points ahead of the revised Budget 2016, fears of IS-led attack in this region, following Jakarta's bombing and external headwinds." HLIB said with higher oil prices, KLCI is likely to stage a technical rebound today to retest the 1644-1650 zones. Malaysia was on high alert yesterday after the Jakarta bombings. (celine.fernandez@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Editor: KLH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 15, 2016 01:26 ET (06:26 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160115ec1f000at\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
