\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares Mixed as China Opens Weak--Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chao Deng   \par \uc2 1,182 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2016\par 03:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2016, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China shares hovered near bear-market territory in Friday morning trading, extending an early-year selloff.\par \par \uc2 The Shanghai Composite Index was last down 0.7% at 2987.21, trading just above its lows from last summer. Its current level is down nearly 20% from its Dec. 22 close. A bear market is defined as a 20% or more drop from a recent high.\par \par \uc2 The Hang Seng Index was off by 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 The energy sector in Australia gave up most of its early morning gains, with the S&P/ASX 200 roughly flat. Japan's Nikkei Stock Average rose slightly by 0.7%. South Korea's Kospi was up 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 Gaining oil prices overnight appeared to have encouraged some investors to buy up energy shares, which have taken a drubbing amid jitters about slowing global growth, with China at the center of those concerns.\par \par \uc2 Liquefied Natural Gas Ltd\uc2 . was up 8.1% and \uc2 Woodside Petroleum Ltd\uc2 . gained 0.7%.\par \par \uc2 "We haven't necessarily hit the bottom," said Mohit Bajaj, managing director at brokerage WallachBeth Capital LLC. The energy sector has lost 8.7% and 10.8% in Australia and Hong Kong respectively year-to-date.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, oil producers to miners and biotechnology stocks, some of the most beaten-down corners of the market, carved out sharp gains in the U.S. The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 1.4% and the S&P 500 rose 1.7%.\par \par \uc2 U.S.-traded oil futures gained overnight 2.4% to $31.20 a barrel. In Asia on Friday, Brent crude oil was down a bit, at $30.85 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Early Friday, \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the yuan at 6.5637 to one U.S. dollar, marking the sixth straight session it has guided the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  roughly steady.\par \par \uc2 In the freely traded offshore market, yuan traded at 6.5984 to one U.S. dollar, roughly unchanged from the previous day, when it weakened by as much as 0.7%.\par \par \uc2 The gap between the values of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on two sides of the border had widened slightly Thursday, causing jitters about how \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would direct the market going forward. A record gap last week as bets by traders pushed the offshore yuan lower had threatened Beijing's campaign to pitch the yuan as a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with international stature, triggering turmoil across the globe.\par \par \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  forced the gap to close earlier this week by buying the freely traded yuan offshore through state-owned banks, according to traders. It guided the onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slightly stronger early Thursday at 6.5616 to one U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen was roughly flat at Yen118 to one U.S. dollar. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has gained 2% year-to-date, as turmoil in Chinese and global markets have sent investors in a rush for safety.\par \par \uc2 The Indonesian rupiah strengthened 0.7% to 13,810 to one U.S. dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had fallen as much as 1% on Thursday after news of explosions in the Southeast Asian country's capital city of Jakarta, although it recovered slightly after suspected government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  following a rate cut by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Hong Kong dollar, which is pegged to the U.S. dollar, traded at 7.7793 to one U.S. dollar, after recording its sharpest drop in more than a dozen years overnight to as weak as 7.7894.\par \par \uc2 Gold prices were up 0.4% to $1078 a troy ounce.\par \par \uc2 Write to Chao Deng at Chao.Deng@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 15 Jan 2016 00:06 ET  Asian Shares Down as China Opens Weak--Update\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Chao Deng \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China shares slipped Friday, as worries about slowing growth in the world's second-largest economy kept a lid on gains in Asia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Throughout the region, stock benchmarks were down slightly, as investors remained nervous following a sharp early-year selloff.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai Composite Index was down 1.5% at 2961.47 midday, still hovering near its lows from last summer and near a bear market, defined as a 20% or more drop from a recent high. The benchmark was down 16% year-to-date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The energy sector in Australia by midday had given up most of its early gains as oil prices dipped. The S&P/ASX 200 was down 0.7%. The Hang Seng Index was down by 0.8% and South Korea's Kospi was off 1.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average fell 0.4%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While China's yuan, the initial trigger of much of the rout, has stabilized for the time being, traders and analysts said investors still worry about how \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 China\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in its markets, after authorities made several policy reversals last week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's nothing really out there giving people encouragement," said Andrew Sullivan, managing director at Haitong International in Hong Kong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Overnight, some of the most beaten-down corners of the market, from oil producers to miners and biotechnology stocks, carved out sharp gains in the U.S. The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 1.4% and the S&P 500 rose 1.7%. U.S.-traded oil futures gained overnight 2.4% to $31.20 a barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But oil prices turned lower during the Asia day, with Brent crude oil last down 0.91% at $30.61 a barrel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We haven't necessarily hit the bottom," said Mohit Bajaj, managing director at brokerage WallachBeth Capital LLC. The energy sector has lost 9% and 12% in Australia and Hong Kong respectively year-to-date.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Early Friday, \f28 \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  set the yuan at 6.5637 to one U.S. dollar, marking the sixth-straight session it has guided the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  roughly steady.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the freely traded offshore market, the yuan traded at 6.6162 to one U.S. dollar, up 0.3% from the previous day. Onshore, where the yuan can trade up or down 2% from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's daily fix, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traded at 6.5866, roughly unchanged from the previous day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Japanese yen was up 0.2% at Yen117.85 to one U.S. dollar. The local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has gained 2% year-to-date, as turmoil in Chinese and global markets have sent investors to rush for safety.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Indonesian rupiah strengthened 0.1% to 13,895 to one U.S. dollar. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had fallen as much as 1% on Thursday after news of explosions in the Southeast Asian country's capital city of Jakarta, although it recovered slightly after suspected government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  following a rate cut by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hong Kong dollar, which is pegged to the U.S. dollar, traded at 7.7846 to one U.S. dollar, after recording its sharpest drop in more than a dozen years overnight to as weak as 7.7894.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices were up 0.4% to $1,078 a troy ounce.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Chao Deng at Chao.Deng@wsj.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 15, 2016 00:06 ET (05:06 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020160115ec1f0002l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
